\par\par \b \uc2 Canadian Policy Makers Shouldn't Rush to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  in Housing Market -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,570 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2021\par 17:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1111 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hot housing market has prompted calls for government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb price growth and speculative activity, but \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Jean-Fran\u231\'00\'E7ois Perrault\uc2  says those calls might be premature. He argues the main factor behind recent price acceleration is a divergence between sales and new listings. Since new listings should rise soon because of the spring season and higher prices, he says, policy makers should wait a few weeks before taking action. Perrault added that any short-term changes should be focused on limiting excessive speculation--possibly through a tax on those who flip properties--while longer-term moves should look at easing obstacles to building various forms of housing. (kim.mackrael@wsj.com; @kimmackrael)\par \par \uc2 1103 ET - Economists polled in March by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  raised their median GDP growth estimate for 2021 to 4.5% from 3.7% in February, and lifted expectations for year-end inflation to 4.1% from the previous 3.9%. A recent uptick in inflation led the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave interest rates unchanged at its March meeting, and expectations are that it will keep the overnight policy rate at the current 4% for the rest of this year. The Bank of Mexico projects GDP growth of 4.8% this year, following an 8.2% contraction in 2020, and the Finance Ministry recently raised its forecast to 5.3% from 4.6%. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1058 ET - The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's decision to allow \uc2 Facebook\uc2  to offer users a service to make payments between individuals will put pressure on banks' fee earnings from their own payments services, according to \uc2 Moody's\uc2  . The service will be available using the WhatsApp messaging application and be linked to \uc2 Facebook\uc2  Pay, and no starting date has been set yet for the offering. Brazilian banks have profited from offering payment services to clients for a fee, and also make money from card-payment processing, according to \uc2 Moody's\uc2  . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched its own electronic-payments system, called PIX, in November and that has already led to a sharp drop in fees from money transfers, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. If the bank permits \uc2 Facebook\uc2  to start processing payments to businesses from individuals, that would put even more pressure on banks' fee income, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1055 ET - Peru's presidential election Sunday is shaping up to be one of the most uncertain in memory, political analysts say, as six candidates vie for a spot in a second-round runoff amid widespread rejection of the political class by Peruvians hit hard by the coronavirus pandemic. An \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  poll released Sunday has Yonhy Lescano at 14.7% support, followed by Hernando de Soto at 13.9%, Veronika Mendoza at 12.4%, \uc2 George Forsyth\uc2  at 11.9%, \uc2 Keiko Fujimori\uc2  at 11.2% and Rafael Lopez Aliaga at 8.2%. Meanwhile, an Institute for Peruvian Studies poll has Fujimori and De Soto tied for first, each with 9.8% support. Lopez Aliaga is third at 8.4%. Lescano is at 8.2% and Mendoza at 7.3%. Forsyth has 5.7% backing. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1051 ET - New orders for factory goods in the U.S. declined by 0.8% in February compared with the previous month, data from the Commerce Department shows. The reading misses the 0.3% increase expected by economists polled by The Wall Street Journal and follows a 2.7% rise in January. The decline in factory orders in February disrupts nine consecutive months of increase, and can be attributed partly to bad weather across the country in the month as well as increased supply-chain challenges for manufacturers. New orders for manufactured durable goods fell by 1.2% in February, according to revised figures, compared with the previous estimate of a 1.1% drop. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1050 ET - The vigorous services-sector activity in the U.S. suggested by the ISM PMI data sets the stage for a robust expansion in the second quarter, \uc2 Oren Klachkin\uc2  , lead U.S. economist at Oxford Economics, says. "All the right pieces for a faster services recovery--expanded vaccine eligibility, reopenings and historic fiscal expansion--are falling into place," he says. The American Rescue Plan will boost the recovery while the American Jobs Plan will also have positive knock-on effects for services as well, Klachkin says. Supply-chain disruptions are likely to hinder activity in the short-term, but they should dissipate as health conditions improve, he says. March's ISM Services PMI came in at a record-high 63.7 from 55.3 the prior month. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1048 ET - Americans haven't saved too much money during the coronavirus pandemic, the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  says. "Although large by historical standards, the savings accumulated by US households during the pandemic do not appear to be 'excessive' when set against the extraordinary need of many American families and the unprecedented government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support them," wrote Florin Bilbiie, Gauti Eggertsson, Giorgio Primiceri and Andrea Tambalotti for the bank. The authors say it's possible Americans might start spending some of this $1.6 trillion in cash once the pandemic abates, but add they only expected a limited increase in spending out of the savings. The authors note that their view on savings doesn't preclude a strong recovery, while adding "spending out of excess savings won't be one of its major drivers." (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1013 ET - The oil industry has gone through years of weak investment in exploration and drilling of new oil fields due to low prices, and these upstream projects kept dropping in 2020 to a 15-year-low due to coronavirus. But Simmons Energy says the current environment is looking good for a bounceback of activity, even as many uncertainties remain. "The confluence of reflating economic activity and oil demand and redefined E&P capital allocation, should lead to tightening market balances, normalizing \uc2 OPEC\uc2 -plus spare capacity and the inception of an upstream reinvestment cycle," the firm says. "This isn't a bad combination for conventional energy, for now." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0901 ET - \uc2 Columbia Threadneedle\uc2 's "Return to Normal Index" for April climbs to 66% from 60% last month. All components show improvement in the wake of mass vaccination. "The monthly jump has made it clear that widespread vaccination can fuel behavioral and economic recovery," the asset management firm says. The index's components include return to school, travel, entertainment, workplace and brick-and-mortar shopping. \uc2 Columbia Threadneedle\uc2  says the greatest rise is happening in in-person schooling. The firm says it's optimistic, but is monitoring infection rates. "If we see a large spike in cases, the momentum carrying the U.S. to our normal range could slow." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0758 ET - US stock futures are sharply higher as investors react to Friday's stronger than expected March jobs report showing the US added 916,000 jobs while the unemployment rate dipped to 6%. Markets were closed for Good Friday. \uc2 GameStop\uc2  is off 15% premarket after the videogame retailer announced plans to sell up to 3.5M shares of its common stock. Later this morning the \uc2 Institute for Supply Management\uc2  is expected to release its March Services report measuring economic activity in services. On Friday the government is due to release its look at inflation on the wholesale level with the March producer price index. Inflation has been a concern on Wall Street in the wake of the $1.9T stimulus plan recently signed into law by President Biden, and current plans for a $2T infrastructure package. S&P futures are up 23 points. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 ET - The USD/JPY could trend upward this week thanks to the faster pace of Covid-19 vaccine distribution in the U.S. relative to Japan, IG says. Rising risk-taking sentiment is also likely to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair, the U.K. broker says, adding that projected growth in first-quarter U.S. corporate earnings should limit any falls in U.S. stock markets despite President Biden's tax-increase plan. The USD/JPY may break 111.00 this week if both U.S. stocks and Treasury yields advance, IG says. The USD/JPY is at 110.58. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0417 ET - Gold prices are calm on a day of reduced trading volumes. The precious metal is down 0.1% at $1,726.40 a troy ounce. After sliding during March, gold's recent drop may have run its course, say analysts at \uc2 Fitch Group\uc2  . Meanwhile, "the likely rise in inflation in March and April could support gold prices in the coming weeks, especially if it rises faster than bond yields," they say. The same could be the case if the U.S. dollar remains weak, and that "will keep a floor under prices and feeds into our view that 2021 prices will overall average on par with 2020," they say. The WSJ Dollar Index--which measures the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a basket of others--is flat on the day. (david.hodari@wsj.com; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 05, 2021 11:11 ET (15:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020210405eh45001ns\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
