\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Says Its Market  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  Are Working -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2020\par 19:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1304 ET - The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  's efforts to stabilize rocky financial markets are showing early signs of success, a top \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official says. Senior Deputy Gov. \uc2 Carolyn Wilkins\uc2  said the \uc2 BOC\uc2  's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 --which have so far expanded the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet to around C$385B, compared with C$120B in early March--have resulted in more activity and narrower spreads. As an example, Wilkins cites the bankers' acceptance market, where she says spreads relative to overnight index swaps have fallen from a high of 120 basis points to around 20 basis points. "It's early days, but we can already see the benefits of these programs," Wilkins said. (kim.mackrael@wsj.com; @kimmackrael)\par \par \uc2 1144 ET - A disconnect between the global-growth outlook and market pricing means the safe-haven dollar should continue to appreciate in coming months, \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  says. \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  's "unbounded" balance sheet in response to coronavirus is unlikely to weigh on the dollar without a "robust" global economy, which will "get worse before it gets better," TD analysts say. Fiscal support from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  also leaves the dollar "better placed on the growth theme," they say. "A rise in US-China tensions poses additional risks." The dollar index rises 0.5% to a four-day high of 99.5990, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1116 ET - Investors' withdrawal of about $140 billion from taxable bond mutual bonds and $28 billion from municipal bond funds dragged bond prices further down during the coronavirus-led selloff, which shows the growing role of mutual funds and exchange-traded funds in the U.S. credit market, says S&P Global Ratings. "These record outflows added to the pressure on bond prices during the recent credit market dislocations as fund managers sold assets to meet redemption demands," says the rating agency. Mutual funds, money market funds, and ETFs held nearly $6 trillion in corporate, government, and municipal bonds as of the end of 2019, making up 16% of the U.S. bond market, it adds. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1101 ET - Joe Gruber is joining the \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2  as its research director, the bank said. Gruber currently serves as deputy director of the International Finance Division for the Fed's Board of Governors in Washington. While at the Board, Gruber was a special advisor for then Vice Chair Stanley Fischer and then for Vice Chair for Supervision \uc2 Randal Quarles\uc2  . Gruber has a long \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tenure, having joined the board in 2001. The \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2  , based on the public comments of its last two leaders, has been one of the more reliably hawkish regional \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  branches, with its leaders frequently worried about low rates, inflation and financial stability risks. The bank also hosts the Fed's premier research conference each year in Jackson Hole, Wyo., although that's in doubt this year due to the coronavirus crisis. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1058 ET - Investors have increased their bets against sterling due to renewed worries about U.K.-EU trade deal talks, ING says, citing CFTC data for the week to April 28. Sterling net positions in the futures market fell for the ninth consecutive week, which is "likely a function of the resurgence of some shorts related to Brexit uncertainty," ING analysts say in a note. "GBP net positioning is now at -4% of open interest, but the risk of additional downside in the gauge is quite material, especially when adding the rising concerns about the economic fallout of extended lockdown measures in the U.K." GBP/USD falls 0.6% to 1.2424 and EUR/GBP declines 0.1% to 0.8779. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1055 ET - The shape of the economic recovery will have little impact on company defaults over the next three years in Europe, say \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's credit strategists. In a an equally sharp fall and rebound in economic activity-- the so-called V-shaped recovery--as well as an U-shaped recovery, which takes longer for the economy to recover, the damage is done by the initial economic contraction, they say. "The real question for credit investors, then, is not so much the shape of the recovery but rather the pace of the initial drop. The bigger the economic contraction in 2Q, the higher the realised defaults will be under any future economic scenario," they add. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1052 ET - After almost 50 days in lockdown, Peru is planning to start to reopen the economy even though the number of Covid-19 cases continues to rise sharply. A decree was published over the weekend allowing mining, and some construction and fishing to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Authorities say the reopening will take place in stages over the next four months, and require companies to adhere to strict health rules to reduce the spread of the coronavirus. Pollster Datum says the plan is backed by 70% of Peruvians, many without jobs due to the economic collapse caused by the lockdown. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1051 ET - \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  doesn't expect any rating action on Italy by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investors Service and DBRS Morningstar at their scheduled reviews on May 8. On April 24, S&P Global Ratings affirmed Italy's rating, while \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  last week downgraded Italy in an out-of-schedule review. "Also in their rating announcement statement \uc2 Fitch\uc2  highlighted that the risk for further action comes if the government does not implement a credible economic growth and fiscal strategy that enhances confidence that general government debt-to-GDP will be placed on a downward path over time," \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  says. "Therefore, suggesting no imminent risk of further action," \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1047 ET - As eurozone governments ramp up government bond issuance to cover the fiscal costs of the coronavirus pandemic, they raised a record EUR123 billion via conventional government bond issuance in April, says \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  . This is the highest monthly conventional issuance amount since the inception of the euro, they say. The strong pace of issuance is set to continue, says \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  . It expects EUR100 billion-EUR110 billion of gross conventional issuance in May. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1043 ET - The euro could fall to $1.05 this summer from $1.0911 at present if EU leaders continue to disagree over how to support the eurozone's most vulnerable economies through the coronavirus crisis, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "While the Eurozone's huge current account surplus may continue to lend support, sentiment in the EUR will be eroded if politics turn sour on the issue of fiscal coherence within the region," Rabobank \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2  . In coming weeks, EUR/USD could drop to 1.08 on safe haven demand for the dollar as U.S.-China tensions resume and global economic data shows the "scale of the demand side crisis that the world economy faces," she says. After that, a drop towards 1.05 over the summer "cannot be ruled out," she adds. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1029 ET - Relatively low yields on local debt issued by emerging markets, relative to the selloff during the 2008 global financial crisis, shows EM outflows aren't material, says \uc2 Jan Dehn\uc2  , head of research at \uc2 Ashmore Group\uc2  . Commenting on \uc2 IMF\uc2  data last week showing that the coronavirus pandemic triggered the worst outflow in the history of EM debt, with $100 billion fleeing the asset class, Dehn says that EM local bond yield stood at 4.87% as of Friday, while yield on EM local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign debt spiked to briefly 8.99% in 2008. The softer selloff is driven by lower inflation rates, lighter positioning among foreign investors due to 10 years of U.S. dollar strength, and more developed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tools in emerging countries. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1022 ET - New orders for manufactured goods drop 10.3% to a seasonally adjusted $445.8B in March as efforts to contain the coronavirus disrupted supply chains and quashed demand. That was the biggest monthly fall in records dating back to 1992. Economists surveyed by WSJ had expected a decline of 9.2%. New orders for durable goods--items designed to last at least three years--decreased 14.7%, compared with a preliminary estimate of a 14.4% fall. That was the second-biggest decline on record. (jeffrey.sparshott@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 04, 2020 13:04 ET (17:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200504eg54002m8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
