\par\par \b \uc2 Liquidity Dries Up in Canadian Corporate Bond Market -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 22:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1745 ET - Investment funds are having trouble finding bids for corporate bonds in Canada's fixed-income market, with risk aversion keeping buyers at bay. "Liquidity in the secondary bond market is worse than it was in the fourth-quarter of 2008," says Ian McKinnon, Chairman of the Canadian Bond Investors' Association. Many funds are suffering redemption requests, he says, which is causing them to sell bonds in their portfolios to meet the demand for money. In many cases, the lack of bids is causing the funds to take losses. "There's now a cost to the withdrawals," he says. CBIA wrote a letter to the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday asking for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the bond markets to help get things moving again. (vipal.monga@wsj.com; @vipalmonga)\par \par \uc2 1734 ET - \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says some 200,000 people in New Zealand will lose their jobs and the economy will contract at least 10% in 2Q as pandemic-control measures bite. The bank estimates economic activity will drop by a third during the four-week national lockdown that began mid-week. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  's New Zealand chief economist \uc2 Dominick Stephens\uc2  says the job losses will push the unemployment rate to high single digits from 4% now while the estimated contraction in the economy is "completely unprecedented in our lifetimes." Finance Minister \uc2 Grant Robertson\uc2  yesterday acknowledged the economic downturn will be much worse than the GFC and that job losses will be substantial even with government measures such as generous wage subsidies. (stephen.wright@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1649 ET - Borrowing at the Fed's discount window nearly doubled over the last week. Discount-window borrowing stood at $50.77B as of Wednesday and was at $28.22B on March 18. That's a lot compared with negligible interest in recent years, but it's still well below the $400B taken in late 2008. Separately, Fed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps with other central banks have exploded amid dollar pressure: On Wednesday they hit $206.1B from near-zero levels the week before. The Fed has been extending the counterparties and terms of its liquidity swaps to ensure no shortage of dollars among major central banks. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1628 ET - The US dollar weakens broadly against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as jobless claims rise an unprecedented 3.28M, even as a more than 6% gain by the Dow puts the index in a bull market. US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens about 1.5% against the euro and 1.6% against the yen. "With the number of new policy measures likely to slow, and economic numbers like these starting to roll in, we still think it is too early to call a trough in broader risk or a top in the Dollar, even as some asset classes that have seen significant government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  start to find their footing," \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. (jonathan.vuocolo@wsj.com; @jonvuocolo)\par \par \uc2 1333 ET - Sterling's rise to a nine-day high of $1.2181 Thursday is driven by a pick-up in market sentiment following efforts by global leaders to tackle the coronavirus pandemic, says John Goldie, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Argentex Group\uc2  . "The pound's assumed dynamic as a risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in prime evidence today as stocks were up, volatility down, U.S. dollar down, all in sync with sterling appreciation--essentially risk is back on, for this afternoon at least," Goldie says. Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government action in response to coronavirus "may have placated investors' fears for the time being" but it seems "very unlikely that the difficulties are over in financial markets," he says. In coming weeks, the "choppiness in markets will remain." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1322 ET - The coronavirus has dramatically lowered the human race's ecological footprint and could have a lasting effect on shopping habits, says David Utley, consumer analyst at \uc2 GlobalData\uc2 . For instance, Utley says, U.K. shoppers are now buying seasonal vegetables grown in the U.K. instead of ones imported from other European countries due to the border lockdown. He says the virus could alter the shopping habits of the 41% of U.K. consumers who say they want to buy environmentally friendly goods but don't. "This will have to be reinforced by government and corporate policy if it's going to stick in the long term, especially after the pandemic ends," Utley says. (dieter.holger@wsj.com; @dieterholger)\par \par \uc2 1311 ET - There is "still no trace" of green stimulus in any of the current government plans to fight the economic fallout from the coronavirus, says consultancy Sustainable Market Strategies. Even in Europe, where leaders have previously pledged to use the next crisis to boost the green economy, no funding has been announced to fight climate change as part of planned spending or actions, SMS says. "While we are still counting the human and economic damage associated to this crisis, one can look at another, long-term silent killer yet to be tamed by governments: climate change," it says. (dieter.holger@wsj.com; @dieterholger)\par \par \uc2 1307 ET - European stocks rise on optimism over global efforts to offset the economic impact of the coronavirus pandemic. The Stoxx Europe 600 gains 2.5%, the FTSE 100 increases 2.2%, the DAX adds 1.3% and the CAC-40 is up 2.5%. The \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  on Wednesday passed a $2.2 trillion rescue package to provide relief to citizens and businesses affected by coronavirus. Meanwhile, G20 leaders on Thursday said they were willing to do whatever it takes to tackle coronavirus. "The united front from the world leaders helped market confidence, because as far as the west is concerned, the battle is in its infancy," \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  analyst David Madden says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1253 ET - Canadian PM Trudeau says senior officials are pressing chartered banks and credit-card companies to offer relief for income-strapped individuals and households, either through extended grace periods or lower interest rates. At a press briefing, Trudeau says high credit-card interest rates pose "a significant challenge for many Canadians at this point. That is why we are encouraging [the financial sector] to take action and alleviate the burden for Canadians." Canadian households are among the most indebted in the developed world, based on a share of after-tax income. In Canada, nonmortgage personal debt outstanding as of January stood at roughly C$630B. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1242 ET - Thursday's rise in U.S. jobless claims to a record high of 3.2 million signal a potential global recession, says Unigestion. The asset manager says its analysis points to a 5% contraction in GDP across 2Q in the U.S. and in Europe, bringing 2020 GDP estimates to falls of 2.6% in the U.S. and 3.5% in Europe respectively. The jobless claims mean its readings now are consistent with a global recession. "The fiscal answer outside the U.S. is insufficient and there remains a global imbalance as the situation appears to be getting more serious by the hour," says Unigestion's Head of Macroeconomic Research Florian Ielpo. "For now, we remain concerned at market liquidity which may be at risk." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1238 ET - The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  's recent efforts to ease the U.S. dollar funding squeeze should particularly benefit the Norwegian krone and sterling, ING says. "NOK has been the key victim of the latest market rout as the krone low liquidity heavily exaggerated the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  downfall," ING analyst \uc2 Petr Krpata\uc2  says. The pound "suffers from the worst current account position" in the G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  space and the external funding needs make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  vulnerable, he says. "Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may still decline further versus USD, but the tentative signs of an improvement on the USD funding front suggest a reducing risk of GBP and NOK meltdown during risk-off days." GBP/USD rises 1.6% to 1.2073 and USD/NOK falls 2.5% to 10.4707. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1230 ET - The euro could fall as low as $1.05 if the coronavirus pandemic worsens and boosts demand for the safe haven dollar, says \uc2 Bhanu Baweja\uc2  , chief strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  . However, "once the dust settles" and the coronavirus crisis eases, the dollar should "begin to weaken" and EUR/USD could rise to $1.15, he says. "We're not expecting much depreciation of the dollar as we need to see the rest of the world recovering and we don't think that growth will improve significantly any time soon." EUR/USD rises 1.2% to a one-week high of 1.1028 as the dollar falls for the fourth consecutive day after \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  action to reduce the dollar funding squeeze and a sharp rise in weekly U.S. jobless claims. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 26, 2020 17:45 ET (21:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200326eg3q004df\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
