\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  Not In  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2 's 'Active Toolkit' -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par 20:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1431 ET - A senior \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  official reiterated during a press conference that it is not the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's practice to engage in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Deputy Governor \uc2 Lynn Patterson\uc2  was asked during a press conference in Hamilton, Ontario, if the \uc2 BoC\uc2  would step in to support the Canadian dollar if it fell below a certain level. "We don't typically do any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It's not part of our policies that we would focus on," Patterson replied. She said that if the loonie fell dramatically the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would want to understand the reasons behind the change, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is not part of an active toolkit that we use." The \uc2 BoC\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to affect movements of the Canadian dollar in more than 20 years. (kim.mackrael@wsj.com; @kimmackrael)\par \par \uc2 1206 ET - The \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  's banking committee has advanced nominees to fill vacancies at the \uc2 U.S. Export-Import Bank\uc2  to the full chamber for approval. A favorable vote would allow ExIm to resume its proper work, including what \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO \uc2 Dennis Muilenburg\uc2  says is $40 billion in a backlog of deals--many for planes. But that is only one hurdle ExIm faces. Mr. Muilenburg says that in September, the export credit agency comes up for reauthorization, which could stir new political battles about the institution. (robert.wall@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1124 ET - European financial and mining stocks are on the back foot after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's rate hold and dovish outlook on the economy offset any potential lift from its announcement of a cash injection for banks. German, Italian and Spanish banks are among the biggest pan-European fallers, while in London, \uc2 Fresnillo\uc2 , \uc2 Glencore\uc2  and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  are among the biggest mining losers. "The move initially propelled the major eurozone equity markets into positive territory, but it was short-lived," says David Madden at \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  . "The underlying message from the \uc2 ECB\uc2  was negative, and that played out in the stock market." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1102 ET - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been pre-emptive rather than reactive, says \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , senior global investment strategist at \uc2 Principal Global Investors\uc2  . By announcing new liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier than the market was anticipating, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  has moved ahead of the curve and provided strong reassurance to the banking sector, and to the real economy to some extent that it will provide support if required, Shah says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  announced a new series of targeted longer-term refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  but these TLTROs themselves are unlikely to provide strong stimulus, but demonstrating its intent to act is half the job done, Shah says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1054 ET - Shares in European banks fall sharply after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday that it would leave interest rates unchanged at least until the end of the year, longer than investors had expected. \uc2 Banco de Sabadell\uc2  shares, which fall 7.7%, and \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  stock, which is down 5.3%, are among the worst performers. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  , \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  , \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  , Credit Agricole, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  and \uc2 UBS\uc2  , among others, fall between 3.3% and 4.9%. (pietro.lombardi@dowjones.com; @pietrolombard10)\par \par \uc2 1053 ET - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's stimulus announcement is having a ripple effect in the federal-funds futures market, where traders are pricing in higher odds of a Federal Reserve rate cut by year-end. Futures recently showed a 14% chance of the Fed lowering short-term interest rates by the end of the year, a jump from 5.9% Wednesday and 6% a week ago, according to \uc2 CME\uc2  . The futures, while volatile, have over the past few months pointed to growing conviction among traders that the Fed will either leave rates unchanged throughout 2019 or lower them. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  's move, spurred by weakening growth in the eurozone and the rest of the world, gives traders more reason to believe that yields across developed markets will remain lower for longer. (akane.otani@wsj.com; @akaneotani)\par \par \uc2 1052 ET - The \uc2 ECB\uc2  's current strategy clearly follows the idea of "if you can't beat it, try to avoid it for as long as possible," says ING economist \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  . A severe downswing in the economy would now require unprecedented measures. But whatever the outcome, "today's \uc2 ECB\uc2  meeting clearly means that \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  will be the first \uc2 ECB\uc2  president who never hiked interest rates during his term in office," says Mr. Brzeski. (nina.adam@wsj.com; @Nina_Adam_)\par \par \uc2 1046 ET - The initial market reaction to \uc2 ECB\uc2  's announcement that it is keeping interest rates unchanged until next year as well as launching another round of targeted longer-term refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  is underwhelming, \uc2 David Riley\uc2  , chief investment strategist at \uc2 BlueBay Asset Management\uc2  , says. European government bonds have rallied but equities, including bank stocks, in the region are down and the euro is modestly weaker, which suggests investors appear to be more focused on the "substantial" but realistic downgrade to the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's forecasts for Eurozone growth in 2019 to 1.1% compared with 1.7% forecasts in December. "The fear that investors have is that despite President [Mario] Draghi's talk of optionality, the reality is that the \uc2 ECB\uc2  has limited monetary ammunition to offset downside risks," he says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1030 ET - \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  once again managed to "over deliver" relative to market expectations but the structural headwinds for the eurozone remain the same as before, says \uc2 Marc Ostwald\uc2  , global strategist and chief economist at \uc2 ADM Investor Services International\uc2  . The structural headwinds will continue to ensure that as long as euro-area governments continue to "under deliver" on reforms and in broader policy terms, the impact of \uc2 ECB\uc2  policy measures will continue to be heavily muted," Ostwald says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1026 ET - Euro "bears have fresh ammunition" after \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's monetary policy decision on Thursday, says Audrey Childe-Freeman, chief strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Knowledge, as the euro falls to a 16-week low of $1.1229. Postponing the first interest-rate increase and cutting eurozone economic growth forecasts to such an extent was surprising, Childe-Freeman says. The euro could fall to $1.10 if it drops below $1.12, she adds. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1025 ET - Even though the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  changed its forward guidance and lowered economic growth forecasts, the main driver for the euro is likely to be the U.S. and China trade discussions, says Audrey Childe-Freeman, chief strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Knowledge. The euro fell to a 16-week low of $1.1229 on Thursday after the \uc2 ECB\uc2  announcement, but "the \uc2 ECB\uc2 -driven euro bearish case that we identify today is a short-term story," says Childe-Freeman. "Over the next few weeks though, the euro outlook will be more a function of international considerations than of domestic and \uc2 ECB\uc2  factors," she says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0954 ET - The fall in eurozone government bond yields accelerate as \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  holds his press conference, giving insight into the Governing Council's decisions and slashing 2019 growth and inflation forecasts. Italian bond yields continue to lead eurozone peers lower, trading 10 basis points lower at 2.52%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . German Bund yields trade 3.7 bps lower at 0.091%. French, Belgian, Spanish, Portuguese and Irish bond yields trade lower by between 5 and 6 basis points. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  delayed its forecast for when it expects to raise interest rates. It now sees interest rates staying at current levels at least through the end of 2019 and announced a new series of targeted longer-term refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 07, 2019 14:31 ET (19:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190307ef37002yp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
