\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Latest on Trump, U.S. Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 1,529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2018\par 17:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and U.S. politics. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1129 ET - The \uc2 Institute for Supply Management\uc2  says its nonmanufacturing index for November will be released on Thursday instead of Wednesday this week. The index-tracking a wide range of US industries such as health care, finance, construction and agriculture, will come out at 10am ET on Thursday, December 6 after President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  declared Wednesday to be a National Day of Mourning to pay tribute to former President George H.W. Bush. The \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  and the Nasdaq will close in his honor, and Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  's testimony before Congress on Wednesday was also canceled. (harriet.torry@wsj.com; @HarrietTorry)\par \par \uc2 1040 ET - The US-China trade spat has rocked soybeans prices this year and Chicago futures are still down 11% over the past six months. It will take more than vague agreements to spark a sustainable soy rally, investors say, and soybeans' global trade flow has also irreparably shifted, with China having upped its imports from Brazil and Argentina in anticipation of US tariffs. "Even if China came back to the US market in a huge way... they would have to buy record amounts every week from now until the end of the marketing year in August," says Zaner Group's Brian Grossman. Soybeans are up 1.8% at $9.11 a bushel. (david.hodari@wsj.com; @Davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 1027 ET - A major seafood group says it's pleased by the US-China tariff truce, but warns American jobs are still at stake. Both US importers and exporters of seafood have been caught in the trade dispute: China in July slapped 25% duties on a range of imported American fish, in response to tariffs imposed by Washington. Dozens of varieties of fish imported to the US from China -- including some caught in America and processed in China -- were then hit by 10% tariffs from the Trump administration. The \uc2 National Fisheries Institute\uc2  says it's pleased the US will not boost those tariffs but that more progress is needed. "Whether it's imports or exports the jobs impacted are here in the U.S.," the group's spokesman said. (jesse.newman@wsj.com; @jessenewman13)\par \par \uc2 0956 ET - \uc2 BMO Capital Markets\uc2  has revised downward its economic growth forecast for Canada in 2019 after Alberta ordered crude-production cuts, equal to 325,000 barrels a day or nearly 9% of output. \uc2 BMO\uc2  now anticipates 2019 growth in Canada of 1.8%, versus earlier estimate of 2%. In particular, growth in 1Q next year is now expected to slow significantly to 0.7% annualized. "This is a modest downgrade to the growth call but there's plenty of uncertainty heading into next year," \uc2 BMO\uc2  told clients. Firm doesn't expect Alberta cut to affect \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  rate-policy plans, although cautioning \uc2 BoC\uc2 's tone may change about outlook when \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues latest rate decision Wednesday and Gov \uc2 Stephen Poloz\uc2  delivers speech Thursday. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 0937 ET - \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  said Alberta's decision to cut crude production by nearly 9% in 2019 should lead to price spread between Western Canadian Select and WTI to narrow by "a couple of bucks" from Friday's $29 level. Firm's economists, though, indicate the production cut in Alberta comes at a time when other factors are already working to compress the spread, which hit a high of $50 in October. Those factors include US refineries coming back online, increased use of rail to ship crude, and the completion next year of \uc2 Enbridge\uc2 's Line 3 pipeline replacement project. Scotiabank says now spread could shrink to the $15 to $20 once everything is accounted for. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 0916 ET - Rising political uncertainty ahead of the \uc2 European Parliament\uc2  elections in May 2019 could further undermine recent positive economic-growth dynamics, says \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  . "Slower GDP growth, higher interest rates and pressures to increase capital expenditure will pose a challenge for debt dynamics in highly indebted sovereigns," says Michele Napolitano, senior director at the ratings firm. He says that changing policy mixes will make fiscal consolidation more challenging, while politics becomes less conducive to tight fiscal policies and reforms. Among the 22 Western European countries in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's report, Austria and Finland have positive ratings outlooks, while Italy, the U.K. and San Marino are on negative outlook. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0853 ET - \uc2 Imperial Oil\uc2 's CEO \uc2 Rich Kruger\uc2  says Alberta's mandatory 8.7% oil output cut, announced Sunday, is risky and could lead to unintended consequences. "Our view remains that free markets work and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  carries trade risks and sends a negative message to investors about doing business in Alberta and \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says in a statement. "Unfortunately, this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears not to recognize the investment decisions companies have made to access higher value markets." He says \uc2 Imperial\uc2  secured room for exports on pipelines and invested in rail infrastructure to get its oil to market, but Alberta's decision introduces a "new risk which will unfortunately need to be considered as it relates to future investments." (vipal.monga@wsj.com; @vipalmonga)\par \par \uc2 0814 ET - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of oil-exporting countries such as the Russian ruble and the Mexican peso rise on the back of the decision to extend the OPEC+ agreement. The extension "is a clear positive for oil exporters," says ING. USD/MXN is last down 1.5% at 20.0999, having fallen to a three-week low 20.008. USD/RUB is last down 0.8% at 66.5755, having fallen to a 10-day low 65.6870. On Friday, the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  (\uc2 OPEC\uc2  ) and Russia agreed to extend oil output cuts until end-2018. Among G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ING expects the Norwegian krone and Canadian dollar to be "the key beneficiaries." USD/CAD falls 0.9% to 1.3177, having dropped to a two-week low of 1.3160. EUR/NOK is down 0.4% at 9.6885. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0754 ET - Most emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are up on Monday following the agreement between the U.S. and China to take a pause in the current trade war, with the U.S. agreeing not to raise tariffs on China imports on Jan. 1 and to keep a truce for 90 days. The U.S.-China temporary truce "is a clear positive for risk assets," ING says. "Coupled with the growing market perception of a less hawkish Fed, it should keep them supported," it says, adding that emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are "placed well, with EM Asia expected to outperform in the near-term." USD/RUB falls 0.6%, USD/BRL is down 0.8% and USD/ZAR loses 1.2%, though the Turkish lira falls slightly after inflation data earlier, while the Indian rupee is also lower. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 ET - Oil prices surge in morning trade, with both Brent crude and West Texas Intermediate up roughly 4%. The oil market, which has seen both benchmarks fall by more than 30% since reaching four-year highs at the start of October, is being buoyed by Russia saying it will join Saudi Arabia in again cutting crude output, and by the US-China trade truce. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analysts say the truce reached at the G-20 summit is good news as a further trade conflict escalation would have weighed on oil demand in the two leading oil-consumer countries. But they add that "at least equally important...was the agreement between Russia's President Putin and the Saudi Arabian Crown Prince bin Salman...to extend the production agreement into next year." (christopher.alessi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 ET - The Hungarian forint leads Monday's rally of central and eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with EUR/HUF earlier falling to a one-week low of 322.04. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says that this is because the forint has lagged recently when emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have risen. Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are staging a rally in the aftermath of the U.S. agreeing with China on a temporary trade dispute truce. EUR/HUF is last down 0.3% at 322.79. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 ET - The temporary U.S.-China trade truce increases the chances of a permanent deal and a Chinese economic boost, says \uc2 Fidelity International\uc2  . The fund manager notes Washington has agreed to delay tariff hikes on $200 billion of Chinese goods. Recent stock-market volatility, continued Federal Reserve tightening and U.S. soybean crops previously destined for China rotting in fields might have given President Trump pause for thought, says \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  fund manager Raymond Ma. A Chinese pledge to buy more U.S. goods may also have helped. "More broadly, we see increasing opportunities among Chinese stocks amid the retreat in valuations this year, as the economy becomes more consumption-driven," he says. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 03, 2018 11:29 ET (16:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020181203eec30027v\par }\page {