\par\par \b \uc2 Global Energy Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,570 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2018\par 17:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering Energy markets. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1152 ET - The FTSE 100 index closes up 1.2% at 7062.41, led by sharp gains for mining and oil companies after an easing in U.S.-China trade tensions following the weekend's G20 summit and as oil prices rise sharply. The U.S. agreed to defer further tariff increases on Chinese goods for now, boosting risk appetite and helping stocks, while oil prices were boosted by Canada opting to cut oil production and reports that Saudi Arabia and Russia will extend their agreement to manage supply. Copper miner Antofagasta is the top riser, up 7.9%, and \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  rise by 7% and 4%, respectively. Oil giant \uc2 BP\uc2  rises 2.3%. However, shares in \uc2 GlaxoSmithKline\uc2  slide 7.6% after the drug company said it will buy Nasdaq-listed cancer drug developer \uc2 Tesaro\uc2  for $5.1 billion. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1134 ET - Commenting on Alberta's extraordinary decision to cut crude-oil production, Canadian Finance Minister \uc2 Bill Morneau\uc2  said Monday "the biggest, most important," way the federal government can contribute to bolster the struggling domestic energy sector is by getting the Trans Mountain pipeline expansion built. At a New York event hosted by Politico , Morneau acknowledged western Canada faces challenges because of depressed price for Alberta crude. Ottawa's goal is to work with Alberta "to deal with the acute problems of getting access to markets." The Liberal government acquired control of the Trans Mountain after \uc2 Kinder Morgan\uc2  threatened to walk away due to political uncertainty. Now, the project faces new delays due to a recent court ruling, which the Liberal government has vowed to address. (paul.vieira@wsj.com, @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1111 ET - An interesting part of Canada's provincial government decision to step in to fix its oil market through forced production cuts is that "recent pricing had appeared to be working towards a 'market solution,'" say analysts at Tudor Pickering. Early last month, heavy-oil benchmark WCS was priced $43-plus less than the US benchmark WTI, but as of Friday had clawed back to only a $28 differential, although maybe that's because some investors saw the writing on the wall for government action. Whatever the case, Houston-based Tudor says the cuts are "hugely positive" for WCS. " We see upside for January pricing to a $15/bbl diff to WTI." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1037 ET - The only thing airline investors worry about more than rising fuel prices is falling prices, and even Brent's push above $60 a barrel can't shake concerns about capacity creep. US carriers lose early gain to all trade down, with latest schedules suggesting a 4.1% rise in 1Q 2019 capacity, a slight rise on last week. Hunter Keay at \uc2 Wolfe Research\uc2  notes United's early-2019 expansion has climbed to 6.1% from 4.7% a month ago. United will also be a focus when Mesa reports 4Q earnings after the close, with the commuter operator seeking additional flying for the Chicago-based major. Mesa the only US carrier in positive territory in early trade. (doug.cameron@wsj.com; @dougcameron)\par \par \uc2 0956 ET - \uc2 BMO Capital Markets\uc2  has revised downward its economic growth forecast for Canada in 2019 after Alberta ordered crude-production cuts, equal to 325,000 barrels a day or nearly 9% of output. \uc2 BMO\uc2  now anticipates 2019 growth in Canada of 1.8%, versus earlier estimate of 2%. In particular, growth in 1Q next year is now expected to slow significantly to 0.7% annualized. "This is a modest downgrade to the growth call but there's plenty of uncertainty heading into next year," \uc2 BMO\uc2  told clients. Firm doesn't expect Alberta cut to affect \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  rate-policy plans, although cautioning \uc2 BoC\uc2 's tone may change about outlook when \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues latest rate decision Wednesday and Gov \uc2 Stephen Poloz\uc2  delivers speech Thursday. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 0946 ET - As Canada delivers on oil-production cuts and with \uc2 OPEC\uc2  saying it will do the same, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says oil prices that shouldn't have fallen so much recently will head back up in a hurry. "We expect the announcement of an \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cut this coming week to extend the rally started Sunday evening (December 2) and push Brent oil prices likely above the mid-$60/bbl level," it says. "Given the likely cautious phrasing of such an agreement - with a focus on stabilizing inventories - it will be the evidence of a decline in exports as well as an end to the ongoing larger-than-seasonal inventory builds that will bring Brent prices back slightly above $70/bbl early in 2019." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0937 ET - \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  said Alberta's decision to cut crude production by nearly 9% in 2019 should lead to price spread between Western Canadian Select and WTI to narrow by "a couple of bucks" from Friday's $29 level. Firm's economists, though, indicate the production cut in Alberta comes at a time when other factors are already working to compress the spread, which hit a high of $50 in October. Those factors include US refineries coming back online, increased use of rail to ship crude, and the completion next year of \uc2 Enbridge\uc2 's Line 3 pipeline replacement project. Scotiabank says now spread could shrink to the $15 to $20 once everything is accounted for. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 0857 ET - \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  lifts its estimate of oil recoverable from an offshore resource called the Stabroek Block in Guyana, one of its key offshore projects, by 25% to more than five billion barrels. The company has now made 10 oil discoveries in the South American nation's waters, and anticipates beginning production there with 120,000 barrels of crude a day there by early 2020. \uc2 Exxon\uc2  controls 45% of the Stabroek Block, with oil companies \uc2 Hess\uc2  and \uc2 Nexen\uc2  controlling 30% and 25%, respectively. (micah.maidenberg@wsj.com, @micahmaidenberg)\par \par \uc2 0853 ET - \uc2 Imperial Oil\uc2 's CEO \uc2 Rich Kruger\uc2  says Alberta's mandatory 8.7% oil output cut, announced Sunday, is risky and could lead to unintended consequences. "Our view remains that free markets work and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  carries trade risks and sends a negative message to investors about doing business in Alberta and \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says in a statement. "Unfortunately, this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears not to recognize the investment decisions companies have made to access higher value markets." He says \uc2 Imperial\uc2  secured room for exports on pipelines and invested in rail infrastructure to get its oil to market, but Alberta's decision introduces a "new risk which will unfortunately need to be considered as it relates to future investments." (vipal.monga@wsj.com; @vipalmonga)\par \par \uc2 0826 ET - Oil prices rise sharply as Russia and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  signaled plans to cut production, while Canada's Alberta province says it too will be mandating output reductions from companies. A trade-war ceasefire between US and China leaders Trump and Xi is adding further tailwinds since it curbs fears of a slowing global economy that would hurt oil demand. "Oil prices are surging as Canada cuts after the Saudi and Russia pact that we see ending the oil bear market," says Peter Cardillo at Spartan Capital. "In summary; short covering along with new position buying will make for a stellar day upward move." WTI was recently 3.9% higher at $52.85/bbl. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0814 ET - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of oil-exporting countries such as the Russian ruble and the Mexican peso rise on the back of the decision to extend the OPEC+ agreement. The extension "is a clear positive for oil exporters," says ING. USD/MXN is last down 1.5% at 20.0999, having fallen to a three-week low 20.008. USD/RUB is last down 0.8% at 66.5755, having fallen to a 10-day low 65.6870. On Friday, the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  (\uc2 OPEC\uc2  ) and Russia agreed to extend oil output cuts until end-2018. Among G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ING expects the Norwegian krone and Canadian dollar to be "the key beneficiaries." USD/CAD falls 0.9% to 1.3177, having dropped to a two-week low of 1.3160. EUR/NOK is down 0.4% at 9.6885. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0811 ET - Qatar's decision to abandon the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  next month caught oil market observers by surprise. But "Qatar leaving \uc2 OPEC\uc2  is largely symbolic as it's a negligible member of the group," says Ehsan Khoman, head of Middle East research at \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  . Mr. Khoman notes that as \uc2 OPEC\uc2  's 11th-largest producer, Qatar comprises just 1.8% of the group's 33.3 million barrels a day in crude output. Nonetheless, the move means \uc2 OPEC\uc2  loses a key diplomatic liaison between Arab Gulf states like Saudi Arabia--the de-facto head of the cartel--and Iran. The withdrawal comes as Qatar has faced an economic blockade from the Saudis over allegations that it finances terrorism, which Doha has denied. \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and its production allies are slated to meet in Vienna this week. (christopher.alessi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 03, 2018 11:52 ET (16:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020181203eec3002ah\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
