\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Latest on Trump, U.S. Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 1,544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2018\par 21:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and U.S. politics. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1502 ET - \uc2 USDA\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Sonny Perdue\uc2  says he is heartened by the trade truce between the US and China, but has few details yet on how it will impact farmers. Speaking at an agricultural conference in Chicago, Perdue says it's unclear yet which--or how many--farm goods China will purchase as part of the recent deal. "We're waiting to flesh that out and determine the numbers and see the ships loading," Perdue said, adding that the ball is in China's court now to prove it's willing to make structural changes to rebalance the US-China trade relationship. "If they do want access to the highest quality products they know where they are and we're willing to sell them as long as we can get progress on other issues," he said. (jesse.newman@wsj.com; @jessenewman13)\par \par \uc2 1134 ET - Commenting on Alberta's extraordinary decision to cut crude-oil production, Canadian Finance Minister \uc2 Bill Morneau\uc2  said Monday "the biggest, most important," way the federal government can contribute to bolster the struggling domestic energy sector is by getting the Trans Mountain pipeline expansion built. At a New York event hosted by Politico , Morneau acknowledged western Canada faces challenges because of depressed price for Alberta crude. Ottawa's goal is to work with Alberta "to deal with the acute problems of getting access to markets." The Liberal government acquired control of the Trans Mountain after \uc2 Kinder Morgan\uc2  threatened to walk away due to political uncertainty. Now, the project faces new delays due to a recent court ruling, which the Liberal government has vowed to address. (paul.vieira@wsj.com, @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1133 ET - The US-China truce on further escalation in the countries' trade dispute hasn't lit a fire under hog futures, at least not yet. Despite China's growing need for imported pork, and low prices in the US, traders anticipated late last week some ceasefire at the G20 in the months-long trade spat between the countries, and there remains much uncertainty as to whether China will change its practices on intellectual property and other policies the Trump administration wants. February-dated contracts recently down 0.2% at 67.4 cents a pound, more lightly traded December contracts down 0.4%. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 1131 ET - Signs of reapproachment between China and the U.S. is certainly good news and "certainly a big step in the right direction," says \uc2 Salman Ahmed\uc2  , chief investment strategist at Lombard Odier Investment Managers. However, it doesn't reflect a definitive shift in the relationship between the two countries. While Lombard Odier IM expects the trade conflict issue to resume as both sides seem far from a fully settled position, for now it expects the market to reject some of the most extreme scenarios which have been weighing on sentiment and this will be reflected in asset prices. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1129 ET - The \uc2 Institute for Supply Management\uc2  says its nonmanufacturing index for November will be released on Thursday instead of Wednesday this week. The index-tracking a wide range of US industries such as health care, finance, construction and agriculture, will come out at 10am ET on Thursday, December 6 after President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  declared Wednesday to be a National Day of Mourning to pay tribute to former President George H.W. Bush. The \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  and the Nasdaq will close in his honor, and Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  's testimony before Congress on Wednesday was also canceled. (harriet.torry@wsj.com; @HarrietTorry)\par \par \uc2 1040 ET - The US-China trade spat has rocked soybeans prices this year and Chicago futures are still down 11% over the past six months. It will take more than vague agreements to spark a sustainable soy rally, investors say, and soybeans' global trade flow has also irreparably shifted, with China having upped its imports from Brazil and Argentina in anticipation of US tariffs. "Even if China came back to the US market in a huge way... they would have to buy record amounts every week from now until the end of the marketing year in August," says Zaner Group's Brian Grossman. Soybeans are up 1.8% at $9.11 a bushel. (david.hodari@wsj.com; @Davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 1027 ET - A major seafood group says it's pleased by the US-China tariff truce, but warns American jobs are still at stake. Both US importers and exporters of seafood have been caught in the trade dispute: China in July slapped 25% duties on a range of imported American fish, in response to tariffs imposed by Washington. Dozens of varieties of fish imported to the US from China -- including some caught in America and processed in China -- were then hit by 10% tariffs from the Trump administration. The \uc2 National Fisheries Institute\uc2  says it's pleased the US will not boost those tariffs but that more progress is needed. "Whether it's imports or exports the jobs impacted are here in the U.S.," the group's spokesman said. (jesse.newman@wsj.com; @jessenewman13)\par \par \uc2 0956 ET - \uc2 BMO Capital Markets\uc2  has revised downward its economic growth forecast for Canada in 2019 after Alberta ordered crude-production cuts, equal to 325,000 barrels a day or nearly 9% of output. \uc2 BMO\uc2  now anticipates 2019 growth in Canada of 1.8%, versus earlier estimate of 2%. In particular, growth in 1Q next year is now expected to slow significantly to 0.7% annualized. "This is a modest downgrade to the growth call but there's plenty of uncertainty heading into next year," \uc2 BMO\uc2  told clients. Firm doesn't expect Alberta cut to affect \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  rate-policy plans, although cautioning \uc2 BoC\uc2 's tone may change about outlook when \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues latest rate decision Wednesday and Gov \uc2 Stephen Poloz\uc2  delivers speech Thursday. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 0937 ET - \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  said Alberta's decision to cut crude production by nearly 9% in 2019 should lead to price spread between Western Canadian Select and WTI to narrow by "a couple of bucks" from Friday's $29 level. Firm's economists, though, indicate the production cut in Alberta comes at a time when other factors are already working to compress the spread, which hit a high of $50 in October. Those factors include US refineries coming back online, increased use of rail to ship crude, and the completion next year of \uc2 Enbridge\uc2 's Line 3 pipeline replacement project. Scotiabank says now spread could shrink to the $15 to $20 once everything is accounted for. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 0916 ET - Rising political uncertainty ahead of the \uc2 European Parliament\uc2  elections in May 2019 could further undermine recent positive economic-growth dynamics, says \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  . "Slower GDP growth, higher interest rates and pressures to increase capital expenditure will pose a challenge for debt dynamics in highly indebted sovereigns," says Michele Napolitano, senior director at the ratings firm. He says that changing policy mixes will make fiscal consolidation more challenging, while politics becomes less conducive to tight fiscal policies and reforms. Among the 22 Western European countries in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's report, Austria and Finland have positive ratings outlooks, while Italy, the U.K. and San Marino are on negative outlook. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0853 ET - \uc2 Imperial Oil\uc2 's CEO \uc2 Rich Kruger\uc2  says Alberta's mandatory 8.7% oil output cut, announced Sunday, is risky and could lead to unintended consequences. "Our view remains that free markets work and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  carries trade risks and sends a negative message to investors about doing business in Alberta and \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says in a statement. "Unfortunately, this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears not to recognize the investment decisions companies have made to access higher value markets." He says \uc2 Imperial\uc2  secured room for exports on pipelines and invested in rail infrastructure to get its oil to market, but Alberta's decision introduces a "new risk which will unfortunately need to be considered as it relates to future investments." (vipal.monga@wsj.com; @vipalmonga)\par \par \uc2 0814 ET - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of oil-exporting countries such as the Russian ruble and the Mexican peso rise on the back of the decision to extend the OPEC+ agreement. The extension "is a clear positive for oil exporters," says ING. USD/MXN is last down 1.5% at 20.0999, having fallen to a three-week low 20.008. USD/RUB is last down 0.8% at 66.5755, having fallen to a 10-day low 65.6870. On Friday, the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  (\uc2 OPEC\uc2  ) and Russia agreed to extend oil output cuts until end-2018. Among G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ING expects the Norwegian krone and Canadian dollar to be "the key beneficiaries." USD/CAD falls 0.9% to 1.3177, having dropped to a two-week low of 1.3160. EUR/NOK is down 0.4% at 9.6885. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 03, 2018 15:02 ET (20:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020181203eec3002xu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pic