\par\par \b \uc2 Nafta Collapse May Not Be Dire for Canada, US Economies -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2017\par 13:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1142 GMT - Terminating NAFTA would hurt the Canadian and US economies, but it would be manageable, according to a new analysis by Bank of Montreal Capital Markets. Canada's economy could be 1% smaller over five years post-Nafta, while the US would take a 0.2% hit. A weaker dollar and government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help mitigate some of the damage in \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Douglas Porter, chief economist at \uc2 BMO\uc2 . But he says that none of the negative effects would happen if Nafta stays as is. "It's what the Brits would call an 'own goal,'" he says. David Jacobson, former US Ambassador to Canada warns that more than the economies could be hurt; the failure of talks could hurt political and security ties between the two countries. (vipal.monga@gmail.com; @vipalmonga)\par \par \uc2 1219 GMT - If bitcoin starts to fall, there isn't much support all the way down to $8,000, ETX Capital says. Nevertheless, bitcoin is surging and ETX reports "sky-high demand" for the cryptocurrency. "We're seeing strong flows with leveraged traders almost entirely long [bitcoin]," it says, adding that bitcoin looks overbought but "not exceptionally so." Resistance lies at $9,700 and if tested, "a push higher could signal further gains and be the last leg towards $10,000," says ETX Capital. Bitcoin currently trades at $9,625.12, according to the CoinDesk Bitcoin Price Index. The cryptocurrency benefits from reports that \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  may use CME futures. \uc2 CME\uc2  is set to launch bitcoin futures in December. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1206 GMT - \uc2 The Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  may fail to pass the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's stress tests, while Lloyds and \uc2 Barclays\uc2  ' consumer credit exposure will also be closely watched, \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  says ahead of the annual tests' results on Tuesday. The trading platform says \uc2 RBS\uc2  's failure to pass last year's tests may be repeated in the face of ongoing settlement issues in the U.S. However, CMC also says that both Lloyds and \uc2 Barclays\uc2  are increasingly exposed to U.K. consumer credit, and the stress test scenario will model a major economic slowdown that could reduce headroom for both banks.\par \par \uc2 (adam.clark@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1105 GMT - Economists kept their forecasts for Brazil's economic growth steady, according to a weekly \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll with about 100 financial institutions. The median forecast for GDP growth in 2017 remained at 0.73% from the previous week. Meanwhile, economists reduced their forecasts for inflation this year to 3.06% from 3.09%. Next year, economists expect Brazil 's economy to grow 2.58% and year-end inflation to reach 4.02%. (samantha.pearson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1155 GMT - Bitcoin is nearing the $10,000 pass-through level which clearing platform \uc2 LedgerX\uc2  eyed in its option introduced a week ago, trading at $9,613.48 on Monday, according to CoinDesk Bitcoin Price Index. The rise in bitcoin, as well as in other cryptocurrencies, pushed the cryptocurrency market capitalization above $300 billion. Bitcoin accounts for more than half of the market. Bitcoin's upward trajectory is fuelled by reports that \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  is considering whether to provide its clients access to trade \uc2 CME\uc2  's bitcoin future contracts upon their launch in December, despite the fact that \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  , \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's CEO, is known to be a fierce bitcoin critic. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1142 GMT - The South African rand rises to a one-month high versus the U.S. dollar, after only S&P downgraded South African local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt to junk on Friday. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  kept the government bonds' rating at its lowest investment grade level. USD/ZAR is last down 0.9% at 13.7616, having fallen to a low of 13.7198, according to Factset. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  did, however, place the country under review for 90 days to evaluate the trajectory of its public finances, within which the annual budget statement is expected to be released. And ING says USD/ZAR could rise to 14.50 or 14.60 since "ZAR faces some sizable risks over the next three months." (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1133 GMT - The amount of U.S. corporate debt held by overseas investors continued to rise steeply over the past two years to a high of $9.1 trillion, according to credit strategists at \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase & Co\uc2  . That came as U.S. companies took advantage of cheap borrowing costs and foreign demand for U.S. corporate bonds increased as their yields surpassed those in other parts of the world. Overseas investors now hold 38% of U.S. corporate debt. Meanwhile, U.S. corporate leverage increased to a peak of 47% of gross domestic product, a level not seen since the financial crisis. ( christopher.whittall@wsj.com )Christopher Whittall O: +44 (0)\par \par \uc2 1123 GMT - Credit strategists at \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase & Co\uc2  . have increased their forecast of corporate bond purchases from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next year to EUR4 billion to EUR5 billion per month. That's up from an initial expectation of EUR3 billion per month. The upgrade comes after the release of minutes from the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's October meeting in which its governing council said the private-sector purchase programs would remain "sizable" and would not decline in strict proportion to the scaling down of the broader asset purchase program. "This adds to a growing body of rhetoric over the past few weeks pointing towards a less than proportionate taper to the corporate bond purchase program," the strategists write.(christopher.whittall@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1059 GMT - \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says "there is scope for meaningful depreciation in USD/JPY" after \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  member \uc2 Hitoshi Suzuki\uc2  said it is possible the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may make slight changes to its yield-curve management program if price growth approaches the 2% target. Even though Japan's inflation is nowhere near the target, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  thinks "the odds are for the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to adjust, or drop, its yield-curve management control program," which aims to keep 10-year Japanese government bond yields at zero. USD/JPY is flat at 111.24, having fallen earlier in overnight trade on the news and on U.S. dollar weakness. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1043 GMT - Greece may not need as much credit from its third bailout program as initially planned, the head of the eurozone's bailout fund European Stability Mechanism \uc2 Klaus Regling\uc2  told a German newspaper. "We are pleased that the amount of the loan for Greece may remain significantly below the ESM program's limit of 86 billion euros," Mr. Regling said. He added that unused funds could be used to repay loans to other creditors, he said. (nektaria.stamouli@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1018 GMT - The U.S. dollar is weak on Monday against the euro and the pound, and until earlier against the Japanese yen too, although all three \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have good news to trade on as well. EUR/USD and GBP/USD trade higher, at 1.1929 and 1.3340, respectively. The DXY dollar index is down 0.1% at 92.7120 and ING expects it to stay above chart support at 92.35 if signs of progress on U.S. tax reform emerge. The data calendar is full for the dollar this week, with the trade balance due Tuesday, second estimate of 3Q GDP due Wednesday, personal consumption expenditures due Thursday, and Institute of Supply Management manufacturing due Friday. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1006 GMT - Greece and its international creditors could reach a staff-level agreement on the completion of the bailout review before a meeting of eurozone finance ministers on Dec 4, according to officials. A delegation of international inspectors will resume talks with Greek officials in Athens Tuesday. If an SLA is reached by next Monday's Eurogroup, the disbursement of the next bailout tranche, which is expected to be around EUR5 billion could take place in January. "Provided the government in Athens keeps up its good work, the third review could be concluded swiftly and the next disbursement might happen as early as the beginning of 2018," European Stability Mechanism managing director \uc2 Klaus Regling\uc2  told Handelsblatt newspaper over the weekend. (nektaria.stamouli@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 27, 2017 07:42 ET (12:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020171127edbr0019j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
