\par\par \b \uc2 Canadian Dollar Falls for 10th Straight Day to New Multiyear Low\b0\par\par\uc2 By David George-Cosh   \par \uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2016\par 22:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 TORONTO--The Canadian dollar fell against the U.S. dollar for the 10th consecutive day as plummeting oil prices continued to weigh on the commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and push it to new multiyear lows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was recently at C$1.4530 from C$1.4358 late Thursday, according to data provider CQG. The greenback rose as high as C$1.4555 or 68.70 U.S. cents, during the Friday trading session, a level it hasn't reached since April 29 2003.\par \par \uc2 The loonie fell early during the overnight trading session as a sharp drop in oil and market expectations of a looming \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  rate cut next week pulled investors away from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With an uncertain outlook in oil prices and several troubling economic indicators, some economists expect the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to cut interest rates during its rate meeting next week.\par \par \uc2 "It's very rare to see these moves extend in this way," said Scotiabank chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Shaun Osborne. "We're well overdue a rebound in the Canadian dollar at this point."\par \par \uc2 Other market watchers, like BMO Capital Markets chief economist Doug Porter, believes the Canadian dollar should trade around current levels until oil prices bottom out.\par \par \uc2 "Lower oil prices are still a net negative for the Canadian economy and for the Canadian dollar," Mr. Porter said.\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Osborne expects the Canadian dollar to recover from its recent lows, he also doesn't expect to see the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  cut rates next week. The Canadian dollar has depreciated enough that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may want to take a wait-and-see approach to gauge whether the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may drive foreign business investment into the country over the next several months before making a move on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 A rate cut will also "convey to the market casual disregard to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's performance going forward," he added.\par \par \uc2 The sudden move in the loonie has also brought to light the slim chance the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the first such time it would do so since 1998. Mr. Osborne doesn't believe it'll happen, but given the extraordinary moves, it isn't outside the realm of possibility, he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to David George-Cosh at david.george-cosh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 15, 2016 16:33 ET (21:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160115ec1f003sg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
