\par\par \b \uc2 Loonie's Slide Unusual But BoC Not Worried -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2015\par 19:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:33 ET - Scotiabank's Shaun Osborne describes the rapid ground the Canadian dollar tumbled this week as "unusual". The 3% slide is something the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  could find itself \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in, especially in an environment of excessive volatility. But Osborne notes the BoC has taken a largely hands-off approach to the loonie, only opting to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exceptional circumstances. Furthermore, direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Canadian dollar would run counter to the need to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  low to spur the nonenergy export sector. "Current circumstances do not even warrant 'smoothing' action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure markets clear properly," says Osborne. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com; @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 13:29 ET - The cause of the outbreak of shiga toxin-producing E.coli tied to Chipotle (CMG) has yet to be determined and it's possible it may never be known. Officials have said produce was the probable cause, but haven't pinpointed a specific item. Finding the source has been a challenge in part because customers eat many of the same food items--and the foods have short shelf lives and are turned over quickly--says Matthew Wise, a CDC epidemiologist involved in the investigation. But "we really want to get to the bottom of this and I'm hopeful we will." Wise adds it's not yet clear when the outbreak will be declared over. (betsy.mckay@wsj.com; @betswrites)\par \par \uc2 13:25 ET - \uc2 Fiat Chrysler\uc2  (FCAU) is carrying far-more inventory than its US light-vehicle market share suggests it should support. WardsAuto.com estimates the company had 654,717 vehicles in inventory at the end of November, 20% more than a year ago and the equivalent of 86 days' worth of supply; the industry average is 65. FCAU has 18% of the industry's light-vehicle inventory even though its market share through November was only 13%. The company's year-earlier inventory share was 15%, Wards says. Inventory gluts are often seen as catalysts for deep discounts and rebates, or a push to sell more units to rental fleets. (john.stoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:23 ET - The Fed is likely to make no more than four interest-rate increases in 2016, although volatility in global assets could derail that view, says Rob Waldner, chief strategist and head of the multi-sector team in fixed income at \uc2 Invesco\uc2 . The bond market currently appears to expect about two interest-rate increases next year, and a faster pace could negatively affect market sentiment, he says. Tighter financial conditions or a stronger US dollar could mean less tightening through the interest-rate channel, he adds. In 2016, potential pressure on risk assets will originate from global sources, including commodity volatility and emerging-markets growth disruptions, he says. \uc2 Invesco\uc2  sees opportunities in US-focused credit sectors, which it says are attractively valued. (daisy.maxey@wsj.com;@DaisyMaxey)\par \par \uc2 13:15 ET - The \uc2 FCC\uc2  sent letters this week to \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  (TMUS), \uc2 Comcast\uc2  (CMCSA) and \uc2 AT&T\uc2  (T) seeking more information about new services they are offering which critics say might circumvent net-neutrality rules. CMCSA's Stream and TMUS' Binge On offer customers streaming video which isn't subject to customers' data caps while T has a sponsored-data plan which also provides a potential way around caps. Critics worry such services could become loopholes of sorts in new net-neutrality rules by giving Internet providers' house-brand streaming-video services a big advantage over independent rivals. The companies say the services are clearly allowed. FCC Chairman Tom Wheeler says the letters don't represent an investigation while GOP commissioners say the letters reflect the vagueness of net-neutrality rules. (john.mckinnon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:11 ET - The Fed's board of governors voted unanimously to raise the discount rate, the interest rate on short-term loans through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's discount window, by a quarter-point to 1%, effective today. Technically, though, discount rates are set by board members at each of the 12 regional Fed banks--subject to approval by the Washington board. The Fed said Wednesday it was approving requests from 10 banks to increase the discount rate. The \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  followed suit later in the day. And today, the board says the \uc2 Minneapolis Fed\uc2  also requested and received an increase in its discount rate. That means all 12 banks are now on the same page in raising the discount rate, which stood at 0.75% since February 2010. (ben.leubsdorf@wsj.com; @BenLeubsdorf)\par \par \uc2 13:08 ET - As \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  (C) adds 2 more women to its board, Chairman \uc2 Mike O'Neill\uc2  says the company will benefit from outgoing \uc2 Intel\uc2  (INTC) President Renee James' expertise as C focuses on digital and mobile banking and \uc2 Ellen Costello\uc2 's years of banking experience. She spent 30 years at Canada's \uc2 BMO\uc2 , rising to head of the company's US \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Oracle\uc2  (ORCL) announced earlier this week that James is also joining its board. C now has 18 directors, 5 of them women. (christina.rexrode@wsj.com; @chris_rexrode)\par \par \uc2 13:00 ET - Some junk bonds are weakening along with the midday slide in stock prices. Not surprising, debt from energy companies is among the hardest hit as oil prices fall anew. But some portfolio managers and traders say the market is still relatively firm if energy names are excluded. More bonds are still up on the day than down, according to MarketAxess data. The iShares ETF which tracks the junk-bond market is down 0.8%. (mike.cherney@wsj.com; @Mike_Cherney)\par \par \uc2 12:56 ET - The latest rout in energy and metals prices is burying ETFs tracking the drillers and miners of oil and gold. The SPDR S&P Oil & Gas Exploration and Production ETF (XOP) hit an intraday low of $29.27, the lowest since July 13, 2009 while the SPDR S&P Metals and Mining ETF (XME) is poised to hit a fresh record low and has fallen by 54% from the year's high. While investors continue to pound commodities prices amid tepid demand and oversupply, producers face an additional challenge: debt levels. (leslie.josephs@wsj.com; @lesliejosephs)\par \par \uc2 12:51 ET - It's a tough 2 years ahead for \uc2 Viacom\uc2  (VIAB), and now S&P downgrades the media company to the brink of junk territory. It no longer thinks VIAB can get leverage/earnings ratio below 3 "because we forecast that its revenue and Ebitda growth will be minimal for the next few years." S&P has the lowest grade on VIAB of the 3 major ratings firms. Shares are down 3.7% at $39.98, putting the month's slump at 20%. (keach.hagey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:44 ET - Citi thinks O'Reilly (ORLY) is so far ahead of its fellow auto-part retailers that valuation concerns should take a back seat to the company's supply-chain network keeping it out front for years. The stock is up 1/3 this year and has a 25 P/E--25% above the past 3 years' average. Citi now sees the stock remaining at that valuation as ORLY should "continue to consistently beat expectations and consolidate a fragmented market." It ticks up EPS views through 2017 while upgrading ORLY to buy after years at neutral and boosts its price target $20 to $295. Shares have fallen 8% after their post-3Q pop to fresh record highs amid some sector concerns, including today's 0.8% decline to $255.62 as the broader market sags. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com; @patmsheridan)\par \par \uc2 12:30 ET - \uc2 General Mills\uc2  (GIS) CEO Ken Powell says that fostering cereal sales growth is its top priority in the US. So far in FY16--which will end in May--its sales are up 1%, he said, and the cereal industry is down less than 1%, compared to a 3% drop in FY15. "I think it's improving because of the quality of renovation and innovation, not only from us, but from other players," he said. "How people think about food is changing, which gives us, in a positive way, a new set of renovations and modifications to capitalize on those new desires." Americans have always looked for healthy attributes, like vitamins or whole grains. But now they are also looking for simpler ingredients and fewer artificial ones, he said. (annie.gasparro@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 17, 2015 13:33 ET (18:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151217ebch0031t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
