\par\par \b \uc2 Poloz: Too Early to Gauge Liberals' Plans -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2015\par 18:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:39 EDT - Journalists today are keen to hear from Bank of Canada Gov. Poloz about the potential boost to economic growth from the incoming Liberal government's pledge to loosen the fiscal purse strings and deliver C$60B in infrastructure spending over a decade. During his press conference, he declined to make a judgment, saying he needs to take a wait-and-see approach until there's concrete measures to analyze. Poloz added he hasn't spoken with PM-elect Justin Trudeau but looked forward to working with him. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 12:27 EDT - Another batch of big write-downs is coming in the oil patch as low prices continue to take their toll. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  predicts E&P companies will take $20B of charges in their 3Q reports. That would add to what's already a record amount for the sector at $59.8B by energy consultant \uc2 IHS\uc2  Herold's count. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  expects the largest write-downs as a share of market cap to come from Chesapeake (CHK), Encana (ECA) and Apache (\uc2 APA\uc2 ). The investment bank says all 3 could take charges equal to more than 1/3 of their caps. (ryan.dezember@wsj.com; @RyanDezember)\par \par \uc2 12:27 EDT - \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  cuts \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (TWTR) to underweight from equal-weight, citing limited user growth, declining engagement along with already high ad load and pricing. Firm slashes its price target to a Street low $24, saying consensus estimates for FY16 and FY17 are overly optimistic about \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's "monetization opportunity." Morgan says TWTR's platform and core use case "remain unclear to us." It says user engagement trends are falling, while \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and Instagram add more users and take up more of their time each day. TWTR shares are off 6.7% at $28.85, and off 43% over the last year. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com; @patmsheridan)\par \par \uc2 12:22 EDT - HR executives and vendors of HR technology speak frequently about the promise of applying big-data analysis to workforce problems. But many privately acknowledge that getting a handle on even the most-basic information, such as total headcount, remains a challenge. After asking a number of HR executives what share of employees are connected to their firms' core workforce software, 70% was the average answer, said Max Simkoff, vice president of analytics at Cornerstone onDemand, at the HR Technology Conference in Las Vegas. He noted the reason is it's costly for employers to convert from legacy HR systems inherited through acquisitions or decisions made locally by overseas offices. Many consultants now offer the service of syncing up that data, but it remains expensive and complex. (lauren.weber@wsj.com; @laurenweberWSJ)\par \par \uc2 12:14 EDT - MIT, which is at the forefront of climate-change research, says it won't sell investments in the fossil-fuel industry despite a year-long student campaign to persuade university managers to divest. Most climate researchers attribute rising global temperatures to carbon emissions from fossil fuels. In a teleconference today on its new 5-year plan to fight climate change, MIT President \uc2 L. Rafael Reif\uc2  says the school will keep investments in more than 200 companies involved in the fossil-fuels industry. In its annual accounting released in June, the university reported its endowment funds were at $13.5B. (lee.hotz@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:03 EDT - Valeant's (VRX) pounding is disproportionately hurting hedge funds, some traders say. The drug market is an industry darling, with some 22% as of mid-year owned by hedge funds, according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ; \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  (PFE) was at 1%. "To the extent that you've had some hedge funds already struggling on the performance side, this isn't helping," says Justin Wiggs of \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2  as to today's VRX tumble adds to recent sharp price declines. On Sept. 28, VRX skidded 17% after members of Congress urged their committee chairman to subpoena VRX to provide more information about drug-price increases. "Let's say you managed to talk your boss into keeping it for fundamental reasons then, and now it gets blown up again, there's a decent chance that trade is no longer in your hands," says Wiggs. (corrie.driebusch@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:54 EDT - Mondelez (MDLZ) is billing its sustainability goals--which will notably reduce carbon-dioxide emissions, address deforestation and otherwise ease environmental impacts--as a "fight against climate change." But the global snack maker is also quick to point out that new targets will also reduce costs and generate efficiencies in the company's supply chain. Food makers have been working on ways to improve profitability as US packaged-food sales remains muted. MDLZ says today that by 2020, it will reduce CO2 emissions from manufacturing by 15%, cut water usage in manufacturing by 10% in areas where water is most scarce and reduce total manufacturing waste by 20%--all relative to its 2013 baseline. (annie.gasparro@wsj.com; @Annie_Gasparro)\par \par \uc2 11:50 EDT - Bonds from Valeant (VRX) are also down, but the decline is muted compared to drug maker's stock plunge on a short seller's report. That suggests debt investors aren't as alarmed about the company's long-term prospects. A 2021 bond trades at 99.6c on the dollar, compared to 102.9c yesterday, according to MarketAxess data. Shares are down 25% at $110.25. (mike.cherney@wsj.com; @Mike_Cherney)\par \par \uc2 11:48 EDT - Is the tinder for a long-desired heating up of inflation building? A report from the Center for Financial Stability says that in September its proprietary measure of money supply rose by 3.8%, a stronger pace than the 1.9% year over year gain seen in the same month a year ago. In almost all the ways the CFS parses the data, there's a growing amount of money out there. In years past, that would have been seen by most economists as a sign inflation would rise. And that would be welcome with inflation so far below the Fed's 2% target. Problem is, linkages between money supply and inflation have broken down, so it's unclear whether this liquidity will do what it once did. In the CFS report, the biggest drivers of money growth were commercial bank savings deposits, demand deposits and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On a broader trend, the CFS money measures ebbed between 2013 and 2014, rose modestly from 2014, and have cooled just a bit over recent months. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:48 EDT - Bank of Canada Gov. Poloz says during his press conference that he doesn't see the need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's still-hot housing market. Action may have to be taken if, for example, Canada was in a "prolonged period of weak growth and low employment." But there's no sign that a housing-market crash scenario is present, says Poloz. "The vulnerabilities that we've identified in the household sector continue to edge higher and that's of course a rational response to interest rates. I don't think that's an inappropriate thing." He adds the financial market's underwriting culture is a good "first line of defense" which can protect the housing market from spilling over into the broader economy. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com; @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 11:46 EDT - Market research from NDCHealth and IMS leads Leerink to predict 3Q sales for \uc2 Bristol-Myers\uc2 ' (BMY) Opdivo will be $225-245M, well ahead of the investment bank's $200M estimate. The data also suggest the anti-PD-1 drug, approved for melanoma and lung cancer, "is now tracking to annualized $1.2B US sales." With a lung-cancer label that's broader than for rival Keytruda from Merck (MRK) and the recent approval of a combined Opdivo and BMY's Yervoy for melanoma, Leerink expects accelerated growth in Opdivo sales "the next several quarters." BMY reports 3Q results Tuesday. (ron.winslow@wsj.com; @ronwinslow)\par \par \uc2 11:43 EDT - The EPX oil-and-gas producer index is down about 25% this year, but \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  says E&P stocks are still expensive. It notes the index is trading around 7.6 times the component companies' estimated future earnings, well above the historical average of 5.8. Wells expects the current multiple to fall when 2016 spending plans and production outlooks are dialed back to account for $50 oil. "As crude expectations have finally become more reasonable, we have begun looking for an entry point. Stock valuations still remain lofty, but in our opinion, the risk/reward is getting more attractive." (ryan.dezember@wsj.com; @RyanDezember)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 21, 2015 12:39 ET (16:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151021ebal0038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
