\par\par \b \uc2 Traders: Valeant Slump Hurting Hedge Funds -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2015\par 18:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:03 EDT - Valeant's (VRX) pounding is disproportionately hurting hedge funds, some traders say. The drug market is an industry darling, with some 22% as of mid-year owned by hedge funds, according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ; \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  (PFE) was at 1%. "To the extent that you've had some hedge funds already struggling on the performance side, this isn't helping," says Justin Wiggs of \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2  as to today's VRX tumble adds to recent sharp price declines. On Sept. 28, VRX skidded 17% after members of Congress urged their committee chairman to subpoena VRX to provide more information about drug-price increases. "Let's say you managed to talk your boss into keeping it for fundamental reasons then, and now it gets blown up again, there's a decent chance that trade is no longer in your hands," says Wiggs. (corrie.driebusch@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:54 EDT - Mondelez (MDLZ) is billing its sustainability goals--which will notably reduce carbon-dioxide emissions, address deforestation and otherwise ease environmental impacts--as a "fight against climate change." But the global snack maker is also quick to point out that new targets will also reduce costs and generate efficiencies in the company's supply chain. Food makers have been working on ways to improve profitability as US packaged-food sales remains muted. MDLZ says today that by 2020, it will reduce CO2 emissions from manufacturing by 15%, cut water usage in manufacturing by 10% in areas where water is most scarce and reduce total manufacturing waste by 20%--all relative to its 2013 baseline. (annie.gasparro@wsj.com; @Annie_Gasparro)\par \par \uc2 11:50 EDT - Bonds from Valeant (VRX) are also down, but the decline is muted compared to drug maker's stock plunge on a short seller's report. That suggests debt investors aren't as alarmed about the company's long-term prospects. A 2021 bond trades at 99.6c on the dollar, compared to 102.9c yesterday, according to MarketAxess data. Shares are down 25% at $110.25. (mike.cherney@wsj.com; @Mike_Cherney)\par \par \uc2 11:48 EDT - Is the tinder for a long-desired heating up of inflation building? A report from the Center for Financial Stability says that in September its proprietary measure of money supply rose by 3.8%, a stronger pace than the 1.9% year over year gain seen in the same month a year ago. In almost all the ways the CFS parses the data, there's a growing amount of money out there. In years past, that would have been seen by most economists as a sign inflation would rise. And that would be welcome with inflation so far below the Fed's 2% target. Problem is, linkages between money supply and inflation have broken down, so it's unclear whether this liquidity will do what it once did. In the CFS report, the biggest drivers of money growth were commercial bank savings deposits, demand deposits and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On a broader trend, the CFS money measures ebbed between 2013 and 2014, rose modestly from 2014, and have cooled just a bit over recent months. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:48 EDT - Bank of Canada Gov. Poloz says during his press conference that he doesn't see the need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's still-hot housing market. Action may have to be taken if, for example, Canada was in a "prolonged period of weak growth and low employment." But there's no sign that a housing-market crash scenario is present, says Poloz. "The vulnerabilities that we've identified in the household sector continue to edge higher and that's of course a rational response to interest rates. I don't think that's an inappropriate thing." He adds the financial market's underwriting culture is a good "first line of defense" which can protect the housing market from spilling over into the broader economy. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com; @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 11:46 EDT - Market research from NDCHealth and IMS leads Leerink to predict 3Q sales for \uc2 Bristol-Myers\uc2 ' (BMY) Opdivo will be $225-245M, well ahead of the investment bank's $200M estimate. The data also suggest the anti-PD-1 drug, approved for melanoma and lung cancer, "is now tracking to annualized $1.2B US sales." With a lung-cancer label that's broader than for rival Keytruda from Merck (MRK) and the recent approval of a combined Opdivo and BMY's Yervoy for melanoma, Leerink expects accelerated growth in Opdivo sales "the next several quarters." BMY reports 3Q results Tuesday. (ron.winslow@wsj.com; @ronwinslow)\par \par \uc2 11:43 EDT - The EPX oil-and-gas producer index is down about 25% this year, but \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  says E&P stocks are still expensive. It notes the index is trading around 7.6 times the component companies' estimated future earnings, well above the historical average of 5.8. Wells expects the current multiple to fall when 2016 spending plans and production outlooks are dialed back to account for $50 oil. "As crude expectations have finally become more reasonable, we have begun looking for an entry point. Stock valuations still remain lofty, but in our opinion, the risk/reward is getting more attractive." (ryan.dezember@wsj.com; @RyanDezember)\par \par \uc2 11:43 EDT - \uc2 GM\uc2  executives say its Chinese joint venture was able to maintain performance in 3Q despite falling sales and prices. CFO \uc2 Chuck Stevens\uc2  said guidance in China hasn't changed; the auto maker still expects slower annual growth in the neighborhood of 3%-5% in the coming years. \uc2 GM\uc2  was able to hit its targets in China largely due to better margins from increased sales of SUVs, crossovers and luxury cars. \uc2 GM\uc2  saw a 4.2% decline in wholesales during 3Q, but that was offset by a 171% increase in SUV sales, and a 12% increase in Cadillac sales. Stevens said \uc2 GM\uc2  has yet to feel any impact from the Chinese government's economic stimulus program, but retail sales in October were up 10%. The Chinese joint venture contributed $1B to \uc2 GM\uc2 's bottom line in 3Q and Stevens said they expect that performance to continue in 4Q. (gautham.nagesh@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:41 EDT - Saying "things could not get much better" for \uc2 Reynolds American\uc2  (\uc2 RAI\uc2 ), CLSA downgrades the cigarette maker to underperform. The stock has doubled since early 2014, gains which in part reflects the company's strength following its acquisition of the Newport brand through its purchase of Lorillard. The investment bank does boost its 2016 EPS view more than 5%. Shares are flat today at $47.63. (tripp.mickle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:28 EDT - Executives think more women will eventually ascend to the C-suite, but most don't have exact plans for moving the needle. A new survey from PR firm \uc2 Weber Shandwick\uc2  finds 73% of 327 global executives surveyed think businesses will achieve gender parity at the C-level by 2030. But only 44% of C-level executives say their organization has put in place specific goals for making that happen. The issue doesn't seem to be high on priority lists; 10% of respondents classified achieving gender parity as unimportant while 40% said it was neither important or unimportant. (rachel.feintzeig@wsj.com; @rachelfeintzeig)\par \par \uc2 11:23 EDT - Of note to heating oil suppliers, building operators and air-conditioning retailers is federal climate experts saying the globally average temperature over land and ocean surfaces last month was the highest for September since recordkeeping began in 1880. So far this year has also been the hottest ever. The federal researchers calculate September's average temperature was 1.62 degrees Fahrenheit (0.9 degree Celsius) above the 20th century's average. The prior September high was set last year; last month topped that by 0.19 degree Fahrenheit. (lee.hotz@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:22 EDT \uc2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Sergio Marchionne\uc2  outside \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  Wednesday kept mum on the current status of \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  members' vote on a tentative labor deal. "I hate making prognostications, especially in view of what happened last time," Marchionne said, referring to earlier deal the US union rejected about three weeks ago. It was the first time in more than 30 years union workers voted down tentative deal with a Detroit auto maker. Voting is expected to conclude Wednesday. (mike.spector@wsj.com; @MikeSpectorWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 21, 2015 12:03 ET (16:03 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151021ebal0031l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
