\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Canada Raises $3.5 Billion in Yankee-Bond Offering\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Dummett   \par \uc2 323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 15:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top North American Financial Services Stories\par \uc2 DJTNAF\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Canada's federal government on Wednesday said it raised $3.5 billion from a three-year global bond offering denominated in U.S. dollars and priced at nine basis points over the three-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The issue represents the third time the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  has raised debt in the so-called Yankee bond market since 2012. The latest issue is the biggest yet.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal earlier on Wednesday had reported the expected size and pricing of the planned issue, first announced by the country's finance ministry on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The initial price talk was of a "low-double-digit" spread over the three-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 . A pricing lower than the expected spread indicates investor demand for the issue was strong.\par \par \uc2 The offering comes after Canada issued a $3 billion, five-year U.S.-dollar bond in February 2014, in what was then the country's first foray into Yankee bonds in two years. The February 2012 issue also had raised $3 billion.\par \par \uc2 Canada is one of a handful of countries rated Triple A by the three major bond-rating firms.\par \par \uc2 The government plans to use proceeds from the issue to "supplement and diversify Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," the finance department said. .\par \par \uc2 The country holds its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in its Exchange Fund Account. Assets in this account provide foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity, and the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  also draws from it if it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to counter any short-term disruptions in the Canadian dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't conducted such an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since September 1998, at the height of the Asian financial crisis. However, it joined other major global central banks in a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the Japanese yen in 2011, and the euro in 2000.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ben Dummett at ben.dummett@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTNAF1120150311eb3b0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
