\par\par \b \uc2 CIBC: BoC Traded Fewer Factories For More Condos -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 18:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:04 EDT - As capital inflows poured into Canada from 2010-3, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to neutralize those hot money flows and the higher Canadian dollar they fueled, notes CIBC. Instead, the Mark Carney-led \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tried to offset the impact on GDP growth by keeping interest rates low enough to stimulate housing and domestic consumption. "In effect, monetary and exchange-rate policy traded off more condos for fewer factories, and we see the signposts of that in recent data trends." The investment bank says a lower loonie and higher commodity prices will help shore up exports in the coming months, but effects of plant closures will last. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 11:25 EDT - Some analysts and traders are cautious about the upcoming $21B sale of 10-year notes due at 1pm. They argue that rising prices, driven by concerns about China and Ukraine, could dent demand from buyers looking for cheaper prices. The to-be-issued notes recently yielded 2.74%, the lowest since the October sale. CRT's rate strategists acknowledge "the complete absence of a concession," but still expect demand to hold up as those same concerns could drive safe-haven investors into the auction. Ten-year notes last up 8/32 to yield 2.741%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 10:50 EDT - In one week, the Fed announces its latest policy stance, and many expect at least a tweak to the way it ties the future path of the policy rate to economic conditions. By Columbia Management's count, any change would mark the seventh time the Fed has altered the way it explains when it might start lifting rates from near-zero. The Fed has used: "some time," "extended period," "mid-2013," "late-2014," "late-2015" and now "well past" the time the jobless rate hits 6.5%. Zach Pandl, chair of Columbia's interest-rate committee, sees the Fed shifting to a qualitative approach which assures investors the FOMC is in no hurry to hike rates and that they will only rise gradually once increases begin. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 10:17 EDT - Pimco is turning rosier on growth prospects in the US and the euro zone this year. In a report Wednesday, Pimco says it expects the US economy to grow 2.5%-3% this year, up from a previous call in December for 2.25%-2.75% growth, boosted by spending in the consumer, corporate and public sectors. The firm expects the euro zone to emerge from recession this year with 1%-1.5% growth, up from 0.25%-0.75% previously expected, driven by broad-based improvement in domestic demand. Pimco expects Japan to be the only major developed economy experiencing decelerated growth this year, at 0.5%-1% from 1%-1.5%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1415 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Bank expects rising HUF volatility and further pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely cutting the key policy rate further from the current all-time low of 2.7%. Standard Bank strategist Demetrios Efstathiou notes however that it's not in the government's interests to see much higher EUR/HUF volatility, and/or a much weaker HUF. For a HUF blow-up, the confidence of locals towards the country's Prime Minister Viktor Orban needs to deteriorate significantly, but as they are about to re-elect Mr. Orban with a strong majority, this is not the case. Standard Bank nevertheless expects some volatility on the continued rate cuts, and recommends buying one-year HUF volatility at 10.45%, targeting 15%, and stop-loss at 8.5%. One-year implied volatility in HUF has risen to 10.4% from the lows of 9%, but still far away from peaks of 15-16% seen in 2011 and 2012, he says. On Wednesday, EUR/HUF rose to a two-year high at 314.51 versus 313.31 on Tuesday. (veronika.gulyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:09 EDT - Treasury prices are gapping higher as US trading heats up. The short base that built back up last week is providing more fuel for buying on today's renewed emerging-market anxieties. Ten-year notes are up 11/32 to yield 2.73%; it got up to 2.821% Friday after the upbeat payrolls report, a level that brought out plenty of buyers. Traders see sellers lined up around 2.60%. Today's price rally is notable as it comes ahead of a $21B 10-year note auction. At 2.73%, that would be the lowest rate offered at a 10-year sale since October. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 9:40 EDT - The euro briefly tipped back above $1.39 last hour as German Finance Minister Schaeuble remarked the common-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc's interest rate is too low from Germany's perspective and that he sees no sign of deflation. While it's drifted back down to $1.3890 (still up 0.2% for the day), such comments highlight why the euro has found persistent support despite its region's growth struggles. The ECB last week again held back on cutting interest rates and launching new easing efforts. While more accommodation would help weaker economies within the bloc, the euro zone's diverse membership complicates that decision. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 9:12 EDT - Despite reporting a narrower current-account deficit than expected, Turkey's political and financial situation remains in flux with local elections being held at month's end. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says bet against the lira by buying EUR/TRY, which it says likely rises back toward TRY3.20; the pair was last up 0.1% at TRY3.1159. On the financial front, the investment bank says the risk for the lira is more about how Turkey will be able to fund any deficit after January data showed weak portfolio inflows. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1258 GMT [Dow Jones] Ireland will auction EUR 1billion in a reopening of its 3.4% Mar 2024 benchmark Thursday, the first Irish bond auction since September 2010. According to analysts at Citi, "this is another important milestone for the Irish market. Given its clean exit from the Troika programme, Ireland's fully funded position for 2014, its IG status and the successful syndication earlier in January, we expect good demand at tomorrow's supply." (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1240 GMT [Dow Jones] The offered tone noted in EUR/CHF Wednesday moves over to USD/CHF with the spot falling to 0.8750, its lowest level since October 2011. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones says the next key prop is the 2012-2014 support line at 0.8746, which should hold, although only above 0.8890 would alleviate immediate downside pressure. USD/CHF now at 0.8751, EUR/CHF trades at a fresh 1-week low of 1.21545. gary.stride@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 8:37 EDT - The greenback is up broadly against a swathe emerging-market and commodities-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as concerns about China's growth spreads over the developing world. Its impact on metal prices is having particularly heavy pressure on nations which rely on commodities exports, with the Australian dollar down 0.3% at US$0.8947 and the South African rand off 0.4% at ZAR10.8995. Meanwhile, the greenback gains 0.4% to C$1.1149. Yen, the go-to haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  especially when it comes to worries about its region's growth, is up against the dollar at Y102.74. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1226 GMT [Dow Jones] Expectations of more CBR rate tightening are running high with the curve pricing in another 150bps of hikes within the next month notes Citi. Cross \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps are trading some 80bp higher Wednesday, driven by the stop-loss fest in OFZs, where the 1-year is now trading up at 9.35%. Citi says the CBR may step in with bids in OFZs through agent banks in case the situation continues to deteriorate as in 2008-2009, the bank noting also that the 10% level is psychological in both OFZ and CCS markets. The next CBR rate set meeting is Friday 14th March. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 12, 2014 13:04 ET (17:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140312ea3c002x0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
