isk quality. \par --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 14 - CREDIT RISK EXPOSURE OF THE LOAN PORTFOLIO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  As at December 31                                        2013 \par                                --------------------------------------------------------- \par                                                                 Impaired \par                                                                 loans and \par                                                                  loans in \par  (in thousands of $)            High quality   Lower quality     default        Total \par -----------------------------  -------------  --------------  ------------  ------------ \par  \par  Day, call and short-term \par   loans to investment dealer \par   and brokers                      $ 119,000            $ --          $ --     $ 119,000 \par  Public and parapublic \par   sectors                            578,325              --            --       578,325 \par  Members \par   Federation                      14,352,289              --            --    14,352,289 \par   Other                              352,807              --            --       352,807 \par  Other entities included \par   in the group \par   scope of Desjardins \par   Group                            1,411,971              --            --     1,411,971 \par  Loans purchased from \par   Desjardins Group                    57,774              --            --        57,774 \par  Personal                          1,604,871         114,635         5,015     1,724,521 \par Business                           1,915,929       2,087,681        10,326     4,013,936 \par                                 $ 20,392,966     $ 2,202,316      $ 15,341  $ 22,610,623 \par  \par                                                          2012 \par                                                                 Impaired \par                                                                   loans \par                                                                 and loans \par                                High quality    Lower quality    in default     Total \par  \par Public and parapublic \par  sectors                         $ 1,065,328             $ --         $ --   $ 1,065,328 \par  Members \par   Federation                      10,802,676               --           --    10,802,676 \par   Other                              184,978               --           --       184,978 \par Other entities included \par  in the group \par  scope of Desjardins \par  Group                             1,417,168               --           --     1,417,168 \par Loans purchased from \par  Desjardins Group                     88,863               --           --        88,863 \par Personal                           1,287,199          103,013        5,751     1,395,963 \par  Business                          1,588,335        1,739,776       14,868     3,342,979 \par                                 $ 16,434,547      $ 1,842,789     $ 20,619  $ 18,297,955 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHART 1 - LOAN DISTRIBUTION BY BORROWER CATEGORY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AS AT DECEMBER 31, 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click on, or paste the following link into your web browser, to view the associated PDF document (please refer to Page\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of the associated PDF Document).\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COUNTERPARTY AND ISSUER RISK \par  \par  Counterparty and issuer risk is a credit risk relative to different types \par  of securities, derivative financial instrument and securities lending transactions. \par  \par  Desjardins Group's Risk Management Office sets the maximum exposure for \par  each counterparty and issuer based on quantitative and qualitative criteria. \par  The amounts are then allocated to different components based on their needs. \par  \par  To properly managing its risk, CCD assigns a credit rating to each counterparty \par  and issuer, based on four external credit assessment institutions (DBRS, \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , S&P and Fitch). CCD uses this rating to establish exposure limits \par  and to calculate capital requirements using the Standardized Approach. These \par  four credit assessment institutions meet the eligibility criteria of the \par  Basel Accord and are authorized by the AMF and OSFI. \par  \par  A large proportion of CCD's exposure is to different levels of government \par  in Canada, Quebec public or parapublic entities and major Canadian banks. \par  For most of them, the credit rating is A- or higher. In addition, CCD is \par  not directly exposed to the sovereign debt of Greece, Portugal, Italy, Ireland \par  or Spain, and its exposure to U.S. and European financial institutions is \par  low. \par  \par  In its derivative financial instrument and securities lending transactions, \par  which include repurchase and reverse repurchase agreements and securities \par  borrowing and lending, CCD is exposed to counterparty credit risk. \par  \par  \par  CCD uses derivative financial instruments primarily for asset-liability \par  management purposes. Derivative financial instruments are contracts whose \par  value is mainly based on an underlying asset, such as interest rates or \par  exchange rates. The vast majority of derivative financial instruments are \par  traded by mutual agreement between CCD and its counterparties, and include \par  forward exchange contracts, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps, interest rate swaps, credit \par  default swaps, total return swaps, forward rate agreements, and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \par  interest rate and stock index options. Other instruments are exchange-traded \par  contracts or contracts traded through a clearing house, and mainly consist \par  of futures and swaps. \par  \par  The credit risk associated with derivative financial instruments refers \par  to the risk that a counterparty will fail to honour its contractual obligations \par  toward CCD at a time when the fair value of the instrument is positive for \par  CCD. The credit risk associated with derivative financial instruments normally \par  corresponds to a small fraction of the notional amount. The replacement \par  cost and the credit risk equivalent are two measures used to quantify this \par  risk. Replacement cost refers to the current replacement cost of all contracts \par  with a positive fair value. Credit risk equivalent is equal to the sum of \par  this replacement cost and the future credit exposure. \par  \par  CCD limits the credit risk associated with derivative financial instruments \par  by doing business with counterparties that have a high credit rating. Note \par  15, "Derivative financial instruments and hedging activities," to the Consolidated \par  Financial Statements presents derivative financial instruments by credit \par  risk rating and type of counterparty. Based on replacement cost, this note \par  indicates that substantially all counterparties have credit ratings ranging \par  from AAA to A. Furthermore, by purchasing hedges through credit derivatives, \par  such as credit default swaps and total return swaps, CCD can transfer credit \par  risk to a counterparty or hedge itself against various types of risk. \par  \par  CCD also limits credit risk with certain counterparties by entering into \par  master agreements called \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Swaps and Derivatives Association\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  (ISDA) agreements, which define the terms and conditions for the transactions. \par  These agreements are legal contracts that bind the counterparties. Most \par  of CCD's agreements provide for netting to determine the net exposure in \par  the event of default. In addition, a Credit Support Annex can be added to \par  the master agreement in order to request that the counterparties to pay \par  or secure the current market value of the positions when such value exceeds \par  a certain threshold. Taking into account master netting agreements, the \par  risk-weighted balance for all CCD's derivative financial instruments as \par  at December 31, 2013 was $385.2 million, compared to $378.0 million as at \par  December 31, 2012. As at December 31, 2013, the amount of collateral that \par  CCD would have to provide in the event of a downgrade was marginal because \par  the replacement cost was positive for the majority of its contracts. \par  \par  Securities lending transactions are regulated by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Investment Industry Regulatory \par  Organization of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  participation agreements. CCD also uses netting agreements \par  with its counterparties to mitigate credit risk and requires a percentage \par  of collateralization (a pledge) on these transactions. \par  \par  CCD accepts from its counterparties only financial collateral that complies \par  with the eligibility criteria set out in its policies. These criteria allow \par  for the timely realization of collateral, if necessary, in the event of \par  default. The types of collateral received and pledged by CCD are mainly \par  cash and government securities. \par  \par  Additional information about credit risk is presented in Note 22, "Commitments, \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 04-03-14 1836GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  guarantees and contingent liabilities," to the Consolidated Financial Statements. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 15 - LOANS, LETTERS OF GUARANTEE AND CREDIT COMMITMENTS BY ECONOMIC SECTOR\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par As at December 31                              2013