 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Quality of loan portfolio\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHART 2 - GROSS IMPAIRED LOANS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As at December 31\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click on, or paste the following link into your web browser, to view the associated PDF document (please refer to Page\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of the associated PDF Document).\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Allowance for credit losses [ 04-03-14 1836GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The impairment of a loan or a group of loans is determined by discounting future expected cash flows at the interest rate inherent to the financial asset. The allowance is equal to the difference between this amount and the carrying amount. This allowance is presented in deduction of assets under "Allowance for credit losses." To determine the estimated recoverable amount of a loan, CCD discounts the estimated future cash flows at the effective interest rate inherent to the loan. When the amounts and timing of future cash flows cannot be estimated with reasonable reliability, the estimated recoverable amount is determined using either the fair value of the collateral underlying the loan, net of expected costs of realization, or the observable market price for the loan. The collateral may vary depending on the type of loan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Individual allowances\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD first reviews its loan portfolios on a loan-by-loan basis to assess credit risk and determine if there is any objective evidence of impairment for which a loss should be recognized in the Consolidated Statements of Income. Loan portfolios for which an individual allowance has not been established are then included in groups of assets having similar credit risk characteristics and are subject to a collective allowance. Individual allowances totalled $3.3 million as at December 31, 2013, compared to $4.9 million as at the same date one year earlier. These amounts represented 21.8% and 23.6%, respectively, of the gross impaired loan portfolio.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 20 - IMPAIRED LOANS BY BORROWER CATEGORY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par As at December 31                                     2013                                  2012         2011 \par                                                            Individual \par                                                             allowances         Net           Net         Net \par (in millions of $          Gross      Gross impaired        for credit       impaired      impaired    impaired \par  and as a %)               loans           loans              losses          loans         loans        loans \par Day, call and short-term \par  loans to investment \par  dealers and brokers      $   119    $    --        --%          $    --  $         --  $        --  $        -- \par Public and parapublic \par  sectors                      578         --        --                --            -- \par Members \par   Federation               14,352         --        --                --            --           --           -- \par   Other                       353         --        --                --            --           --           -- \par Other entities included \par  in the group \par  scope of Desjardins \par  Group                      1,412         --        --                --            --           --           -- \par Loans purchased from \par  Desjardins \par  Group                         58         --        --                --            --           --           -- \par Personal                    1,725          5      0.29                --             5            6            8 \par Business                    4,014         10      0.25                 3             7           10           20 \par Total                     $22,611    $    15                     $     3   $        12  $        16  $        28 \par As a percentage of                                                                             0.09         0.15 \par  gross loans                                      0.07%                          0.05%            %            % \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 21 - SPECIFIC COVERAGE RATIO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  As at December 31                    2013         2012          2011 \par (as a %) \par Impaired loan portfolio coverage \par  ratio                                21.8%        23.6%          29.4% \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Collective allowance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The method used by CCD to determine the collective allowance takes into account the risk parameters of the various loan portfolios, in particular through the integration of sophisticated credit risk models. These collective allowance impairment models take into account certain factors such as probabilities of default (loss frequency), loss given default (extent of losses) and gross exposures at default. These parameters, which are based on historical losses, are determined according to the category and the risk rating of each loan. The measurement of the collective allowance also depends on management's assessment of current credit quality trends with respect to business sectors, the impact of changes in its credit policies, and economic conditions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The collective allowance stood at $45.9 million as at December 31, 2013, versus $47.9 million as at December 31, 2012. An allowance related to off-balance sheet exposures of $34.0 million as at December 31, 2013, compared to $29.1 million as at the end of 2012 was recognized under "Other liabilities - Other" in the Consolidated Balance Sheets. The collective allowance reflects management's best estimate of allowances for credit losses regarding loans not yet individually identified as impaired.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Impaired loans\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is objective evidence of impairment when one of the following conditions is met: there is reason to believe that a portion of the principal or interest cannot be collected; or the interest or principal repayment is contractually 90 days past due, unless the loan is fully secured or in the process of collection; or the interest or principal is more than 180 days in arrears.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The volume of impaired loans decreased $5.3 million, from $20.6 million as at December 31, 2012 to $15.3 million at year-end 2013, due to the repayment and write-off of some of these loans. Net impaired loans-i.e. the gross amount less individual allowances for these loans-was $12.0 million as at December 31, 2013. Net impaired loans outstanding accounted for less than 0.1% of the gross loan portfolio as at December 31, 2013 and 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Provision for credit losses\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD recognized a provision for credit losses of $1.6 million for the year ended December 31, 2013. This represents a $5.8 million decrease from one year earlier, when a $7.4 million provision was recorded. This decrease was primarily due to an improvement in the quality of the business loan portfolio, as well as the decrease in the average term of this portfolio, which had a favourable effect on the amount of the collective allowance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MARKET RISK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market risk refers to the risk of changes in the fair value \par  of financial instruments resulting from fluctuations in the \par  parameters affecting this value, in particular, interest \par  rates, exchange rates, credit spreads and their volatility. \par  \par  CCD is exposed to market risk primarily through positions \par  taken in the course of its traditional financing and trading \par  activities. CCD has adopted policies that set out the principles, \par  limits and procedures to use in managing market risk. \par  \par  INTEREST RATE RISK MANAGEMENT \par  \par  CCD is exposed to interest rate risk, which represents the \par  potential impact of interest rate fluctuations on net interest \par  income and the economic value of equity. Interest rate risk \par  is the main component of market risk for CCD's traditional \par  banking activities other than trading, such as accepting \par  deposits and granting loans, as well as for its securities \par  portfolio used for long-term investment purposes and as liquidity \par  reserves. \par  \par  Sound and prudent management is applied to optimize net interest \par  income while minimizing the negative incidence of interest \par  rate movements. The established policies describe the principles, \par  limits and procedures that apply to interest rate risk management. \par  Simulations are used to measure the impact of different variables \par  on changes in net interest income and the economic value \par  of equity. \par  \par  Desjardins Group's asset and liability management committee \par  (the Asset/Liability Committee) is responsible for analyzing \par  and approving the global matching strategy on a monthly basis \par  while respecting the parameters defined in interest rate \par  risk management policies. \par  \par  The following table presents the potential impact before \par  income taxes on the non-trading portfolio of a sudden and \par  sustained 100-basis-point increase or decrease in interest \par  rates on net interest income and the economic value of equity. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 22 - INTEREST RATE SENSITIVITY (BEFORE INCOME TAXES)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par As at December 31                       2013                      2012 \par                                              Economic                  Economic \par                               Net interest   value of   Net interest   value of \par (in thousands of $)             income(1)    equity(2)    income(1)    equity(2) \par Impact of a 100-basis-point \par  increase in interest \par  rates                        $ 148         $ (2,559)        $ 2,085   $ (3,140) \par Impact of a 100-basis-point \par  decrease in interest \par  rates                        $ (1,423)     $ 2,622        $ (5,988)     $ 3,770 \par  \par  \par (1) Represents the sensitivity of net interest income for the next 12 months. \par  (2) Represents the sensitivity of the present value of assets, liabilities \par  and off-balance sheet instruments. \par  \par  \par Interest rate sensitivity is based on the earlier of the repricing or maturity \par  date of the assets, liabilities and derivative financial instruments used \par  to manage interest rate risk. The situation presented reflects the position \par  on that date only and can change significantly in subsequent years depending \par  on the preferences of members and clients and the application of policies \par  on interest rate risk management. \par  \par  Some Consolidated Balance Sheets items are considered non-interest-rate \par  sensitive instruments, such as non-performing loans, non-interest-bearing \par  deposits, non-maturity deposits with an interest rate not referenced to \par  a specific rate (such as the prime rate), and equity. As dictated in its \par  policies, CCD's management practices are based on prudent assumptions with \par  respect to the maturity profile used in its models to determine their interest \par  rate sensitivity. \par  \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RISK MANAGEMENT \par  \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk may be defined as the risk that the actual or expected \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 04-03-14 1836GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  value of assets denominated in a foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be higher or lower \par  than that of liabilities denominated in the same \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par  Overall, CCD's exposure to this risk is low because the majority of its \par  transactions are conducted in Canadian dollars. However, in certain specific \par  situations, CCD may become exposed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, particularly \par  with respect to the U.S. dollar and the euro. This exposure mainly arises \par  from its intermediation activities with members and clients, and its financing \par  and investing activities. To ensure that its exposure is properly controlled \par  and limited, CCD uses, among other things, derivative financial instruments \par  such as forward exchange contracts and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps. \par  \par  \par MANAGEMENT OF MARKET RISK RELATED TO TRADING ACTIVITIES - VALUE-AT-RISK \par  \par  The market risk of trading portfolios is managed on a daily basis under \par  a specific policy. The main tool used to measure the market risk of trading \par  portfolios is "Value at risk" (VaR), which represents an estimate of the \par  potential loss over a certain period of time at a given confidence level. \par  \par  A Monte Carlo VaR is calculated daily on the trading portfolios using a \par  99% confidence level and a holding horizon of one day. It is therefore \par  reasonable to expect a loss exceeding the VaR figure once every 100 days. \par  The calculation of VaR is based on historical data for a one-year interval. \par  \par  The following table presents the aggregate VaR of CCD's trading activities \par  by risk category as well as the diversification effect. Interest rate risk \par  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk are the two risk categories to which CCD is exposed. \par  The definition of a trading portfolio meets the various criteria defined \par  in the Basel Capital Accord. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 23 - VaR BY RISK CATEGORY (TRADING PORTFOLIO)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      As at                                As at \par                     December     For the year ended      December    For the year ended \par                     31, 2013      December 31, 2013      31, 2012     December 31, 2012 \par (in thousands \par  of $)                        Average     High     Low             Average    High     Low \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2         $ 67    $ 128    $ 548     $ 3       $ 40     $ 42   $ 114     $ 1 \par Interest \par  rate                    558      609    1,104     303        357      467     777     269 \par Diversification \par  effect (1)             (57)    (107)   N/A(2)  N/A(2)       (26)     (44)  N/A(2)  N/A(2) \par Aggregate \par  VaR                   $ 568    $ 630  $ 1,187   $ 359      $ 371     $ 65   $ 745   $ 264 \par  \par  \par (1) Represents the risk reduction related to diversification, namely the \par  difference between the sum of the VaR for the various market risk \par  categories and the aggregate VaR. \par  (2) Not applicable: The highs and lows of the various market risk categories \par  can refer to different dates. \par  \par  \par BACK TESTING \par  \par  Back testing is conducted to validate the VaR model used by comparing on \par  a daily basis the VaR with the profits or losses (P&L) on CCD's portfolios. \par  \par  CCD performs back testing daily, applying a hypothetical P&L to its trading \par  portfolios. The hypothetical P&L is calculated by determining the difference \par  in value resulting from changes in market conditions between two consecutive \par  days. The portfolio mix between these two days remains static. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following chart presents changes in VaR for trading activities as well as the profits and losses related to these activities. During the fourth quarter of 2013, the actual P&L did not exceed the VaR.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHART 3\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click on, or paste the following link into your web browser, to view the associated PDF document (please refer to Page\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of the associated PDF Document).\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STRESS TESTING \par  \par  From time to time, certain events that are considered highly unlikely may \par  occur and have a significant impact on trading portfolios. These events \par  at the tail-end of the distribution are the result of extreme situations. \par  \par  The approach used to measure the risk related to highly unlikely but plausible \par  events is applied through the use of a stress testing program (sensitivity \par  tests, historical scenarios and hypothetical scenarios) at regular intervals. \par  Stress testing results are analyzed together with the VaR calculations \par  in order to detect vulnerability to such events. The stress-testing program \par  is reviewed periodically to ensure that it is kept current. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LIQUIDITY RISK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Liquidity risk refers to CCD's capacity to raise the necessary funds (by \par  increasing liabilities or converting assets) to meet a financial obligation, \par  whether or not it appears on the Consolidated Balance Sheets. \par  \par  In its role as Treasurer to Desjardins Group, CCD is exposed to liquidity \par  risk. Liquidity risk is managed in order to ensure that Desjardins Group \par  has timely and cost-effective access to the funds needed to meet its financial \par  obligations as they become due, in both routine and crisis situations. \par  Managing this risk involves maintaining a sufficient level of liquid securities, \par  ensuring stable and diversified sources of funding, monitoring indicators \par  and adopting a contingency plan to implement in the event of a liquidity \par  crisis. \par  \par  Liquidity risk management is a key component of the overall risk management \par  strategy. Desjardins Group and CCD have established policies describing \par  the principles, limits, risk appetite and tolerance thresholds and procedures \par  that apply to liquidity risk management. \par  \par  Policies are reviewed on a regular basis to ensure that they are appropriate \par  for the operating environment and market conditions. They are also updated \par  to reflect regulatory requirements and sound liquidity risk management \par  practices. \par  \par  The implementation of Basel III will strengthen international minimum liquidity \par  requirements through the application of a liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) \par  and a net stable funding ratio (NSFR). The Basel Committee recently issued \par  a timetable and guidance for phase-in of the LCR effective 2015. The rules \par  for applying NSFR requirements are still under review and should come into \par  effect in 2018. Desjardins Group already monitors these two ratios on a \par  regular basis under its liquidity risk management policy and intends to \par  comply with the new standards once they become effective. \par  \par  \par  Desjardins Group's Treasury segment ensures stable and diversified sources \par  of institutional funding by type, source and maturity. It uses a wide range \par  of financial products and borrowing programs on various markets for its \par  financing needs. \par  \par  It should be noted that systems are in place for the issuance of covered \par  bonds and the securitization of CMHC-insured loans. Furthermore, Desjardins \par  Group is eligible for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's various \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  programs \par  and loan facilities for Emergency Lending Assistance advances. \par  \par  \par LIQUIDITY RISK MEASUREMENT AND MONITORING \par  \par  Desjardins Group determines its liquidity needs by reviewing its current \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and evaluating its future forecasts for balance sheet growth \par  and institutional funding conditions. Various analyses are used to determine \par  the actual liquidity levels of assets and the stability of liabilities \par  based on observed behaviours or contractual maturities. Maintaining reserves \par  of high-quality liquid assets is required to offset potential cash outflows \par  following a disruption in capital markets, or events that would restrict \par  its access to funding or result in a serious run on deposits. \par  \par  The minimum liquid asset levels to be maintained by the caisse network, \par  the Federation and CCD are specifically prescribed by policies. Daily management \par  of securities and the reserve level to be maintained is centralized at \par  Desjardins Group Treasury and is subject to monitoring by the Risk Management \par  Division under the supervision of Desjardins Group's Finance and Risk Management \par  Committee. Securities eligible for liquidity reserves must meet high security \par  and negotiability criteria and provide assurance of their adequacy in the \par  event of a severe liquidity crisis. The securities held are largely \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canadian \par  government\f28 \uc2 \f2  securities. \par  \par  In addition to regulatory ratios, a Desjardins-wide stress testing program \par  has been set up. This program incorporates the concepts put forward in \par  "Basel III: International Framework for Liquidity Risk Measurement, Standards \par  and Monitoring." The scenarios make it possible to measure the extent of \par  potential cash outflows in a crisis situation, to implement liquidity ratios \par  and levels to be maintained across Desjardins Group and to assess the potential \par  marginal cost of such events, depending on the type, severity and level \par  of the crisis. \par  \par  \par Liquidity risk indicators \par  \par  The purpose of monitoring liquidity indicators daily is to quickly identify \par  a lack of liquidity, whether potential or real, within Desjardins Group \par  and on capital markets. Warning levels subject to an escalation process \par  are established for each of these indicators. If one or more indicators \par  trigger a warning level, the Desjardins Group Finance and Risk Management \par  Committee is immediately alerted. This committee would also act as a crisis \par  committee should the contingency plan need to be applied. \par  \par  Desjardins Group has a liquidity contingency plan providing for, in particular, \par  an internal crisis committee vested with special decision-making powers \par  to deal with crisis situations. This plan lists the sources of liquidity \par  available in exceptional situations. In addition, it lays down the decision-making \par  and information process based on the severity level of a potential crisis. \par  \par  The aim of the plan is to allow quick and effective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order \par  to minimize disruptions caused by sudden changes in member and client behaviour \par  and potential disruptions in capital markets or economic conditions. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources of refinancing [ 04-03-14 1836GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Core financing, which includes capital and a diversified deposit portfolio, is the foundation upon which CCD's liquidity position depends. Total deposits presented on the Consolidated Balance Sheets amounted to $27.3 billion as at December 31, 2013, up $4.7 billion since December 31, 2012. This growth was mainly due to market debt, which is CCD's preferred source of refinancing. In addition, CCD diversifies its refinancing sources in order to limit its dependence on a single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . As shown in Chart 5, in addition to the Canadian dollar deposit base, 37% of CCD's refinancing was denominated in U.S. dollars and 4% in euros, funds being obtained primarily under short-term and medium-term note programs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHART 4 - DEPOSITS BY CATEGORY CHART 5 - DEPOSITS BY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 AS AT DECEMBER 31, 2013 AS AT DECEMBER 31, 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click on, or paste the following link into your web Click on, or paste the following link into your web\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 browser, to view the associated PDF document browser, to view the associated PDF document\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (please refer to Page 8 of the Associated PDF (please refer to Page 9 of the Associated PDF\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Document).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Document).\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 REFINANCING PROGRAMS AND STRATEGIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Treasurer of Desjardins Group, CCD satisfies the needs of the organization's members and clients, and implements the appropriate strategies to determine, measure and manage the related risks. A policy on the adequacy and administration of cash flows as well as a management plan for refinancing have been put in place to that end. In 2013, CCD maintained its cash flow at an adequate level to respond to Desjardins Group's needs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To secure long-term refinancing at the best cost, CCD continues to be present on the federally-guaranteed mortgage loan securitization market under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canada Mortgage\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bonds (CMB) program. In addition, to ensure stable refinancing, CCD diversifies its sources from institutional markets. It therefore regularly makes use of the financial markets when conditions are favourable and makes public and private issues of term notes on Canadian, U.S. and European markets as required.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The main programs currently used by CCD are:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table 24 - MAIN REFINANCING PROGRAMS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As at December 31, 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Refinancing program                  Maximum authorized amount \par Medium-term notes (Canadian)         $5 billion \par Covered bonds (multi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )       EUR5 billion(1) \par Short-term notes (European)          EUR1 billion \par Short-term notes (U.S.)              US$7 billion \par Medium-term notes (multi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )   EUR7 billion \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) This maximum authorized amount covers CCD's Structured Covered Bond Program and Legislative Covered Bond Program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2013, CCD also participated in new issues under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canada Mortgage\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bonds (CMB) Program. The aggregate amount of assets securitized through this program was $1.7 billion in 2013. CCD also issued $2.2 billion of medium-term notes on the Canadian market and US$500.0 million through its multi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  medium-term deposit note program during the same period. On January 29, 2014, CCD also obtained the CMHA's accreditation for its legislative covered bond program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD's medium-term notes totalled $14.8 billion as at December 31, 2013, compared to $10.8 billion as at December 31, 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit ratings on issued securities\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Maintaining competitive credit ratings is instrumental to accessing sources of wholesale funding, obtaining low funding costs and boosting Desjardins Group's credibility and recognition among institutional investors and counterparties.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rating agencies analyze Desjardins Group primarily on a combined basis, since CCD's credit ratings are backed by the Group's financial strength. The agencies recognize Desjardins Group's strong capitalization, the stability of its operating surplus earnings, its significant market share in Quebec and the quality of its assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the first quarter of 2013, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgraded the credit ratings of CCD as well as the ratings of five other Canadian financial institutions. This agency stated that this decision was essentially due to the economic situation in Canada, which showed signs for concern such as high consumer debt levels and elevated housing prices. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  also said that financial institutions are more vulnerable than in the past to downside risks weighing on the Canadian economy. Management is of the opinion that this decision has more to do with this agency's concern about Canada's economic situation than with the quality of CCD's loan portfolio or balance sheet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the following quarters of 2013, the four ratings agencies confirmed the credit ratings of the securities issued by CCD. The ratings assigned by the four agencies are with a stable outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the announcement on January 15, 2014 of an agreement under which Desjardins Group will purchase State Farm Canada's businesses, Standards & Poor's, Moodys and DBRS indicated that CCD's rating would remain unchanged.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On January 24, 2014, Fitch affirmed CCD's credit ratings, as well as those of the six major Canadian banks. The agency mentioned that these financial institutions were well positioned to withstand a moderate downturn in the real estate market and deterioration in consumer credit profiles due to their stable surplus earnings, good credit quality, strong financing and liquidity positions and sound capital ratios. On January 27, 2014 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  once again affirmed CCD's ratings, with a stable outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD thus has excellent credit ratings that are among the best of the major Canadian and international banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 25 - CREDIT RATINGS OF SECURITIES ISSUED\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                 DBRS     Standard  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2   Fitch \par                              & \par                           Poor's \par -----------  ----------  --------  -------  ----- \par Short-term   R-1 (high)    A-1       P-1     F1+ \par Medium- and      AA         A+       Aa2     AA- \par  long-term, \par  senior \par -----------  ----------  --------  -------  ----- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Contractual obligations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Contractual obligations are commitments with respect to minimum future payments and impact CCD's liquidity needs. Such contractual obligations are recognized in the Consolidated Balance Sheets or are off-balance sheet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Table 26 presents financial liabilities as well as other obligations by \par  remaining contractual term to maturity. The amounts presented include principal \par  and interest, if any. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 26 - CONTRACTUAL OBLIGATIONS BY TERM TO MATURITY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par As at December 31                                                  2013 \par (in thousands of          Payable             Less than              1 to                Over \par $)                        on demand             1 year              5 years             5 years            Total \par Liabilities \par Deposits            $ 2,006,447                $ 12,741,020          $ 13,400,194  $ 159,852          $ 28,