\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Canada's industry minister sees C$ range as 'relatively steady'\b0\par\par\uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2014\par 20:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Industry Minister says C$ movements within a steady range\par \par \uc2 * Says higher C$ good for some firms, bad for others\par \par \uc2 * Says would be "imprudent" to comment on monetary policy (Adds C$ details, quotes, finance minister's comments)\par \par \uc2 By Randall Palmer and Louise Egan\par \par \uc2 OTTAWA, Jan 10 (Reuters) - Canada's Industry Minister, James Moore, said on Friday that the Canadian dollar has been trading within a steady range, suggesting the government is not too worried about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's week-long slide to a four-year low.\par \par \uc2 "I think the range of fluctuations has been relatively steady," Moore told Reuters in a telephone interview, when asked if volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate was a concern.\par \par \uc2 "I know that this is an endless debate about the value of the dollar and what it means for firms. For some firms a higher dollar is a good thing and for others it creates challenges, and the inverse is equally true," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar hit a fresh four-year low against the U.S. dollar on Friday after Canadian data showed surprise job losses in December. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later traded at C$1.0893 to the greenback, or 91.80 U.S. cents, still weaker than Thursday's close of C$1.0852, or 92.15 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar in 2013 suffered its worst year since the financial crisis and many investors expect it to fall even further out of favor this year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  lets the exchange rate float freely and only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in extraordinary circumstances, when extreme volatility is deemed a threat to the stability of the financial system. It has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to affect the value of the Canadian dollar since 1998.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Friday, Moore, a rising star in Conservative Prime Minister \uc2 Stephen Harper\uc2 's government, answered several questions about the economy, jobs and the federal budget in a news conference in Vancouver where he announced details of an upcoming wireless spectrum auction.\par \par \uc2 These issues are usually the remit of Finance Minister \uc2 Jim Flaherty\uc2 , who issued a short statement on jobs on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Flaherty on Sunday told CTV television that Bank of Canada Governor \uc2 Stephen Poloz\uc2  had recently indicated to a meeting of provincial and federal finance ministers that "there might be some softening in the dollar." Flaherty added that "with the dollar in the 90's somewhere, it's good for manufacturing and we can still travel reasonably."\par \par \uc2 Flaherty also weighed in on monetary policy in remarks that were criticized by some economists as inappropriate in a country where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  operates largely independently of the government of the day.\par \par \uc2 Moore, when pressed for further comment on the dollar, declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 "I think it would be imprudent for me as industry minister to comment and speculate on monetary policy. The impacts of a parity dollar versus an 80-cent dollar is something about which I know there is plenty of speculation that you can write about without my comments." (Editing by Chizu Nomiyama, Jeffrey Hodgson and Paul Simao)\par \par \uc2 CANADA-ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (INTERVIEW, UPDATE 1) | CANT | ABN | D | E | RBN | FCA | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140110ea1a00142