\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT:  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  won't be  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  in housing market\b0\par\par\uc2 1,526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2013\par 19:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Jim Flaherty says at a press conference he has no current plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's housing market. That should come as some surprise as more than a few watchers consider the country to be in the middle of a housing bubble of sorts as sales and prices continue to climb after last year's mortgage-tightening changes. But with Canadian household-debt ratios on the way down, there might be less need to raise the alarm on the sector despite the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  standing pat on its key policy rate for the next year or so. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com, @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 13:39 EDT - IHS Global Insight says the Venezuelan healthcare sector is approaching a crisis because of shortages of drugs and medical supplies, and that it expects "a total systemic breakdown" if "emergency measures" aren't taken by the government. "Hospitals are lacking everything, and citizens are being requested to bring anything from plastic gloves to bed sheets or needles, and most hospitals lack disinfectants, resulting in surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  posing quite a high risk." (ezequiel.minaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:11 EDT - Investors need to focus more sharply on iron ore production costs as prices will slip in coming years when new Australia and Brazil projects hit the seaborne market, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. Outside China, CS identifies 140M metric tons a year of existing supply which would be uneconomic by 2016 at a market price of $90/metric ton, even though the economics of the 'Big 3' miners' expansion tonnage from Australia and Brazil is robust at, or even slightly below, this price. China could also be a much larger and lower-cost producer than generally thought long term, CS says. Investors really need to discover how price-elastic China's iron ore producers are, as that will determine whether market prices can find support as low as $76/ton, or whether support is nearer $119, CS says. (diana.kinch@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:54 EDT - Amid a disappointing quarter from Staples (SPLS), Janney wonders if the office-supply retailer not having any Apple (AAPL) hardware makes the company destined to be a tech also-ran. "It is simply hard to be a major technology player when you don't have AAPL products in store. There is still hope that SPLS can further develop their relationship with AAPL, but until then, we just don't think they can have a world-class technology offering. SPLS offers mobile in about 500+ stores with a goal to be in about 1,000 stores by year-end, but--once again--without Apple, the potential for success is limited." SPLS does carry AAPL accessories. Amid its F2Q miss and reduced view, SPLS is down 14%--easily the S&P 500's weakest stock--after surging nearly 40% since late April. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Libor rates might have been tainted somewhat by the manipulation scandal, but the benchmark gauge of short-term funding cost for banks has delivered some good news. The three-month dollar Libor Wednesday was set unchanged at 0.2621%, a two-year low. The rate has ticked down from around 0.31% at the end of 2012, even as the 10-year Treasury yield has soared nearly a full percentage point. Fears over a breakup of the euro zone have faded dramatically, allowing European banks easier funding access, all while the ECB mulls ways to reassure investors that low rates will stay for longer. The 3-month euro Libor rate was even lower at 0.15%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Should the FOMC minutes prove to be on the dovish side, KMJ Capital's Ken Jakubzak is ready. The head of the Chicago-based $10M boutique trading firm plans to scoop up the euro below $1.34 for a ride to $1.37 the next few weeks. In case of hawkish minutes and dollar strength, Jakubzak will sell euros on a break of $1.33. It's down 0.3% today at $1.3375. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Buyers pushing bond yields down from the 10-year's peak at 2.86%. Fears that the Fed minutes will signal QE tapering in September is sapping appetite for stocks and commodities, in turn driving some cash into haven Treasurys. If this pattern holds, any selloff in bonds on the minutes this afternoon could be cushioned if riskier assets also fall. Housing is starting to show some cracks due to higher yields: the Mortgage Bankers Association reported mortgage applications filed in the US last week dropping 5% from the week before. If the Fed raises further concerns over rising yields, bond bears could reduce anti-Treasury wagers and spur a rally in bonds. Ten-year notes now down just 1/32, yielding 2.823%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 12:38 EDT - Amid recent talk about how stock-market correlations have dissipated and things have become more of a picker's paradise, take note that since the \uc2 Dow\uc2  industrials' Aug. 2 peak, all but \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT)--thanks to its 1% gain today--have fallen since the high. And for the price-weighted index, usual suspects are making up the lion's share of the DJIA's 700-point-plus decline. \uc2 IBM\uc2  is responsible for 75 points, with \uc2 Chevron\uc2  (CVX) and United Tech (UTX) the source of nearly 60 and 50 points, respectively. Of course the trio has 3-digit stock prices, so that makes them easier on a daily-stock-change basis to inflict more damage, or gains, on the DJIA. But they've also fallen percentage-wise a bit more than the index's 4.5% drop within the past 3 weeks. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:49 EDT - With the job market such a central target for the Fed's QE efforts, investors should be keen to sift carefully through any employment-related discussion in the July minutes. Recent job gains and declines in the unemployment rate have helped fuel September QE taper talk, though officials have been clear that their job-market assessments run deeper. In the minutes, stronger expressions of concern about the recent predominance of part-time employment gains, sluggish wages or a stubbornly low participation rate could dovishly suggest some hesitancy to get hasty on tapering, and give bulls a little vigor. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 A recent survey by Edward Jones shows 63% of US adults have no idea rising yields mean lower bond prices and hence shrinking returns. The result came from 1,008 telephone and cell phone interviews between July 26 and July 29, with a margin of error of 3%. Twenty-four percent polled say they feel completely in the dark about the potential effects of rising yields. Many bond managers say it's been an educational process as yields rose the past few months. Bond funds and ETFs have suffered $100B outflows since the start of June. Traders warn a further rise in yields would drive more redemptions as the 10-year yield approaches 3%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:32 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  slashes its target on lowering Brazilian iron-ore miner MMX Mineracao (MMXM3.BR) to BRL1.90 from BRL2.80 as the future of the company founded by \uc2 Eike Batista\uc2  remains cloudy. As some or all of the company may ultimately be sold, the investment bank notes MMXM3.BR faces "high risks of further delays and lower-than-estimated shipments.". It adds that with BRL1.2B of short-term debt and BRL1B of committed capital spending the next year, the miner might not be able to wait for an equity partner before raising capital if the debt can't be refinanced. Goldman thinks MMXM3.BR could need BRL1.3-1.5B "to meet short-term liquidity needs." Shares are up 4.4% today at BRL2.37, cutting this year's swoon to 47%. (diana.kinch@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Shorter-dated Treasurys have been largely spared from this summer's selloff. Credit Bernanke who reiterated last month that even if the Fed moves to taper bond purchases, the short-term policy rate won't be raised for a long time. Investors will zero in on the minutes today to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come up with a plan to strengthen forward guidance, perhaps discussing lowering the unemployment rate threshold for hiking rates. Risk for short-term Treasurys is if data shows growth gathering speed, which would increase anxiety over the Fed raising rates sooner. In this case, short-dated yields could rise more sharply than longer-dated yields, causing the yield curve to flatten, says Richard Bryant, Treasury trader at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2 . (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-21-13 1342ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020130821e98l000ck\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
