\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Any Other Time, Treasurys Would've Rallied on Minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2013\par 20:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 July's Fed minutes sounded a clearly cautious tone: Members were worried about the economy sustaining growth in the near-term and they remained unconvinced about labor-market improvements. But the overpowering fact is that those views aren't bad enough to postpone tapering expectations. So while Treasurys would've likely rallied on these minutes if it were released under any other circumstance, the tapering trump card make its a tricky market for bond bulls to step in. Prices are coming off post-minutes lows, but are still far from the day's highs right before they were released. Ten-year notes are off 10/32 to yield 2.857%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Government of Canadas dropped sharply with US Treasurys in the immediate aftermath of the Fed's minutes, whose considered tone didn't dislodge the idea that tapering of the Fed's bond buying in September looks almost like a done deal. Move is entirely consistent with the Canadian market's stronger-than-usual tendency to follow Treasurys in recent weeks, given that the Fed is heading to a shift in policy, while the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  continues to sit tight. Ten-year Canada bond yielding 2.726% vs 2.682% late yesterday, according to bond trading platform CanDeal. (don.curren@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The FOMC minutes didn't exactly offer a ringing endorsement for an imminent rollback of the Fed's bond-buying program, but it didn't contain anything to dissuade investors from expecting a September or October tapering either. The dollar rose slightly against the euro and yen, but not taking off by any means. With less than a month to go until September's Fed meeting, the market is searching for a definitive datapoint that will provide clearer guidance on the strength of the economy--and this onus falls on the August nonfarm payrolls report to be released September 6. Dollar at Y97.84, up 0.6%. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Dollar jumps against the euro and yen after minutes from the Fed's July meeting, as investors assess that Fed members broadly support Fed Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2 's tapering blueprint. While members seem very mixed on the health of the US economy, "the takeaway is that there wasn't a greater push within the Fed to alter Bernanke's timeline for tapering," says Brian Daingerfield of RBS. Two things to remember: Bernanke's timeline doesn't necessarily mean a September taper, as he has repeatedly warned that it will depend strongly on US data. Plus, this Fed meeting occurred before the release of the July jobs report, which underwhelmed expectations. EURUSD at 1.3363, down 0.4%; USDJPY at 97.83, up 0.6%. (nicole.hong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 At this point, it doesn't matter if Fed officials are a bit more optimistic or pessimistic about the US economy. Tapering QE next month looks like a growing reality, unless the Fed says otherwise outright. Some hopes central bankers might delay tapering had held Treasurys up, but prices are now caving after the July FOMC minutes. Only "some" on the board were worried that higher rates would hold back the economy, while "several" believed it'd have little effect. That's giving Treasurys the green light to fall further. Ten-year notes are now down 13/32, yielding 2.866%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 One factor in the upcoming FOMC minutes that could boost Treasury prices lies in any hint of a strengthened forward guidance on policy rates, say Citi rates strategists. The primary dealer argues that tapering in September is priced in by markets, but a surprise may come in the discussion of a lower jobless-rate threshold, meaning the labor market would have to show more improvement before the Fed is willing to increase rates. This case favors the front-end of the curve, but with the very front end yielding so little, "we see the maximum benefit at the five-year part of the curve," Citi says. Five-year notes last down 2/32 to yield 1.56%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Jim Flaherty says at a press conference he has no current plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's housing market. That should come as some surprise as more than a few watchers consider the country to be in the middle of a housing bubble of sorts as sales and prices continue to climb after last year's mortgage-tightening changes. But with Canadian household-debt ratios on the way down, there might be less need to raise the alarm on the sector despite the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  standing pat on its key policy rate for the next year or so. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com, @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 Libor rates might have been tainted somewhat by the manipulation scandal, but the benchmark gauge of short-term funding cost for banks has delivered some good news. The three-month dollar Libor Wednesday was set unchanged at 0.2621%, a two-year low. The rate has ticked down from around 0.31% at the end of 2012, even as the 10-year Treasury yield has soared nearly a full percentage point. Fears over a breakup of the euro zone have faded dramatically, allowing European banks easier funding access, all while the ECB mulls ways to reassure investors that low rates will stay for longer. The 3-month euro Libor rate was even lower at 0.15%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Should the FOMC minutes prove to be on the dovish side, KMJ Capital's Ken Jakubzak is ready. The head of the Chicago-based $10M boutique trading firm plans to scoop up the euro below $1.34 for a ride to $1.37 the next few weeks. In case of hawkish minutes and dollar strength, Jakubzak will sell euros on a break of $1.33. It's down 0.3% today at $1.3375. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Buyers pushing bond yields down from the 10-year's peak at 2.86%. Fears that the Fed minutes will signal QE tapering in September is sapping appetite for stocks and commodities, in turn driving some cash into haven Treasurys. If this pattern holds, any selloff in bonds on the minutes this afternoon could be cushioned if riskier assets also fall. Housing is starting to show some cracks due to higher yields: the Mortgage Bankers Association reported mortgage applications filed in the US last week dropping 5% from the week before. If the Fed raises further concerns over rising yields, bond bears could reduce anti-Treasury wagers and spur a rally in bonds. Ten-year notes now down just 1/32, yielding 2.823%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 A recent survey by Edward Jones shows 63% of US adults have no idea rising yields mean lower bond prices and hence shrinking returns. The result came from 1,008 telephone and cell phone interviews between July 26 and July 29, with a margin of error of 3%. Twenty-four percent polled say they feel completely in the dark about the potential effects of rising yields. Many bond managers say it's been an educational process as yields rose the past few months. Bond funds and ETFs have suffered $100B outflows since the start of June. Traders warn a further rise in yields would drive more redemptions as the 10-year yield approaches 3%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Shorter-dated Treasurys have been largely spared from this summer's selloff. Credit Bernanke who reiterated last month that even if the Fed moves to taper bond purchases, the short-term policy rate won't be raised for a long time. Investors will zero in on the minutes today to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come up with a plan to strengthen forward guidance, perhaps discussing lowering the unemployment rate threshold for hiking rates. Risk for short-term Treasurys is if data shows growth gathering speed, which would increase anxiety over the Fed raising rates sooner. In this case, short-dated yields could rise more sharply than longer-dated yields, causing the yield curve to flatten, says Richard Bryant, Treasury trader at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2 . (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj) [ 21-08-13 1846GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130821e98l000l9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
