\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ Blog:It's \uc2 Stephen Poloz\uc2 's First Day on the Job\b0\par\par\uc2 485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2013\par 15:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Canada Real Time blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/canadarealtime/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/canadarealtime/ }}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Nirmala Menon\par \par \uc2 Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a new boss. \uc2 Stephen Poloz\uc2  became the ninth governor of the Bank of Canada Monday, returning to head an institution that he quit in the early 1990s as an ambitious economist who wanted to pursue a career in the private sector.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Poloz, 57 years old, took the reins from Mark Carney, who left to head the Bank of England. All eyes are now on Mr. Poloz to see what, if anything, he will do differently from his famous predecessor. Some observers suggest his previous job--until last week, he was CEO of Export Development Canada, the government-owned export financing agency--may make him more sympathetic to exporters, who have been grappling with the strong Canadian dollar in recent years.\par \par \uc2 No one expects Mr. Poloz to depart from the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets except in exceptional circumstances. But some economists suggest he might drop the mild rate-tightening bias the Bank has had for more than a year, a move that would have the impact of weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even more than it already has in recent sessions.\par \par \uc2 Others, however, argue there's no reason to drop the bias, with data last week showing Canada's first-quarter economic growth was the strongest in six quarters, driven by the best gain in exports since the final quarter of 2011. This is balanced by weak inflation, with consumer prices in April growing at the slowest annual pace since October 2009, hence the argument for maintaining the status quo on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Poloz won't have much time to ease into his role. Later this week, he will appear before the House of Commons finance committee for a two-hour grilling by Canadian lawmakers, something he will have to do at least twice a year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Poloz is an unknown quality in financial markets, so his remarks will be carefully parsed to gauge his policy leanings. Every word he says--even the way he says them--may be interpreted as reinforcing or rebutting prevailing views on the direction of Canadian monetary policy, according to Desjardins Capital Markets economic strategist Jimmy Jean, who pointed out that even the fact that Mr. Poloz is coming from an export-finance agency has prompted some observers to be dovish.\par \par \uc2 "Just blinking the wrong way could be interpreted in all sorts of ways," Mr. Jean told Canada Real Time. "The market doesn't really know him, it's a learning curve for everybody." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 03, 2013 09:41 ET (13:41 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130603e96300329\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
