38 \par  Other assets                     3,422,217           463,825                14                    400,297 \par --------------------------- \par  Total credit risk             $ 35,005,466      $ 10,421,331                30   %            $ 8,930,131 \par ---------------------------  --------------  ----------------  ----------------  -------  ---------------- \par  Market risk                                        $ 503,200                                    $ 610,100 \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk(2)                                  532,459                                      541,208 \par ---------------------------  --------------                    ---------------- \par  Total risk-weighted \par   assets                                         $ 11,456,990                                 $ 10,081,439 \par ---------------------------  --------------  ----------------  ----------------  -------  ---------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Net exposure, after credit risk mitigation (net of specific allowances under the Standardized Approach but not under the Internal Ratings-Based approach, in accordance with the AMF Guideline).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) The Basic Indicator Approach was used to assess \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 3.3\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ANALYSIS OF CASH FLOWS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This section presents an analysis of the Consolidated Statements of Cash Flows included in the Consolidated Financial Statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because of the nature of CCD's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , most of the items that generate income and expenses are liquidities. As a result, normal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trigger significant fluctuations in liquidity affecting numerous items, such as loans, deposits and securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2012, cash and cash equivalents increased by $247.5 million compared to a decrease of $273.5 million in 2011, and amounted to $454.7 million as at December 31, 2012 compared to $207.2 as at December 31, 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating activities generated liquidities of $165.2 million in 2012. The liquidity needs resulting from the growth in CCD's various loan portfolios and the decrease in amounts payable to brokers and dealers were met through issuances of notes. In 2011, these activities had generated liquidities of $192.7 million, as liquidities from issuances of deposit notes had more than offset the increase in loans and securities purchased under reverse repurchase agreements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financing activities required liquidities of $153.9 million in 2012, as CCD paid the remuneration on capital stock for the previous year. In 2011, an issuance of capital shares and the payment of remuneration on capital stock had generated net liquidities of $113.9 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lastly, cash flows from investing activities totalled $236.2 million in 2012, as a result of the decrease in available-for-sale securities. In 2011, these activities required liquidities of $580.2 million, as available-for-sale securities increased during the year, in particular following the $300.0 million capital injection completed in 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 3.4\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OFF-BALANCE SHEET ARRANGEMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the normal course of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , CCD enters into various off-balance sheet arrangements, including credit instruments, contractual commitments, financial assets held as collateral and special purpose entities, including securitization and assets under management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CREDIT INSTRUMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In order to meet its members' and clients' financing needs, CCD makes credit instruments available to them. Credit instruments include guarantees and standby letters of credit and credit commitments representing authorized amounts that have not been used by members and clients. The risks associated with these credit instruments are managed according to the same strict rules as those applied to Consolidated Balance Sheet items. In management's opinion, no unusual risk result from these off-balance sheet items.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These instruments expose CCD to credit and liquidity risks. Management of these risks is described in section 4.0 of this MD&A. Table 12 shows the contractual amounts of credit instruments by remaining maturities. Since several of these credit instruments will mature or will be terminated without any cash outflow, the contractual amounts of these commitments do not represent future liquidity needs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Note 20, "Commitments, guarantees and contingent liabilities", to CCD's Consolidated Financial Statements provides further information on these credit instruments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 12 - CREDIT INSTRUMENTS BY MATURITIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  As at December 31                                2012                         Total \par                             ----------------------------------------------- \par  (in millions of              One year     1 to        Over        Total \par   dollars)                     or less    5 years     5 years       2012       2011 \par --------------------------  ----------  ---------  ----------  ------------  -------- \par  Guarantees and standby                                     $ \par   letters of credit              $ 233      $ 141           1         $ 375     $ 481 \par  Credit commitments             10,697      6,172          51        16,920    16,770 \par                                                             $ \par  Total credit instruments     $ 10,930    $ 6,313          52      $ 17,295   $17,251 \par --------------------------  ----------  ---------  ----------  ------------  -------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CONTRACTUAL COMMITMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the normal course of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , CCD, like other major Canadian financial institutions, pledges part of its liquid assets in order to participate in the clearing and payments systems. In addition, CCD has contractual commitments under which it must make future payments on leases. Note 21, "Off-balance Sheet Arrangements", to CCD's Consolidated Financial Statements provides information on these contractual commitments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINANCIAL ASSETS HELD AS COLLATERAL\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD receives financial assets as collateral as a result of transactions involving securities purchased under reverse repurchase agreements and transactions on derivatives. Such transactions are carried out under normal conditions for these types of transactions. Note 20, "Commitments, guarantees and contingent liabilities", to CCD's Consolidated Financial Statements provides additional information on financial assets received as collateral.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SPECIAL PURPOSE ENTITIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the normal course of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , CCD enters into various financial transactions with special purpose entities (SPEs) to diversify its sources of financing and manage its capital. These SPEs are usually created for a unique and distinct purpose, and they often have a limited life. They are used to legally isolate the financial assets they hold from the transferring organization. SPEs are not operating entities and generally have no employees. Under IFRS, they may be included on the Consolidated Balance Sheets if, in substance, the relationship between this entity and CCD indicates that control exists. Detailed information concerning significant exposure to SPEs is provided below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECURITIZATION\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD participates in the Mortgage-Backed Securities Program under the National Housing Act to manage its liquidities and capital. Transactions carried out under this program require using an SPE, the Canada Housing Trust, set up by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canada Mortgage and Housing Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CMHC) under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canada Mortgage\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bonds (CMB) Program. Note 8, "Securitization and other transferred financial assets", to the Consolidated Financial Statements provides more information on the financial assets transferred by CCD through securitization transactions. [ 27-02-13 1829GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To carry out securitization transactions, CCD groups CMHC-guaranteed residential mortgages (previously acquired from member caisses of Desjardins Group) under mortgage-backed securities (NHA MBSs) and transfers them to the SPE in exchange for monetary consideration. The SPE then finances these purchases by issuing CMBs to investors. Furthermore, under this program, CCD acquires interests in securitized mortgage loans from Desjardins Group member caisses. The loans and interests acquired through these transactions do not meet the recognition criteria since member caisses retain substantially all the risks and rewards related to these securitized loans and interests. In addition, CCD treats these transfers as collateralized financing arrangements and recognizes a liability. This liability represents the consideration received from CMHC with respect to the loans and interests in mortgage loan securitization that do not meet the derecognition criteria.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As at December 31, 2012, outstanding \f28 \uc2 \f2 NHS\f28 \uc2 \f2  MBSs issued by CCD and sold to the Canada Housing Trust totalled $5.0 billion. However, some securitization transactions entered into before January 1, 2010 resulted in derecognition, as CCD elected to apply the derecognition requirements. At the time of transfer, these transactions were therefore recognized as sales, and CCD retains certain interests in excess interest margins, which are retained interests, and assumes responsibility for servicing the transferred mortgage loans. As at December 31, 2012, the aggregate of the original assets transferred and derecognized was $0.6 billion, compared to $1.8 billion at the end of 2011. On the same date, assets representing retained interests that CCD continues to recognize with respect to these transactions amounted to $7.5 million ($28.3 million as at December 31, 2011).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ASSETS UNDER MANAGEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD performs liquidity management on behalf of third parties. These assets under management are not the property of CCD and therefore are not reflected on the Consolidated Balance Sheets. Management fees are received in exchange for the liquidity management services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4.0 RISK MANAGEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 4.1\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK MANAGEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  The shaded boxes of this section present information on credit, market \par   and liquidity risk in accordance with IFRS 7, "Financial Instruments: \par   Disclosures". In addition, this section contains an analysis of how \par   CCD assesses its risks and a description of its risk management objectives, \par   policies and methods. IFRS 7 provides that risk disclosures may be included \par   in the MD&A. Consequently, the shaded boxes are an integral part of \par   the Consolidated Financial Statements, as explained in Note 22, "Financial \par   instrument risk management", to the Consolidated Financial Statements. \par ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD is exposed to various types of risks in the normal course of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including credit risk, counterparty and issuer risk, market risk, liquidity risk, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, strategic risk and reputation risk. Strict and effective management of these risks is a priority for CCD, its purpose being to support its major orientations, including those regarding its financial stability as well as its sustained and profitable growth, while complying with Basel requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 INTEGRATED RISK MANAGEMENT FRAMEWORK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD's objective in risk management is to optimize the risk-return trade-off, within tolerance limits set, by applying integrated risk management and control strategies, policies and procedures throughout the organization's activities. It also aims to provide, through the Integrated Risk Management Framework, a prudent and appropriate management framework that complies with accepted accountability and independence principles.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As integral parts of this management framework, risk appetite and tolerance determine the risk type and level that CCD is prepared to assume to achieve its business and strategic objectives. They provide a basis for integrated risk management by promoting a better understanding of risks and its impact on the risk profile. This framework provides for a system of risk indicators that are monitored on a regular basis to ensure that CCD's risk profile matches the degree of risk appetite and tolerance desired by senior management and the Board of Directors in view of the mission, vision and values of Desjardins Group and CCD. The Board of Directors is responsible for approving the risk appetite and tolerance framework, which must reflect Desjardins Group's financial and strategic objectives.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Integrated Risk Management Framework also includes the overall \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  infrastructure and the risk management governance structure, which are supported by all the explicit and implicit rules, values, and ways of thinking and acting prevailing at Desjardins Group and CCD. This framework promotes exchanges between Desjardins Group's risk management function, business segments and regulated entities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To promote sound risk management and enhance risk management capabilities, risk management training sessions are held on a regular basis. The organization intends, through a continuing professional development plan, to continue updating the knowledge of the members of the participating bodies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK MANAGEMENT GUIDELINES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Integrated Risk Management Framework is based on risk management guidelines that provide for the following, among other things:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      The accountability of CCD with regard to the risks inherent to its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Application at every level of the organization in order to obtain a comprehensive vision of risk exposure;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The existence of a process to determine the appropriate capital level based on the risks assumed;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Taking risk management into account when formulating strategic plans and business strategies and in the resulting decisions;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Thorough risk assessment when launching a new product or introducing projects with a strong financial impact.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK MANAGEMENT GOVERNANCE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Integrated Risk Management Framework is based on a solid risk governance structure that reflects CCD's organizational reality.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD's Board of Directors is responsible for guiding, planning, coordinating and monitoring all CCD's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and in such capacity, it participates actively in the oversight of the major risks to which CCD is exposed. The Board of Directors is in particular responsible for adopting the overall orientations and strategies proposed by senior management as well as risk management policies aimed at ensuring sound and prudent management of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . To discharge its specific risk management responsibilities, the Board is supported by the Risk Management Commission, the Audit and Inspection Commission and the Board of Ethics and Professional Conduct. Additional information about these bodies is found in the Cooperative Governance section of the Annual Report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD's Management Committee must, inter alia, make recommendations to the Board of Directors concerning risk management policies and strategies and ensure that they are implemented efficiently and effectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Independent units complete CCD's risk management governance infrastructure. The Risk Management Executive Division is a strategic function whose primary role is to act as a partner in Desjardins Group's development in identifying, measuring and managing risks while ensuring the longevity of Desjardins Group regulated entities, including CCD. It is up to the risk management function to recommend and establish risk management policies and to set up the appropriate infrastructure, processes and practices to target all major risks. Monitoring and control of the various risks is a shared responsibility that is assumed, among others, by the business sectors and teams responsible for regulatory compliance and financial governance. They complement the work of those responsible for risk management to ensure that service offer is adapted to the growing regulatory requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Desjardins Group Monitoring Office (DGMO) is an independent and objective organization assurance and advisory function that assists Desjardins Group's and CCD's management personnel in carrying out their governance responsibilities. In addition, it oversees and advises management with respect to its responsibility to manage in a sound and prudent manner. In so doing, it contributes to improving Desjardins Group's and CCD's overall performance and maintaining members', the public's and regulatory bodies' confidence in Desjardins Group and CCD. The DGMO includes the internal audit activities of Desjardins Group's subsidiaries and components, including CCD, as well as activities related to the audit and inspection of the caisse network.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK MEASUREMENT AND DISCLOSURE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK MEASUREMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD uses quantitative as well as qualitative techniques to determine its risk exposure. It ensures that an appropriate selection of measurement tools and mitigation techniques are designed and maintained in order to support business development.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk quantification is performed both in the current economic context as well as in hypothetical situations, simulating crises integrated across the entire organization. Sensitivity tests and crisis scenarios are used as additional risk analysis tools to measure the potential impact of exceptional but plausible events on profitability and capital levels. Institution-wide crisis scenarios are developed on the basis of the anticipated economic outlook under distress conditions. The results of these analyses help detect potential vulnerabilities to risk factors of the various \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK DISCLOSURE [ 27-02-13 1829GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk reports on all significant risks are prepared for the Management Committee, the Risk Management Commission and CCD's Board of Directors. These reports provide risk indicators and information about capital, particularly capital adequacy in relation to CCD's risk profile. Constantly being updated, these reports present the latest risk management developments to ensure that decision-making bodies receive timely information on major risks that is both practical and forward-looking.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BASEL II CAPITAL ACCORD\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Basel II is an international capital adequacy tool designed to align regulatory capital requirements more closely with risk exposure and to further the continuous development of the risk assessment capabilities of financial institutions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Basel II framework essentially rests on three pillars: the first pillar sets out the requirements for risk-weighted regulatory capital; the second pillar deals with the supervisory review process; and the third pillar stipulates financial disclosure requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In accordance with the guideline on adequacy of capital base standards adapted to reflect the Basel II provisions, CCD is currently using the Standardized Approach for exposure to credit risk and market risk and the Basic Indicator Approach for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk. This provision is also used to calculate capital ratios.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Again this year, numerous efforts were made to enhance the implementation of sound risk management practices and to align regulatory capital requirements more closely with risk exposures. Desjardins Group and CCD are continuing to invest in improving their tools and systems and aligning them with industry best practices for the main types of risks. In recent years, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank for International Settlements\f28 \uc2 \f2  has issued new requirements (Basel III) for the global regulation of capital standards. These new rules, which regulators will integrate into their guidelines effective January 1, 2013, will increase not only capital requirements but also risk management requirements. In addition to the changes made to the level and definition of eligible capital and the measurement of risk-weighted assets, Basel III has, under Pillar 2, implemented new liquidity requirements and raised expectations concerning many management practices. The disclosure standards, which are associated with Pillar 3, have also been enhanced. CCD continues its development by integrating these new regulatory requirements into its risk management framework.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CREDIT RISK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Credit risk is the risk of losses resulting from a borrower's or a counterparty's \par   failure to honour its contractual obligations, whether or not these \par   obligations appear on the balance sheet. \par  \par   CCD is exposed to credit risk through its direct loans to businesses \par   and government (excluding the Desjardins network entities, these loans \par   accounted for close to 20% of assets on the Consolidated Balance Sheets \par   as at December 31, 2012 and 2011), but also through various other commitments \par   including letters of credit, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lines and transactions \par   involving derivative financial instruments and securities. \par ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  CREDIT RISK MANAGEMENT \par  \par   CCD is accountable for its performance and it therefore has some latitude \par   regarding the framework it uses and the approval given, and is also \par   equipped with the corresponding management and monitoring tools and \par   structures. To assist CCD in this area, Desjardins Group has set up \par   centralized structures and procedures to ensure that this risk management \par   framework allows effective management that is also sound and prudent. \par  \par   Desjardins Group set up a Risk Management Executive Division with two \par   divisions that manage credit risk for Desjardins Group as a whole and \par   for CCD. These divisions share responsibilities based on the major activities: \par   credit approval, quantification, monitoring and reporting. \par  \par   CREDIT RISK FRAMEWORK \par   The credit risk framework is comprised of policies and standards that \par   govern the credit risk management elements for CCD, such as the responsibilities \par   and powers of the parties involved, the limits imposed by our risk tolerance, \par   the rules governing credit granting and file administration and, lastly, \par   the rules for disclosing CCD's exposure to credit risks. \par  \par   Together, these monitoring activities, policies and practices determine \par   conduct with respect to risk management and control. \par ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  CREDIT GRANTING \par  \par   This responsibility is assumed by the various units grouped together \par   according to their respective client base. The qualifications of professionals, \par   their approval responsibilities and the depth of the analysis required \par   depend on the product's features as well as the complexity and extent \par   of the risk associated with the transactions. \par --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  BUSINESS LOANS \par  \par   Credit is granted to businesses based on an analysis of the various \par   parameters of each file, where each borrower is assigned a risk rating. \par   These ratings are assigned individually following a detailed examination \par   of the financial, market, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and management characteristics \par   of the business. \par  \par   The characteristics of each borrower are analyzed using models based \par   on internal and external historical data, taking into account the specific \par   features of the borrower's economic sector and the performance of comparable \par   businesses. These analyses are performed using systems that can make \par   quantitative comparisons and are supplemented by the professional judgment \par   of the personnel handling the file. \par  \par   The scoring system has 19 ratings, broken down into 12 levels, each \par   of which represents a default probability level. \par ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table provides a comparison of internal ratings and ratings assigned by external agencies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 13 - RATING ACCORDING TO RISK LEVEL\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   Ratings       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2           S&P              Description \par -----------  -------------  -------------  ------------------------ \par    1 to 2      Aaa to Aa3     AAA to AA- \par                                            ------------------------ \par     2.5         A1 to A3       A+ to A- \par  3 \u224\'00\'E0 4   Baa1 to Baa3   BBB+ to BBB-        High quality \par -----------  -------------  -------------  ------------------------ \par  4.5 to 5.5    Ba1 to Ba3     BB+ to BB- \par                                            ------------------------ \par   6 to 7.5     B1 to Caa1     B+ to CCC+ \par   8 and 9      Caa2 to C      CCC to C-          Lower quality \par -----------  -------------  -------------  ------------------------ \par                                             Impaired loans or loans \par   10 to 12         D              D                in default \par -----------  -------------  -------------  ------------------------ \par  \par  \par  The use of internal ratings and estimates has been expanded into other \par   risk management and governance activities such as establishing analysis \par   requirements and file authorization levels, the different types of follow-up \par   and the disclosure of portfolio risk quality. \par ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 14 - LOAN PORTFOLIO'S EXPOSURE TO CREDIT RISK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  As at December 31                                       2012 \par                              ----------------------------------------------------------- \par                                                                Impaired \par                                  Highest                         loans \par  (in thousands of dollars)       quality      Poor quality    or defaults      Total \par ---------------------------  --------------  -------------  -------------  ------------- \par  \par  Public and parapublic \par   sectors                       $ 1,065,328           $ --           $ --    $ 1,065,328 \par  Members \par   Federation                     10,802,676             --             --     10,802,676 \par   Other                             184,978             --             --        184,978 \par  Other entities included \par   in the group perimeter \par   of Desjardins Group             1,417,168             --             --      1,417,168 \par  Loans purchased from \par   Desjardins Group                   88,863             --             --         88,863 \par  Personal                         1,287,199        103,013          5,751      1,395,963 \par  Business                         1,588,335      1,739,776         14,868      3,342,979 \par                              --------------  -------------  ------------- \par                                $ 16,434,547    $ 1,842,789       $ 20,619    $18,297,955 \par ---------------------------  --------------  -------------  -------------  ------------- \par  \par                                                          2011 \par                              ----------------------------------------------------------- \par                                                                Impaired \par                                  Highest                         loans \par                                  quality      Poor quality    or defaults      Total \par ---------------------------  --------------  -------------  -------------  ------------- \par  \par  Day, call and short-term \par   loans to investment \par   dealers and brokers              $ 91,000           $ --           $ --       $ 91,000 \par  Public and parapublic \par   sectors                         1,904,756             --             --      1,904,756 \par  Members \par   Federation                      9,678,649             --             --      9,678,649 \par   Other                             230,195             --             --        230,195 \par  Other entities included \par   in the group perimeter \par   of Desjardins Group             1,886,748             --             --      1,886,748 \par  Loans purchased from \par   Desjardins Group                  137,636             --             --        137,636 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 27-02-13 1829GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Personal                           997,291         95,806          7,728      1,100,825 \par  Business                         1,275,826      1,511,135         30,926      2,817,887 \par                                $ 16,202,101    $ 1,606,941       $ 38,654    $17,847,696 \par ---------------------------  --------------  -------------  -------------  ------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHART 1\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (REFER TO DOCUMENT << MDA CCD - CHART 1 -T4-2012 >>)\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/8419Y_-2013-2-27.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/8419Y_-2013-2-27.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Counterparty and issuer risk \par  \par   Counterparty and issuer risk is a credit risk to which CCD is exposed \par   relative to different types of securities, derivative financial instruments \par   and securities lending transactions. \par  \par   Desjardins Group's Risk Management Executive Division sets the maximum \par   exposure for each counterparty and issuer based on quantitative and \par   qualitative criteria. The amounts are then allocated to different components \par   based on their needs. \par  \par   To properly managing its risk, CCD assigns a credit rating to each counterparty \par   and issuer, based on four external credit assessment institutions (DBRS, \par   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , S&P and Fitch). This rating is used to establish exposure limits \par   and to calculate capital requirements using the Standardized Approach. \par   These four credit assessment institutions meet the eligibility criteria \par   of the Basel Accord and are authorized by the AMF and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Office of \par   the Superintendent of Financial Institutions\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par  \par   A large proportion of CCD's exposure is to different levels of government \par   in Canada, Quebec public or parapublic entities and major Canadian banks. \par   Their credit ratings are usually A- or higher. Furthermore, CCD is not \par   directly exposed to the sovereign debt of the European countries most \par   affected by the recent financial upheaval, more specifically Greece, \par   Portugal, Italy, Ireland and Spain. Its exposure to U.S. and European \par   financial institutions is marginal. \par  \par   In its derivative financial instrument and securities lending transactions, \par   which include repurchase and reverse repurchase agreements as well as \par   securities borrowing and lending, CCD is exposed to counterparty credit \par   risk. \par  \par   CCD uses derivative financial instruments primarily for asset-liability \par   management purposes. Derivative financial instruments are contracts \par   whose value is mainly based on an underlying asset, such as interest \par   rates or exchange rates. The vast majority of derivative financial instruments \par   are traded over-the-counter between CCD and its counterparties, and \par   include forward exchange contracts, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps, interest rate swaps, \par   credit default swaps, total return swaps, forward rate agreements, and \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , interest rate and stock index options. The other transactions \par   are carried out as part of regulated trades and mainly consist of futures. \par  \par   The credit risk associated with derivative financial instruments refers \par   to the risk that a counterparty will fail to honour its contractual \par   obligations toward CCD at a time when the fair value of the instrument \par   is positive for CCD. The credit risk associated with a derivative financial \par   instrument normally corresponds to a small fraction of the notional \par   amount. The replacement cost and the credit risk equivalent are two \par   measures used to quantify this risk. The replacement cost refers to \par   the current replacement cost of all contracts with a positive fair value. \par   The credit risk equivalent is equal to the sum of this replacement cost \par   and the future credit exposure. \par  \par   CCD limits the credit risk associated with derivative financial instruments \par   by doing business with counterparties that have high credit ratings. \par   Note 13, "Derivative financial instruments and hedging activities", \par   to the Consolidated Financial Statements presents derivative financial \par   instruments by credit risk rating and type of counterparty. Based on \par   replacement cost, this note shows that substantially all the counterparties \par   have credit ratings ranging from AAA to A. Furthermore, by purchasing \par   hedges through credit derivatives, such as credit default swaps and \par   total return swaps, CCD can transfer credit risk to a counterparty or \par   hedge itself against various types of risk. \par  \par   CCD also limits credit risk with certain counterparties by entering \par   into \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Swaps and Derivatives Association\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ISDA) master agreements \par   that define the terms and conditions for the transactions. These agreements \par   are legal contracts binding the counterparties. The majority of agreements \par   entered into by CCD provide for the use of netting to determine the \par   net amount of exposure in the event of default. In addition, a Credit \par   Support Annex can be added to the master agreement in order to request \par   that the counterparties pay or obtain guarantees on the current market \par   value of the positions when this value exceeds a certain threshold. \par   Taking into account master netting agreements, the risk-weighted balance \par   for all CCD's derivative financial instruments as at December 31, 2012 \par   was $378.0 million ($356.3 million as at December 31, 2011). As at December \par   31, 2012, the amount of collateral that CCD would have to provide in \par   the event of a downgrade was marginal because the replacement cost was \par   positive for the majority of the contracts. \par  \par   Securities lending transactions are governed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Investment Industry \par   Regulatory Organization of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  participation agreements. To mitigate \par   credit risk, CCD also uses netting agreements with its counterparties \par   and requires a percentage of collateralization (a pledge) on these transactions. \par  \par   CCD accepts from its counterparties, and also gives them, financial \par   collateral that complies with the eligibility criteria set out as part \par   of its policies. These criteria promote a quick realization, if necessary, \par   of collateral in the event of default. The types of collateral received \par   and given by CCD are mainly cash and government securities. \par  \par   Additional information about credit risk is presented in Note 20, "Commitments, \par   guarantees and contingent liabilities", to the Consolidated Financial \par   Statements. \par ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 15 - LOANS, LETTERS OF GU