par  As a percentage of gross                                                                    0.09 \par   loans                                                 0.11%                                   %            0.15% \par -------------------------------  -------  ---  -------  ----      ----  --------      -----------  ----  --------- \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 21 - SPECIFIC COVERAGE RATIO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  (as a percentage)                         2012          2011 \par ------------------------------------  -------------  ------------ \par  Impaired loan portfolio coverage \par   ratio(1)                              23.6   %       29.4   % \par ------------------------------------  ------  -----  ------  ---- \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) The specific coverage ratio is equal to total individual allowances divided by total gross impaired loans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COLLECTIVE ALLOWANCE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The method used by CCD to determine the collective allowance takes into account the risk parameters of the various loan portfolios, in particular through the integration of sophisticated credit risk models. These collective allowance impairment models take into account certain factors such as probabilities of default (loss frequency), loss given default (extent of losses) and gross exposures at default. These parameters are based on historical losses and are determined according to the category and the risk rating of each loan. The measurement of the collective allowance also depends on management's assessment of current credit quality trends with respect to business sectors, the impact of changes in its credit policies, and economic conditions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The collective allowance stood at $47.9 million as at December 31, 2012, versus $47.1 million as at December 31, 2011. An allowance related to off-balance sheet exposures of $29.1 million as at December 31, 2012, compared to $28.7 million as at the end of 2011 was recognized under "Other liabilities - Other" in the Consolidated Balance Sheets. The collective allowance reflects management's best estimate of allowances for credit losses regarding loans not yet individually identified as impaired.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IMPAIRED LOANS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is objective evidence of impairment when one of the following conditions is met: there is reason to believe that a portion of the principal or interest cannot be collected; or the interest or principal repayment is contractually 90 days or more past due, unless the loan is fully secured or in the process of collection; or the interest or principal is more than 180 days in arrears.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The volume of impaired loans decreased $18.0 million, from $38.6 million as at December 31, 2011 to $20.6 million at year-end 2012, due to the repayment and write-off of some of these loans. Net impaired loans-i.e. the gross amount less individual allowances for these loans-was $15.8 million as at December 31, 2012. Net impaired loans outstanding accounted for less than 0.1% of the gross loan portfolio as at December 31, 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PROVISION FOR CREDIT LOSSES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD recognized a $7.4 million provision for credit losses for the year ended December 31, 2012, an increase of $4.6 million from the $2.8 million provision recognized in 2011. This increase was primarily due to growth in outstandings for the Business and Institutional Services loan portfolio as well as an individual provision arising from the settlement related to a credit file considered impaired. It should nevertheless be mentioned that the increase was partially offset by a favourable change in the collective allowance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  MARKET RISK \par  \par   Market risk refers to the risk of changes in the fair value of financial \par   instruments resulting from fluctuations in the parameters affecting \par   this value; in particular, interest rates, exchange rates, credit spreads \par   and their volatility. \par  \par   CCD is exposed to market risk primarily through positions taken in \par   the course of its traditional financing and trading activities. CCD \par   has adopted policies that set out the principles, limits, and procedures \par   to use in managing market risk. \par  \par   INTEREST RATE RISK MANAGEMENT \par  \par   CCD is exposed to interest rate fluctuations, which may potentially \par   impact net interest income and the economic value of equity. Interest \par   rate risk is the main component of market risk for CCD's traditional \par   banking activities other than trading, such as accepting deposits and \par   granting loans, as well as for the securities portfolio used for long-term \par   investment purposes and as liquidity reserves. \par  \par   Sound and prudent management is applied to optimize net interest income \par   while minimizing the negative impact of interest rate movements. The \par   policies established describe the principles, limits and procedures \par   that apply to interest rate risk management. Simulations are used to \par   measure the impact of different variables on changes in net interest \par   income and the economic value of equity. \par  \par   The Desjardins Group Asset/Liability Committee (the Asset/Liability \par   Committee) is responsible for analyzing and approving the overall matching \par   strategy on a monthly basis while respecting the parameters defined \par   in interest rate risk management policies. \par  \par   The following table presents the potential impact before income taxes \par   on the non-trading portfolio of a sudden and sustained 100-basis-point \par   increase or decrease in interest rates on net interest income and the \par   economic value of equity. \par ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 22 - INTEREST RATE SENSITIVITY (BEFORE INCOME TAXES)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  As at December 31                         2012                         2011 \par                                ---------------------------  --------------------------- \par                                    Net         Economic         Net         Economic \par                                  interest        value        interest        value \par  (in thousands of dollars)       income(1)    of equity(2)    income(1)    of equity(2) \par -----------------------------  -----------  --------------  -----------  -------------- \par  Impact of a 100-basis-point \par   increase in interest rates       $ 2,085       $ (3,140)        $ 815       $ (4,322) \par  Impact of a 100-basis-point \par   decrease in interest rates     $ (5,988)         $ 3,770    $ (2,966)         $ 4,517 \par -----------------------------  -----------  --------------  -----------  -------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Represents the sensitivity of net interest income for the next 12 months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) Represents the sensitivity of the present value of assets, liabilities and off-balance sheet instruments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Interest rate sensitivity is based on the earlier of the repricing \par   or maturity date of the assets, liabilities and derivative financial \par   instruments used to manage interest rate risk. The situation presented \par   reflects the position on that date only and can change significantly \par   in subsequent years depending on the preferences of members and clients \par   and the application of policies on interest rate risk management. \par  \par   Some Consolidated Balance Sheets items are considered non interest \par   rate-sensitive instruments, such as non-performing loans, non-interest-bearing \par   deposits, non-maturity deposits with an interest rate not referenced \par   to a specific rate (such as the prime rate), and equity. CCD's management \par   practices are based, as required by its policies, on prudent assumptions \par   regarding the maturity profile used in its models in order to determine \par   their interest rate sensitivity. \par  \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RISK \par  \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk may be defined as the risk that the actual or \par   expected value of assets denominated in a foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be \par   higher or lower than that of liabilities denominated in the same \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par   Overall, CCD's exposure to this risk is low because the majority of \par   its transactions are conducted in Canadian dollars. However, in certain \par   specific situations, CCD may become exposed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, \par   particularly with respect to the U.S. dollar and the euro. This exposure \par   mainly arises from its intermediation activities with members and clients, \par   and it financing and investing activities. To ensure that such exposure \par   is well controlled and limited, CCD uses, among other things, derivative \par   financial instruments such as forward exchange contracts and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par   swaps. \par -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  MANAGEMENT OF MARKET RISK RELATED TO TRADING ACTIVITIES - VALUE-AT-RISK \par  \par   The market risk of trading portfolios is managed daily under a specific \par   policy for that purpose. The main tool used to measure the market risk \par   of trading portfolios is "Value-at-Risk" (VaR), which represents an \par   estimate of the potential loss for a certain period of time at a given \par   confidence level. \par  \par   A Monte Carlo VaR is calculated daily, using a 99% confidence level, \par   on the trading portfolios for a holding horizon of one day. It is therefore \par   reasonable to expect a loss exceeding the VaR figure once every 100 \par   days. The calculation of VaR is based on historical data over a one-year \par   interval. \par  \par   The following table presents the aggregate VaR of CCD's trading activities \par   by risk category as well as the diversification effect, which represents \par   the difference between aggregate VaR and the sum of the VaR for the \par   different risk categories. Interest rate and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risks \par   are two risk categories to which CCD is exposed. The definition of \par   a trading portfolio meets the various criteria defined in the Basel \par   Capital Accord. \par ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 27-02-13 1829GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 23 - VaR BY RISK CATEGORY (TRADING PORTFOLIO)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                      2012                                     2011 \par                     --------------------------------------  ---------------------------------------- \par                        As at        For the year ended         As at         For the year ended \par                       December          December 31           December           December 31 \par                          31                                      31 \par ------------------  ----------  --------------------------  ----------  ---------------------------- \par  (in thousands                   Average     High      Low               Average       High      Low \par   of dollars) \par ------------------  ----------  --------  -------  -------  ----------  --------  ---------  ------- \par                                                 $ \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2          $ 40      $ 42      114      $ 1        $ 39      $ 95      $ 526      $ 2 \par  Interest rate             357       467      777      269         275       339        989      117 \par  Diversification \par   effect(1)               (26)      (44)   N/A(2)   N/A(2)        (53)      (86)     N/A(2)   N/A(2) \par ------------------  ----------  --------  -------  -------  ----------  --------  ---------  ------- \par                                                 $ \par  Aggregate VaR           $ 371     $ 465      745    $ 264       $ 261     $ 348    $ 1,040    $ 133 \par ------------------  ----------  --------  -------  -------  ----------  --------  ---------  ------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Risk reduction related to diversification, namely the difference between the sum of the VaR for the various market risk categories and the aggregate VaR.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) Not applicable: the highs and lows of the various market risk categories can refer to different dates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  As at December 31, 2012, the aggregate VaR was $370,879, with the interest \par   rate VaR being the largest component. This aggregate VaR was lower \par   than its annual average of $465,460. The risk mitigation related to \par   diversification was $26,411 as at December 31, 2012. \par  \par   BACK TESTING \par  \par   Back testing is conducted to validate the VaR model used by comparing \par   the VaR daily with the profits or losses (hereinafter called "P&L") \par   on CCD's portfolios. \par  \par   CCD performs back testing daily, applying a hypothetical P&L to its \par   trading portfolios. The hypothetical P&L is calculated by determining \par   the difference in value resulting from changes in market conditions \par   between two consecutive days. The portfolio mix between these two days \par   remains static. \par --------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chart below presents changes in VaR for trading activities as well as the profits and losses related to these activities. During the fourth quarter of 2012, the actual P&L did not exceeded VaR.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHART 3\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (REFER TO DOCUMENT <<MDA CCD - CHART 3 -T4-2012 >>)\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/8419Y_-2013-2-27.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/8419Y_-2013-2-27.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  STRESS TESTING \par  \par   From time to time, certain events that are considered highly unlikely \par   may occur and have a significant impact on trading portfolios. These \par   events at the tail-end of the distribution are the result of extreme \par   situations. \par  \par   The approach used to measure the risk related to events that are highly \par   unlikely, but plausible, is applied through the regular use of a stress \par   testing program (sensitivity tests, historical scenarios and hypothetical \par   scenarios). Stress testing results are analyzed together with the VaR \par   calculations in order to detect vulnerability to such events. The stress \par   testing program is reviewed periodically to ensure that it is kept \par   current. \par --------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  LIQUIDITY RISK \par  \par   Liquidity risk refers to CCD's capacity to raise the necessary funds \par   (by increasing liabilities or converting assets) to meet a financial \par   obligation, whether or not it appears on the Consolidated Balance Sheets. \par  \par   Through Desjardins Group, CCD is exposed to liquidity risk during potential \par   crisis situations that would result in exceptional cash outflows and \par   make it difficult for the Desjardins Group Treasury to find sufficient \par   funding to meet this sudden demand. For instance, such extreme situations \par   could occur following massive withdrawals in the caisse network, which \par   might occur if members lose confidence in the strength of the institution. \par   Liquidity risk management is a key component of the overall risk management \par   strategy. CCD and Desjardins Group have established policies describing \par   the principles, limits, risk appetite and tolerance levels and mechanisms \par   applicable to liquidity risk management. \par ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  LIQUIDITY RISK MANAGEMENT \par  \par   Liquidity risk is managed in order to ensure that CCD and Desjardins \par   Group have access, on a timely basis and in a profitable manner, to \par   the funds needed to meet their financial obligations as they become \par   due in both routine and crisis situations. Managing this risk involves \par   maintaining a sufficient level of liquid securities, ensuring stable \par   and diversified sources of funding, monitoring indicators and adopting \par   a contingency plan to implement in the event of a liquidity crisis. \par  \par   Policies and standards are reviewed on a regular basis to ensure that \par   they are appropriate for the operating environment and prevailing market \par   conditions. They are also updated based on regulatory requirements \par   and sound liquidity risk management practices. \par -------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  LIQUIDITY RESERVES \par  \par   The minimum liquidity reserves that must be maintained by the caisse \par   network, the Federation and CCD are prescribed in policies and standards \par   specific to each one. Daily management of securities and the reserve \par   level to be maintained is centralized at Desjardins Group Treasury \par   and is subject to risk management monitoring by the Risk Management \par   Division under the supervision of Desjardins Group's Finance and Risk \par   Management Committee. Eligible securities must meet high security and \par   negotiability standards. The securities held in these funds are largely \par   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canadian government\f28 \uc2 \f2  securities. \par  \par   The aggregate liquidity reserves held in these funds provide Desjardins \par   Group and CCD with some room to manoeuvre and are monitored daily. \par   In addition to complying with specific policies and standards, the \par   levels maintained must provide assurance of their adequacy in the event \par   of a possible severe liquidity crisis directly affecting Desjardins \par   Group and CCD, as it would be the case, for instance, if its credit \par   rating were downgraded by rating agencies. A Desjardins Group-wide \par   crisis scenario program has been implemented for this purpose. This \par   program incorporates the concepts put forward by BIS under the document \par   entitled "Basel III: International Framework for Liquidity Risk Measurement, \par   Standards and Monitoring." The scenarios make it possible to measure \par   the extent of potential cash outflows in a crisis situation, to implement \par   liquidity ratios and levels to be maintained throughout Desjardins \par   Group, and to assess the potential marginal cost of such events, depending \par   on the type, severity and level of the crisis. \par  \par   The arrival of Basel III will also strengthen international minimum \par   liquidity requirements. This accord introduced a liquidity coverage \par   ratio (LCR) to allow financial institutions to face potential liquidity \par   crisis situations, and a net stable funding ratio (NSFR) to address \par   mismatches between assets and funding sources. Even though the Basel \par   Committee is still studying these two ratios and there is currently \par   an observation period, the AMF expects Desjardins Group and CCD to \par   calculate these ratios in order to benefit from an observation period \par   before the planned effective dates of January 1, 2015 for the LCR and \par   January 1, 2018 for the NSFR. Desjardins Group already monitors these \par   two ratios on a regular basis for management information purposes and \par   intends to comply with the new standards when they become effective. \par ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \par  \par  \par  SOURCES OF FUNDING \par  \par   CCD ensures stable and diversified sources of institutional funding \par   by type, source and maturity. It uses a wide range of financial products \par   and borrowing programs on various markets to meet its financing needs. \par  \par   It should be noted that systems are in place for the securitization \par   of CMHC-insured loans. Furthermore, CCD is eligible for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's various \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  programs and the loan facilities for \par   Emergency Lending Assistance advances. \par -------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  LIQUIDITY RISK INDICATORS \par  \par   The purpose of monitoring liquidity indicators daily is to quickly \par   identify a lack of liquidity, whether potential or real, within \par   CCD and Desjardins Group and on capital markets. For each liquidity \par   indicator defined, warning levels are established and subject \par   to an escalation process. If one or more indicators trigger a \par   warning level, the Finance and Risk Management Committee is immediately \par   alerted. This committee would also act as a crisis committee \par   should the contingency plan need to be applied. \par  \par   The indicators are divided into two categories: indicators specific \par   to CCD and Desjardins Group, and capital market indicators. The \par   first category includes indicators that monitor the level of \par   CCD's and Desjardins Group's flexibility, members' behaviour, \par   the credit spreads facing CCD, and the effectiveness of institutional \par   funding sources. Market indicators apply primarily to the credit \par   spreads observed for financial institutions and provinces compared \par   to federal bonds. \par ------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  CONTINGENCY PLAN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 27-02-13 1829GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   CCD and Desjardins Group developed a liquidity contingency plan providing \par   for, in particular, an internal crisis committee vested with special \par   decision-making powers to deal with crisis situations. This plan lists \par   the sources of liquidity available in exceptional situations. It also \par   prescribes a decision-making and information process based on the severity \par   level of a possible crisis. \par  \par   The objective of the plan is to make it possible to quickly and effectively \par   minimize disruptions caused by sudden changes in members' and clients' \par   behaviour and potential disruptions in capital markets or economic \par   conditions. \par ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SOURCES OF FINANCING\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Desjardins Group's treasurer, CCD meets the needs of its members and clients. Its first priority is to implement appropriate strategies to identify, measure and manage risks. These activities are regulated by a liquidity sufficiency and administration policy and a refinancing management policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In order to maintain stable and diversified refinancing, CCD ensures the diversification of its sources of financing from institutional capital markets. It therefore regularly resorts to the capital market when conditions are favourable, and occasionally makes public and private issues of term notes on Canadian, U.S. and European markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2012, CCD managed to maintain sufficient liquidity to meet Desjardins Group's needs through its rigorous treasury policy, solid institutional refinancings and the contribution of the caisse network. CCD's strict treasury strategy allowed it to be well positioned to confidently weather the period of global financial stabilization that is currently beginning.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In keeping with the strategy of increasing the duration of institutional refinancing and its mission as Desjardins Group treasurer, CCD issued debt securities on various markets in 2012. In March, it issued medium-term covered bonds in the amount of US$1.5 billion on the U.S. market. In October 2012, it also issued medium-term notes in an amount of $800 million. This second issue, the largest of its kind on the Canadian market, was very well received by investors. The presence of Desjardins on these different markets also helped expand its pool of institutional investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD continued to be present on the federally-guaranteed mortgage loan securitization market under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canada Mortgage\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bonds Program (CMB). CCD has in fact been active in this area, with participation in new issues of approximately $1.5 billion in 2012, namely four five-year fixed rate CMB issues, and one five-year floating rate CMB issue. The main objective of the program is to obtain a source of long-term financing at the lowest price on the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overall, these transactions made it possible to adequately meet the liquidity needs of Desjardins Group, to better diversity sources of financing and to further extend their average term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following is a summary of CCD's various borrowing programs:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the Canadian market:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -   a medium-term note program of up to $5 billion; \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the international market:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -   a European short-term note program of EUR1 billion; \par    -   a U.S. short-term note program of US$5 billion; \par    -   a global multi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  medium-term note program of EUR7 billion. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It should be noted that the medium-term multi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  note program is currently in the process of being renewed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Charts 4 and 5 present the breakdown of deposits as at December 31, 2012 by category and by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHART 4\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (REFER TO DOCUMENT << MDA CCD - CHART 4 - T4-2012 >>)\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/8419Y_-2013-2-27.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/8419Y_-2013-2-27.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 CHART 5\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (REFER TO DOCUMENT << MDA CCD - CHART 5 - T4-2012 >>)\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/8419Y_-2013-2-27.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/8419Y_-2013-2-27.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 RATING AGENCIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The credit ratings assigned to CCD by ratings agencies are instrumental to its access to sources of wholesale financing and the cost of such financing, as well as Desjardins Group's credibility and recognition among institutional investors. Rating agencies assign investment-grade ratings to securities issued by CCD and recognize the financial strength of Desjardins Group, its capitalization, the stability of its operating surplus earnings, its leading role in local markets and the quality of its assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On December 13, 2012, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgraded the credit ratings of CCD and five other Canadian financial institutions. This agency said it was concerned about the debt levels of Canadians and the consequences they could have on financial institutions, particularly on their mortgage loan portfolios and the volume of credit losses. It expects a slowdown in credit demand and greater pressure on the margins of the country's financial institutions, in a low interest rate environment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, on January 28, 2013, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgraded the credit ratings of CCD and five other Canadian financial institutions. This agency stated that this decision was essentially due to the economic situation in Canada, that shows signs of concern, including high consumer debt levels and elevated housing prices. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  also clarified that financial institutions are more vulnerable than in the past to downside risks the Canadian economy faces.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Management is of the opinion that these decisions have more to do with the agencies' concern about Canada's economic situation than with the quality of CCD's loan portfolio or balance sheet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even after the downgrade, CCD's credit ratings remain among the best in Canada and compare favourably with those of many large international and Canadian financial institutions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The credit ratings and outlooks assigned by Fitch and DBRS to CCD's various securities were unchanged in fiscal 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 24 - CREDIT RATINGS OF ISSUED SECURITIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                     Standard \par                            DBRS      & Poor's  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2   Fitch \par ----------------------  ----------                      ----- \par Short-term              R-1 (high)  A-1        P-1      F1+ \par Medium- and long-term, \par  senior                 AA          A+         Aa2      AA- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CONTRACTUAL OBLIGATIONS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Contractual obligations lead to commitments through contracts under which