\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Issue For M Stanley: Client Behavior, Not Collateral\b0\par\par\uc2 1,380 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2012\par 17:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:11 (Dow Jones) If \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrades \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's (MS) credit rating by three notches from A2 to Baa2, it could result in a collateral call of about $5B for MS, Bernstein says. However, the possible collateral requirement "should not be a problem for MS" as firm has $179B in cash and "highly liquid un-hypothecated" securities available to meet the impact of a downgrade or unexpected cash demand, Bernstein says; adds most severe impact of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  move is client behavior as it estimates much of the greater than 4-year maturity OTC derivatives business would likely migrate away from MS to other Baa rated banks. MS down 2.5% at $15.75. (brett.philbin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:02 (Dow Jones) While raving about \uc2 Millennial Media\uc2 's (MM) business, analysts that were part of the mobile-ad concern's March IPO recommend investors stay on the sidelines. The offering priced at $13, and MM nearly doubled in its debut, but shares have been sliding since. They're off another 13% today at $16.47. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says MM has a "strong competitive position," but it starts coverage at neutral because the "potential is already reflected in the stock's premium valuation." \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2  also start at neutral-equivalent ratings. (alexandra.scaggs@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:58 (Dow Jones) Local Alabama governments will have to pay higher costs to borrow unless state officials show they are willing help bankrupt Jefferson County, says Municipal Market Advisors. A bill that would give JeffCo limited taxing ability--and the power to replace a major source of lost revenue--could be voted on by the state house as soon as Wednesday. But it faces some opposition, and time during the state's regular legislative session is running out. "This remains a very ugly situation," MMA says. "The state continues to show almost no meaningful interest in aiding its largest county... The implications for other local bondholders are clear." (kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:51 (Dow Jones) Analysts have good things to say about apartment operator Post Properties (PPS) after it reported a solid 1Q with FFO well ahead of consensus. Same-store performance "sits at the top of the apartment pack and condo sales seem to have been accelerated," \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  writes. Austin and Houston performed well, as did Charlotte and Atlanta. New York and DC were the weakest. Says Raymond James: PPS is a "compelling multifamily story...bolstered by well-located, high-quality assets in attractive job markets and a recognizable brand poised for growth in an improving economy." PPS up 0.3% at $49.50. (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:50 (Dow Jones) Andersons (ANDE) sees a "challenging" 2Q as profits from its wheat storage business struggle to keep pace with year-ago levels. The grain handler's 1Q EPS of 98c beats Street's expectation of 86c on strong grain trading earnings, but its ethanol division barely ekes out a profit amid a glut of ethanol that's weighed on margins industry-wide. ANDE also expects weaker ethanol margins in 2Q. Shares down 2.7% to $48.40, up almost 11% year-to-date. (ian.berry@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:49 (Dow Jones) US stocks may just be having a bit of a delayed reaction to the weekend election results. "I'm kind of surprised it wasn't down more so yesterday," says Stephen J. Carl at The Williams Capital Group. "Overseas, there's a problem there. I think it's going to spill over into other markets." Trading in London was closed for Monday for a UK holiday, so for some investors, today was the first chance to sell on increased uncertainty in Greece as well as France. Dow industrials are at session lows, down 165 at 12843. (matthew.jarzemsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:46 (Dow Jones) The Canadian dollar is captured by stealth "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " by foreign central banks. This is CIBC's Avery Shenfeld's provocative conclusion. Foreign central banks are snapping up CAD and selling USD, helping bolster the Canadian unit, Shenfeld said during a panel at the Bloomberg \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Economic Summit in Toronto. "If that's not \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I don't know what is," he said. Shenfeld said that to offset those CAD positive moves, the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  should develop a scheme to purchase other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "and turn those flows the other way." (david.george-cosh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:44 (Dow Jones) First quarter results for Brazil's largest retailer, Companhia Brasileira de Distribuicao (CBD PCAR5.BR), were better than expected, given the poor economic performance in Brazil, says CBD chairman Abilio Diniz. "We don't see any problems ahead, wither with regards to credit or non-payment," Diniz says. "I see a much better second half." The company wants to gain market share and reduce expenses, to be ready for when the economy starts to pick up, he added. In NY, CBD off 3.7% at $44.37. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:41 (Dow Jones) Intel's (INTC) new Romley server chip is presenting a big opportunity for Micron (MU). Brian Shirley, the head of MU's DRAM business, says during Jefferies conference that MU has the most excitement right now around its server business, which is benefiting from Romley launch. INTC's processor requires more memory, and Shirley says servers are one area where memory isn't treated as just a commodity product. Expects server memory \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to grow faster than some of MU's other businesses during next several years. MU is "supply limited," in servers, he adds, and would like more capacity to increase shipments for that market. MU off 4.8% to $6.19. (shara.tibken@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:40 (Dow Jones) Despite Chile's low on-month April CPI gain of 0.1%, \uc2 Banco de Credito e Inversiones\uc2  (BCI.SN) says the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise the benchmark rate by a quarter of a percentage point to 5.25% sooner than expected. BCI considers the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stop its monetary stimulus by raising the rate in its monthly meeting next week. According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most recent monthly survey of analysts' expectations, the benchmark rate will likely increase by 25 or 50 basis points before the year ends. Chile CPI gained 3.5% on the year in April, well within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target tolerance range. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a target of 3%, plus or minus one percentage point. (graciela.ibanez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:37 (Dow Jones) Sales of construction excavators in China fell 43% last month vs year ago as weakness in equipment demand continues. \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 's (CAT) April excavator volume was down 47%, but its share of the China's excavator market YTD is mostly unchanged vs year ago at 5.8%. Bulging machinery inventories and sluggish demand likely to weigh on the market for most of the year. But year-earlier comparisons for excavators should become easier to beat as impact of higher interest rates in China begin showing up in last year's equipment volumes. Monthly sales average for 2H 2011 was just 8,450 excavators. By comparison, April's sales volume was 15,370 excavators. CAT down 2.7% at $94.59. (robert.tita@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:36 (Dow Jones) In a sign of worker confidence, the number of people voluntarily quitting their job rose to 2.15M in March from 2.07M in February, says the Labor Department, hitting the highest level since October 2008. Remember, that's as financial markets were in freefall. As a percent of employment, the quit rate stayed at 1.6%, higher than it was during the recession. A rising quit rate is seen as a sign people are confident they can quickly find a job after leaving their previous one. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.co \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-08-12 1111ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120508e858000da\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
