\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Foreign Central Banks ' \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2 ' In CAD -CIBC\b0\par\par\uc2 1,375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2012\par 16:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:46 (Dow Jones) The Canadian dollar is captured by stealth "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " by foreign central banks. This is CIBC's Avery Shenfeld's provocative conclusion. Foreign central banks are snapping up CAD and selling USD, helping bolster the Canadian unit, Shenfeld said during a panel at the Bloomberg \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Economic Summit in Toronto. "If that's not \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I don't know what is," he said. Shenfeld said that to offset those CAD positive moves, the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  should develop a scheme to purchase other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "and turn those flows the other way." (david.george-cosh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:44 (Dow Jones) First quarter results for Brazil's largest retailer, Companhia Brasileira de Distribuicao (CBD PCAR5.BR), were better than expected, given the poor economic performance in Brazil, says CBD chairman Abilio Diniz. "We don't see any problems ahead, wither with regards to credit or non-payment," Diniz says. "I see a much better second half." The company wants to gain market share and reduce expenses, to be ready for when the economy starts to pick up, he added. In NY, CBD off 3.7% at $44.37. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:41 (Dow Jones) Intel's (INTC) new Romley server chip is presenting a big opportunity for Micron (MU). Brian Shirley, the head of MU's DRAM business, says during Jefferies conference that MU has the most excitement right now around its server business, which is benefiting from Romley launch. INTC's processor requires more memory, and Shirley says servers are one area where memory isn't treated as just a commodity product. Expects server memory \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to grow faster than some of MU's other businesses during next several years. MU is "supply limited," in servers, he adds, and would like more capacity to increase shipments for that market. MU off 4.8% to $6.19. (shara.tibken@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:40 (Dow Jones) Despite Chile's low on-month April CPI gain of 0.1%, \uc2 Banco de Credito e Inversiones\uc2  (BCI.SN) says the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise the benchmark rate by a quarter of a percentage point to 5.25% sooner than expected. BCI considers the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stop its monetary stimulus by raising the rate in its monthly meeting next week. According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most recent monthly survey of analysts' expectations, the benchmark rate will likely increase by 25 or 50 basis points before the year ends. Chile CPI gained 3.5% on the year in April, well within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target tolerance range. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a target of 3%, plus or minus one percentage point. (graciela.ibanez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:37 (Dow Jones) Sales of construction excavators in China fell 43% last month vs year ago as weakness in equipment demand continues. \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 's (CAT) April excavator volume was down 47%, but its share of the China's excavator market YTD is mostly unchanged vs year ago at 5.8%. Bulging machinery inventories and sluggish demand likely to weigh on the market for most of the year. But year-earlier comparisons for excavators should become easier to beat as impact of higher interest rates in China begin showing up in last year's equipment volumes. Monthly sales average for 2H 2011 was just 8,450 excavators. By comparison, April's sales volume was 15,370 excavators. CAT down 2.7% at $94.59. (robert.tita@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:36 (Dow Jones) In a sign of worker confidence, the number of people voluntarily quitting their job rose to 2.15M in March from 2.07M in February, says the Labor Department, hitting the highest level since October 2008. Remember, that's as financial markets were in freefall. As a percent of employment, the quit rate stayed at 1.6%, higher than it was during the recession. A rising quit rate is seen as a sign people are confident they can quickly find a job after leaving their previous one. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:33 (Dow Jones) Having broken below $1.30, the euro is bouncing just above yesterday's low of $1.2955 but hasn't been able to break through. Options investors may be defending positions put on last week that were based on a view that the euro would hold its range and possibly pop higher, says Luca Avellini at JW Partners, a $140M fund of funds invested in 23 managers who focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategies. For example, he knows of a $1B options position with a $1.27 strike put and $1.33 knockout, which means the investor is likely buying euros at current levels to avoid hitting the strike. Avellini adds corporates with euro revenues have also sold $1.29 put options, which triggers buying at that level. (matt.walter@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:32 (Dow Jones) Investors hoping for confirmation -- or a denial -- from Micron (MU) that it's the likely buyer of Elipda need to keep waiting. Brian Shirley, the exec in charge of MU's DRAM business, says during Jefferies conference today that he can't comment on any speculation and there won't be anything official from MU at this time regarding what's in the press. Japanese publications have reported MU was picked as the winning bidder for rival DRAM maker Elpida, which declared bankruptcy in March. Shirley says MU always looking at opportunities to make itself more competitive. Notes additional capacity is one way to do that. MU off 4.5% to $6.21. (shara.tibken@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:29 (Dow Jones) Mexico's IPC index down 2%, wiping out all of the previous session's gains and a record high close, as heavyweight \uc2 America Movil\uc2  L shares dive 6.1% to MXN17.53 after the firm says it wants to raise stake in Dutch telecom operator \uc2 Royal KPN NV\uc2  to 28%. \uc2 America Movil\uc2 , Latin America's biggest wireless provider, had sparked yesterday's rally by adding nearly 5% after successfully appealing a government regulator's planned fine of nearly $1B. Actinver says it expects very high levels volatility in the IPC for months to come, with individual issues perhaps swinging in an even wider range than the market as a whole. (laurence.iliff@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:24 (Dow Jones) \uc2 OfficeMax\uc2 's (OMX) 1Q results show the office products supplier is holding its own and that it is making progress against a strategic plan that was laid out last fall, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. The first phase of the plan is to strengthen the core "and there was clear evidence of that this quarter," with business contract margins expanding 180 bps and the closing of 23 underperforming stores, firm says. A major plus is that OMX is not sacrificing margins to achieve its goals firm says. "Margins appear to be stable to modestly improving, reflecting better assortments, a focus on private brands, and more disciplined pricing." OMX up 10% to $4.86. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:21 (Dow Jones) Given Europe's ongoing woes, it's "probably not" the time to invest in private equity real estate or other consumer-type deals. However, infrastructure transactions are attractive because distribution networks of essentials such as gas are relatively insulated from economic volatility, \uc2 CPP Investment Board\uc2  Exec VP Mark Wiseman said at the Bloomberg Canada Economic Summit. Wiseman also said Canada is increasingly becoming an exporter of capital because of country's economic strength. (ben.dummett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:17 (Dow Jones) An underperforming manufacturing sector may help shift Canada's economic strengths to its resource space, says RBC's Eric Lascelles. He adds a strong Canadian dollar will help ensure much of Canada's growth will come from the commodity-linked market. "It's a function of our own economic position." (david.geroge-cosh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.co \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-08-12 1047ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120508e858000c6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
