\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Canada's Carney says may need to keep rates low\b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2011\par 16:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Carney sees U.S. slowdown, C$ weighing on economy\par \par \uc2 * Says headwinds reflected in inflation data, exports\par \par \uc2 * Neutral rate may be lower than before\par \par \uc2 * Unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not the best option\par \par \uc2 OTTAWA, June 24 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to keep interest rates low as the economy faces "substantial headwinds," \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  Governor Mark Carney said in an interview published on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy may still need to be stimulative in order to close the output gap and in order to get inflation back to target," Carney is quoted as saying in an interview with the Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 The bank has kept its key interest rate on hold at 1 percent since last September, after lifting it from a rock-bottom 0.25 percent. Its next rate decision is July 19.\par \par \uc2 Most market players surveyed by Reuters on May 31 forecast the bank would resume rate increases in September, but some have since pushed their forecasts even further out.\par \par \uc2 As reflected in yields on overnight index swaps, swaps traders have priced out the probability of rate hikes for the remainder of this year. <BOCWATCH>\par \par \uc2 Carney named the U.S. economic slowdown and the strong Canadian dollar as two major factors that could hinder Canada's economic expansion.\par \par \uc2 "We see the headwinds both in our export performance and in the inflation data," he said of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , referring to core inflation, which was 1.6 percent in April.\par \par \uc2 Overall inflation has been above 3 percent -- and above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  target range -- for two months but is expected to moderate to 2 percent by mid-2012. [ID:nN20206551]\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar is currently trading around C$0.98 to the U.S. dollar, or $1.02.\par \par \uc2 Carney repeated the long-standing policy that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength if extreme volatility threatened the economy. Unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the best option, he said.\par \par \uc2 Carney said the bank may not need to raise rates to a "neutral" level just as the economy reaches its production capacity, and repeated the idea that the neutral rate, which he declined to specify, might be lower than it was before the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Despite the obstacles Canada faces, Carney sees economic growth gathering speed in the second half of this year, helped by a recovery in business investment. [ID:nN1E75L1WZ]\par \par \uc2 Carney has raised concerns in recent comments about possible overheating in the Canadian housing market, where prices in some cities, notably Vancouver, have skyrocketed.[ID:nN15107810]\par \par \uc2 Higher short-term interest rates could be a tool to curb high housing prices, he said, but he added that regulatory action should be the first option.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Louise Egan; editing by Jeffrey Hodgson and Peter Galloway)\par \par \uc2 CANADA-ECONOMY/CARNEY (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110627e76o00100\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
