\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Japan Keeps Threat Of  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Alive\b0\par\par\uc2 Takashi Nakamichi ,Bob Davis  \par \uc2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2011\par 22:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's finance minister said Thursday he would take "decisive steps" to stabilize the yen if it becomes "excessively volatile," even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had "calmed down" since last month's concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Group of Seven leading nations.\par \par \uc2 Yoshihiko Noda's remarks suggest that Japanese authorities haven't been spooked by the yen's moderate rebound over recent days, but would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets--either jointly with the G-7 or unilaterally--if the yen's appreciation accelerates.\par \par \uc2 Surges in the yen could hinder Japanese efforts to revive an export-led economy hit hard by a massive earthquake and tsunami on March 11. Gains in the yen make Japanese exports less competitive oversea, reduce the value of profits sent home and often prompt Japanese manufacturers to shift production overseas.\par \par \uc2 Noda said he would use a meeting of G-7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs scheduled for Thursday evening to "call on them to continue to monitor the market carefully and cooperate as appropriate," Noda told Dow Jones in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "I'd like to maintain relationships in which we could cooperate as appropriate when there is excessive volatility or disorderly movement in the market."\par \par \uc2 Noda also left the door open for unilateral action as well, saying, "Our fundamental stance is that if there is excessive volatility or disorderly movement, we will take decisive steps."\par \par \uc2 The G-7---the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, the U.K., Italy and \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 ---\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on March 18 to curb what they saw as excessive strength in the yen. Their first concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a decade came after the dollar hit a record low of Y76.25 on March 17. Neither Japan nor the group has stepped into the market since.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Thursday, the dollar fell briefly to Y82.94, breaching the Y83 level for the first time in two weeks as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors trimmed their positions ahead of the release of U.S. economic data. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been falling since it rose to around Y85.50 on April 6.\par \par \uc2 At 0002 GMT on Friday, the dollar was at Y83.63.\par \par \uc2 Even so, Noda said that since the joint G-7 action, "the market has calmed down." On March 17, the day before the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said "I saw the most excessive volatility."\par \par \uc2 Noda, meanwhile, said the government of Prime Minister Naoto Kan may consider introducing new subsidies to encourage manufacturers in the quake-hit region to relocate their factories to other places in Japan instead of overseas.\par \par \uc2 After the government compiles the first of a planned series of extra budgets this month, "there will be debate over what policy measures are needed to revive Japan, and that (subsidy issue) may become a topic of discussions then," he said.\par \par \uc2 Noda's remarks reflect concerns in Japan that damage from the disaster, combined with the yen's strength, may prompt makers to rebuild factories overseas, accelerating the hollowing out of Japan's manufacturing industries.\par \par \uc2 He also said the nuclear power plant in Fukushima prefecture--where crippled reactors have emitted large amounts of radioactive substances--"remain in a worrisome state." He pledged "all-out efforts" by the Japanese government to prevent any further dispersion of such materials and to quickly provide accurate information on the plant to foreign countries.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi of Dow Jones Newswires and Bob Davis of The Wall Street Journal; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com; bob.davis@wsj.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCOMM1120110414e74e0017q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
