\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Dollar Edges Higher Despite Chinese Rate Increase\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2011\par 15:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar edged slightly higher Tuesday, shrugging off an interest rate increase in China that battered its Australian counterpart and instead taking support from firm oil prices and positive sentiment.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar is trading at C$0.9654 from C$0.9674 late Monday.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  surprised markets overnight by raising benchmark lending and deposit rates by 25 basis points when Hong Kong and Chinese markets were closed for a public holiday. While such moves have weighed on the risk-sensitive Canadian dollar in the past, there was no sustained impact Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "There was almost no reaction on [the Canadian dollar] from China's interest rate hike and instead strong oil prices are supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a report from Camilla Sutton, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital.\par \par \uc2 "Oil remains the most important risk to [Canadian dollar] traders. Our base case is that oil prices remain elevated this year, which combined with Canada's fundamental position, should keep [it] well supported," Sutton said .\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures fell Tuesday after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates, but remain at elevated levels.\par \par \uc2 Market sentiment is broadly favorable for the Canadian dollar, and will limit any moves towards the downside in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said David Watt, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In general, it's just the sentiment out there that the Canadian dollar is poised to do well," Watt said.\par \par \uc2 Watt said that sentiment will support a continued gradual appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which touched its highest level since November 2007 Monday before receding modestly.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to have slow, steady gains. Nothing spectacular, but the direction is pretty clear," Watt said.\par \par \uc2 The government of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that it spent $124 million \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the joint G7 effort on March 18 to quell strength in the Japanese yen, the bank's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since a joint effort to stabilize the euro in 2000. The bank sold the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bought the Canadian dollar.\par \par \uc2 There were no other significant data releases Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, jobs data for March will be released.\par \par \uc2 The data will likely be robust and could provide added support for the Canadian dollar, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Economists believe the Canadian economy created 28,000 jobs in March, following a gain of 15,100 in the previous month.\par \par \uc2 -Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020;\par \par \uc2 don.curren@dowjones.com [ 04-05-11 0937ET ]\par \par \uc2 1158   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110405e7450007u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
