\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Dollar Higher As Oil Prices Wax, Nuclear Fears Wane\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Johnson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar was sharply higher early Monday, boosted by relative calm in the nuclear crisis in Japan and the outlook for higher commodity prices amid the conflict in Libya.\par \par \uc2 "The Canadian dollar got wings overnight," said Rahim Madhavji, president of Knightsbridge \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was at $0.9760, from 0.9859 late Friday, according to CQG.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of further coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, is helping to curb yen appreciation for now. The Canadian dollar was at Y82.94, from Y82.01 late Friday.\par \par \uc2 Japanese financial markets were closed Monday for Vernal Equinox Day.\par \par \uc2 Power was restored to the Daiichi nuclear plant in Japan, restoring some cooling functions for the reactor and signalling an end to rolling blackouts in Tokyo. And \uc2 Sony\uc2  and Nissan announced that they would restart some \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, providing a more encouraging economic backdrop and putting a spring in investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 In Libya, meanwhile, the U.S. and its allies were intensifying air strikes against forces loyal to Col. Moammar Gadhafi, emboldening the pro-democracy uprising.\par \par \uc2 "Market optimism from Japanese progress in restarting power to its damaged nuclear reactors is being countered by concerns that the repercussions of allied involvement in Libya is hard to quantify," said Bob Block, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at BMO Capital Markets in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 For now, the concerns are giving commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like Canada's, a boost. Oil prices were also sharply higher, with West Texas Intermediate crude back above $103.\par \par \uc2 "The correlation between CAD and oil remains notably strong," said Camilla Sutton, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto. The Canadian dollar's correlation with interest rate spreads meanwhile has been slipping, and was recently at 0.57.\par \par \uc2 "With military action having begun in Libya, we would expect upward pressure on oil to continue and CAD to move higher," Sutton said.\par \par \uc2 There are no major Canadian data releases on tap for Monday.\par \par \uc2 At the close of the trading day Tuesday, however, the federal budget is due to be released, an event that could trigger Canada's third federal election in five years.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tend to react unfavorably to political uncertainty," Sutton said. However, the Canadian dollar to date has seemed content to ignore domestic political drama, keeping its focus fixed on oil prices and geopolitical events abroad.\par \par \uc2 Traders expect the U.S. dollar to travel between C$0.9710 and C$0.9810 Monday.\par \par \uc2 There was no sign over the weekend of further yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "but its potential is impacting activity," BMO's Block said, with investors wary of buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid threat of another \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coordinated selloff.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, there was a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. Federal Reserve, the Bank of Japan, the Bank of Italy, Bundesbank, Bank of France and Bank of England--the first such move in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 The central banks dumped yen Friday and bought their domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a bid to keep the yen's record surge from further damaging an economy ravaged by an earthquake, a tsunami and a nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 -Karen Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2022; karen.johnson@dowjones.com [ 03-21-11 0854ET ]\par \par \uc2 1158   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110321e73l0006u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
