\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Widespread Talk In Market That Central Banks Still Selling Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 16:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Central banks could still be in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets selling yen, according to widespread talk among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and analysts.\par \par \uc2 As part of their coordinated effort to depress the value of the yen, central banks are likely still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, keeping the dollar above Y81, according to major bank analysts and traders.\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve and \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early New York trading, following \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in European and Asian hours by other central banks, including the Bank of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Late morning in New York, neither the Bank of Japan nor Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would comment to Dow Jones Newswires whether Japan was in markets still to curb yen strength.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  did not immediately return calls.\par \par \uc2 Late morning in New York, the dollar was up sharply against the yen, trading at Y81.14 from Y78.89 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG.\par \par \uc2 Investors are on alert for other rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Andrew J. Johnson, Stephen Bernard, Javier David and Erin McCarthy contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 18, 2011 11:23 ET (15:23 GMT)\par \par \uc2 DJ Widespread Talk In Market That Central Banks Still Selling Yen By Bradley Davis Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Central banks could still be in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets selling yen, according to widespread talk among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and analysts.\par \par \uc2 As part of their coordinated effort to depress the value of the yen, central banks are likely still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, keeping the dollar above Y81, according to major bank analysts and traders.\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve and \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early New York trading, following \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in European and Asian hours by other central banks, including the Bank of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Late morning in New York, neither the Bank of Japan nor Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would comment to Dow Jones Newswires whether Japan was in markets still to curb yen strength.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  did not immediately return calls.\par \par \uc2 Late morning in New York, the dollar was up sharply against the yen, trading at Y81.14 from Y78.89 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG.\par \par \uc2 Investors are on alert for other rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Andrew J. Johnson, Stephen Bernard, Javier David and Erin McCarthy contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110319e73i00akl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic