\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Encana Enhances Kitimat LNG's Chances -Analyst\b0\par\par\uc2 1,160 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:44 (Dow Jones) Encana's (ECA) purchase of a 30% interest in the Kitimat LNG project in Canada's west coast increases the chances that the first major initiative to export North American gas will move forward--and soon, says Tudor Pickering Holt. The research firm notes a final investment decision is likely to come in late 2011, with the last hurdle being getting long-term gas prices indexed on oil. Shares up fractionally premarket amid a broader market rise. (angel.gonzalez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:37 (Dow Jones) Canadian dollar falls back after 8 a.m. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help push the yen lower. "I'm guessing that it's not as big as people had anticipated," says C.J. Gavsie, managing director of corporate and institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at BMO Capital Markets. He estimates that the tally likely was in the tens, not hundreds, of millions. The Canadian dollar was at Y82.79 recently, according to CQG. That's down from at Y83.33 at 8 a.m. EDT, at the time of the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:27 (Dow Jones) Fed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was nominal, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could re-enter later in the day, says Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon in New York. He calls the initial move symbolic to show the Fed stood with the Bank of Japan, but that it was also a "warning shot across the bow" of speculators trying to fight the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar is at about Y81.37 in Friday morning trading in New York from around Y79 late Thursday. (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:12 (Dow Jones) One day after Colombia President Juan Manuel Santos hinted Canada-based Greystar's (GSL.T) Angostura open-pit gold mine project was unlikely to be granted environmental approval, the company late Thursday gave up trying. "What happens next?" asks Chile-based Celfin Capital. "It's a case of rip it up and start again, one imagines, with an underground project now needing to be designed. And it could well be that they need a partner to help them pick up the slack given the massive investment they have already made." (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:01 (Dow Jones) The G7's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's strength hurt Treasurys overnight while stocks rallied, but tensions in Libya and uncertainty about Japan's nuclear crisis bring buyers back to safe-haven bonds. Some traders also say Treasurys could benefit as Japan need to recycle their dollar proceeds from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds, especially short to intermediate maturities, tend to be the parking slot. The 10-year note is 1/32 higher, yielding 3.251%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:52 (Dow Jones) The dollar and euro rises sharply against Japan's yen as the Group of Seven finance ministers agreed to the first coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since 2000. The dollar jumped at high as Y82.00 in Friday trading, from about Y78.84, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was most recently at Y81.49. Confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  thus far by the Bank of England, Bundesbank, \uc2 Banque de France\uc2 , Bank of Italy, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of Japan. US and Canada seen jumping in after regular trading starts in North America. The euro climbed to a high of Y115.56 from a low of Y110.61. (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:49 (Dow Jones) This week's run of Canadian data has been sparkling, notes \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , "suggesting that our high-side 1Q GDP forecast of 3.7% may not be high enough." (And that the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's 2.5% forecast is also too low.) What that means for future rate hikes, however, is murky. "At the moment, there are too many events outside Canada's borders that may influence the ability of Canada's economy to stay on course, and the April Monetary Policy Report will provide the Bank with its next opportunity to update its forecast as it sees fit." (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:44 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Nike\uc2  (NKE) should see near-term margin compression amid increased costs, note analysts as they cut estimates on the athletic-apparel giant following weaker-than-expected F3Q. Susquehanna sees at least three more quarters of margin hits amid higher input and air-freight costs and capacity shortages. While orders remain strong, the investment bank expects "premium valuations to come in some given lowered growth expectations and an uncertain margin outlook," so its price target falls to $82 from $90 on NKE. FBR lowers its forecast to $91 from $98 as it believes in the company's strength but notes it will be nearly a year before NKE's price hikes kick in. Shares off 6.3% premarket at $80; Citi calls under that "a buying opportunity." (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:35 (Dow Jones) BMO notes both core and headline inflation is easing in Canada while the rest of the world grapples with price pressures. The February drop in core prices reflects a spike in hotel charges because of the Winter Olympics a year ago, an impact that won't be part of March's data. But the low starting point is very favorable, BMO says. "Suffice it to say that this keeps the pressure well off the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to get back in tightening mode any time soon." (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 (Dow Jones) Stocks, bonds and commodities are generally higher around the world as the G7 agreed to move in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem "excess volatility and disorderly movements," though that's aimed at stemming the yen's recent gains. The yen fell to 81.42 per dollar from around 79 late Thursday in New York. The Nikkei rose 2.7%, but finished the week off 10%. European stocks are higher and US equities are poised to build on yesterday's rebound, with S&P 500 futures up 8.8 points. Nymex crude oil is continuing its march past $100, rising 1% to $102.50/barrel. And while many countries' bond yields are down, the US 10-year Treasury is up slightly to 3.27%. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:08 (Dow Jones) Traders say \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  will not likely act before 8 am EDT as part of the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quell the yen's strength. They expect the bank to follow the pattern of its global peers and act during usual trading hours, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Canadian dollar/yen cross. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-18-11 0844ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110318e73i0005b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
