\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Dollar Ends Historic Day Flat Vs US Dollar, Higher Vs Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Johnson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 20:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar ended a historic day in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market flat against its U.S. counterpart, and sharply higher against the yen, after a coordinated bid by the Group of Seven central banks to soften the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was at C$0.9859 at 3:30 p.m. EDT (1930 GMT), from C$0.9810 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT) and C$0.9862 late Thursday, according to data provider CQG.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar was at Y82.01 late Friday against the yen, off its highs of Y83.34 immediately after the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to buy Canadian dollars against the yen. It gained sharply in overnight trading, rising from Y80.00 late Thursday after the G-7 released an initial statement committing to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed early Friday that it had joined a "concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " by selling Japanese yen. The size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't disclosed Friday, but will be revealed in \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly international reserves data, set for release on April 5.\par \par \uc2 "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside of the Bank of Japan in general was not massive. I think the Bank of Japan is doing all of the heavy lifting," said Steve Butler, managing director at Scotia Capital in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 According to the Canadian finance department's international reserves data, yen holdings were worth $252 million in February out of total foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $47.33 billion.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar launched higher against the yen immediately after the official buying action, then swiftly backed off its highs, probably because the market had effectively priced in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in advance, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "I'm guessing that it's not as big as people had anticipated," said C.J. Gavsie, managing director of corporate and institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at BMO Capital Markets in Toronto, commenting on the scope of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He estimated that the tally likely was in the tens of millions, not the hundreds of millions.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the joint G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday morning, markets were reacting to the United Nations resolution overnight to impose a no-fly zone over Libya and its authorization of military action to protect Libyan civilians.\par \par \uc2 Libya ordered an immediate cease-fire in the wake of the UN resolution, Libya's foreign minister said. Doubts surfaced over whether a truce was feasible with rebels calling it a "bluff."\par \par \uc2 The commodity-linked Canadian dollar was little moved on the developments, though the news did send crude prices into negative territory.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Yemen, more than 30 people were killed when armed men opened fire on the largest antigovernment protest held to date in the center of the capital San'a.\par \par \uc2 In domestic data Friday, Canadian consumer prices were lower than expected in February in spite of higher gasoline costs, with the key core rate posting the slowest annual gain in records dating to 1984, as hotel charges declined sharply from a year ago when prices soared because of the Winter Olympics in Vancouver.\par \par \uc2 On a monthly basis, the core consumer price index grew 0.2%, missing the 0.4% rise the market was expecting. Meanwhile the annual rate slowed to 0.9% from 1.4%, Statistics Canada said Friday. The market expected a 1.1% rise in the core rate, which excludes prices of eight volatile components, including energy and some food prices.\par \par \uc2 The mild inflation figures suggest there's no immediate pressure on the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to begin raising interest rates again.\par \par \uc2 These are the exchange rates at 3:30 p.m. EDT (1930 GMT), and 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     USD/CAD     0.9859     0.9810      0.9862 \par     EUR/CAD     1.3964     1.3868      1.3824 \par     CAD/JPY     82.01      83.17       80.00 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Karen Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2022; karen.johnson@dowjones.com [ 03-18-11 1547ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1158   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i000b7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
