\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Dollar Off Highs Against Yen, Stronger Against US Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren and Karen Johnson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 14:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar is slightly higher against the dollar but is well off its highs against the yen Friday morning after the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in more than 10 years as part of a joint international effort to quell strength in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar is at Y82.54 against the yen after hitting Y83.34 immediately after the bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to buy Canadian dollars against the yen. It gained sharply in overnight trading, rising from Y80.00 late Thursday after the G7 group of advanced economies released its initial statement committing to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Traders attributed the Canadian/yen pair's retreat from its immediate post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  highs to fact the size of the transaction fell short of market expectations and that market had effectively priced in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in advance.\par \par \uc2 "I'm guessing that it's not as big as people had anticipated," said C.J. Gavsie, managing director, corporate and institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales, at BMO Capital Markets in Toronto, noting in the moments after the reported \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the Canadian dollar was coming off its peak.\par \par \uc2 He estimates that the tally was in the tens of millions, not the hundreds of millions.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not disclose the size of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar is at C$0.9833 from C$0.9862 late Thursday as a broad investor appetite for risk supported the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the G7's concerted efforts on behalf of Japan.\par \par \uc2 "I think the principal way the overnight news has played out, taken together, is better risk appetite," said \uc2 Adam Cole\uc2 , chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 Strength in global equity markets in response to the G7's declaration has supported all risk-sensitive assets, including the Canadian dollar, he said.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the joint G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday morning, the United Nations voted late Thursday to impose a no-fly zone over Libya and authorized "all necessary measures" to protect Libyan civilians.\par \par \uc2 Libya has ordered an immediate ceasefire in the wake of the United Nations resolution, Libyan Foreign Minister Musa Kusa said Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar registered little response to the news, though crude futures moved into negative territory.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar has also been able to gain ground broadly despite news that domestic consumer price data for February fell short of analysts' expectations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday followed moves by the Bank of Japan, Europe Bank of Italy, Bundesbank, Bank of France and Bank of England.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined in a G-7 pledge Thursday to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in a decade as Japan dumped massive quantities of yen Friday, aiming to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record surge from further damaging an economy ravaged by an earthquake, a tsunami and a nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 It marks the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since a joint effort to stabilize the euro in the fall of 2000.\par \par \uc2 -Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020;\par \par \uc2 don.curren@dowjones.com [ 03-18-11 0918ET ]\par \par \uc2 1158   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0008r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
