\par\par \b \uc2 CANADA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  DEBT-C$ gains fade vs yen, little changed vs USD\b0\par\par\uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  says took part in yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * C$ jumps vs yen after BoC \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but then fades\par \par \uc2 * C$ rises to $1.0141\par \par \uc2 * Bonds flat, weigh Libya against rising stocks\par \par \uc2 * Canada annual inflation rate eases in February\par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 By Ka Yan Ng\par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar finished little changed against the greenback Friday, and the early gains it made against the yen on G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  faded a bit as the session wore on.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  took tame domestic inflation data in stride, and attracted only muted attention for most of the day. Traders were focused instead on the move by Group of Seven central banks, including the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 , to sell the yen, which had risen to record highs against some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to anxiety over the crisis in quake-ravaged Japan.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's participation will be reflected in the country's foreign reserves report in early April, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar had weakened against the yen, with one Canadian dollar buying as little as 78.03 yen on Thursday, a level not seen since April 2009.\par \par \uc2 It jumped as high as 83.34 yen immediately after the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but by session's end, had eased to around 81.85 yen.\par \par \uc2 "Canada has been lackluster today all day in a really tight range," said Firas Askari, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at BMO Capital Markets. "Canada/yen has come off just because dollar/yen has come off."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar made sharp gains after the G7 action but later fell back.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said it was not important how much the G7 central banks may have spent but the show of unity was the point. \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  noted that Canada's yen reserves are relatively small, $252 million as of March 3, and that any movement in the yen versus the Canadian unit is more likely to be dictated by U.S. Federal Reserve action than by that of the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The holdings of reserves that the (\uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 ) has of Japanese yen are fairly small so I think this is to provide moral support and to underpin the consensus across the G7 to support Japan," said David Tulk, chief Canada macro strategist at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a lackluster session against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , finishing at C$0.9861 to the U.S. dollar, or $1.0141, little changed from Thursday's North American session close at C$0.9863 to the U.S. dollar, or $1.0139.\par \par \uc2 It finished a volatile week -- during which it fell more than 2-1/2 cents to hit its weakest point since Feb. 11 -- down 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 TAME INFLATION, BOC SEEN IN NO HURRY TO UP RATES\par \par \uc2 Data Friday showed Canada's annual inflation rate in February cooled to 2.2 percent from 2.3 percent in January, just below the consensus forecast of 2.3 percent, and the core rate fell to its lowest level on record at 0.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 The docile price environment suggested the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  can hold off on raising interest rates, and a Reuters poll showed more primary dealers now say the first rate hike of 2011 will come in the second half of the year rather than the first.\par \par \uc2 Overnight index swaps, which trade based on expectations for the key \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, showed the market is more bearish on rate rises than the primary dealers, implying the year's first rate rise won't come until October.\par \par \uc2 Government bonds were flat to mildly higher, outperforming U.S. Treasuries, as a cease-fire declaration in Libya pared safety bids as did rises on global stock markets.\par \par \uc2 The two-year Canadian government bond was off 1 Canadian cent to yield 1.608 percent, while the 10-year bond gained 12 Canadian cents to yield 3.173 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Claire Sibonney; Editing by Peter Galloway)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CANADA/DOLLAR-BONDS (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110321e73i000r2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
