\par\par \b \uc2 CANADA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  DEBT-C$ rebounds to end higher, bonds fall\b0\par\par\uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 15:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * C$ finishes at C$0.9863 per US$, or $1.0139\par \par \uc2 * Bonds give ground on profit-taking, more stable stocks\par \par \uc2 * G7 ministers to meet, market on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  watch\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  wholesale trade up 1.5 pct, forecast was 0.6 pct\par \par \uc2 * Next up: Canada inflation seen staying tame in February\par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 By Ka Yan Ng\par \par \uc2 TORONTO, March 17 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar <CAD=D4> ended higher against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, ending a three-day slide, gaining support from higher oil prices and economic data that suggested Canada's economy got off to a strong start in 2011.\par \par \uc2 U.S. oil, a key driver for the commodity-linked Canadian dollar, rose by more than $3 to above $101 a barrel as unrest in Saudi Arabia, Bahrain and Libya heightened concerns about supply disruptions. [O/R]\par \par \uc2 "I would almost have expected somewhat better from the Canadian dollar, given all the factors that seem to be in its favor today," said David Watt, senior fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We are doing better on the day but we are still somewhat shy of most of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Japanese yen."\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar finished at C$0.9863 to the U.S. dollar, or $1.0139, up from C$0.9918 to the U.S. dollar, or $1.0083, at Wednesday's close.\par \par \uc2 Impeding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains was continuing anxiety about developments in Japan's nuclear crisis. Engineers worked through the night to restore a power cable to a crippled nuclear plant in the hope of restarting pumps desperately needed to pour cold water on overheating fuel rods and avert a catastrophe. [ID:nL3E7EH18S]\par \par \uc2 Market focus was squarely on the Japanese yen, which retreated from its record high against the U.S. dollar. Market watchers were also on guard for the outcome of a Group of Seven conference call, where finance ministers and central bankers will discuss possible steps to calm volatile financial markets roiled by fears about the crisis in Japan. [FRX/] [ID:nN17296393]\par \par \uc2 "I don't think we're going to see a clear statement that they're going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Watt. "I think we'll see a strong statement that central banks have a very strong vested interest in orderly markets, which we did not get late last night in dollar/yen or any of the other yen crosses."\par \par \uc2 Supportive factors for the Canadian dollar on Thursday included firmer stock markets -- Toronto and New York were both up more than 1 percent -- and positive Canadian economic data.\par \par \uc2 Canada's January wholesale trade grew at more than twice the pace expected, while foreign investors remained enthusiastic buyers of Canadian bonds in the first month of the year, with foreign net investment in Canadian securities the strongest since last September. [ID:nN17178665]\par \par \uc2 BONDS FALL\par \par \uc2 Despite the market's concerns over Japan, some analysts suggested the positive domestic data could nudge the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  closer to raising interest rates in the first half of this year from the current 1.0 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Both releases today are a gentle reminder that the macro conditions in Canada are in a healthy condition, said Mazen Issa macro strategist at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Our long-standing view has been an interest rate hike in July. Tomorrow's CPI report should help strengthen this case.\par \par \uc2 Unlike the situation in many other countries, inflation in Canada is expected to remain tame and of limited concern to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Economists see the overall inflation rate remaining above the bank's 2 percent target, but nearly a percentage point of that is due to higher sales taxes. The data is due at 7 a.m. on Friday. [ID:nN11170328]\par \par \uc2 Canadian short-dated bonds gave up some of this week's gains as investors booked profits and world stocks stabilized.\par \par \uc2 The two-year government bond <CA2YT=RR> fell 15 Canadian cents to yield 1.605 percent, while the 10-year bond <CA10YT=RR> shed 28 Canadian cents to yield 3.188 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ka Yan Ng; editing by Rob Wilson)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CANADA/DOLLAR-BONDS (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110320e73h000gm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
