\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Canada Dollar Strength Could Collide With BOC Rhetoric\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren   \par \uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2011\par 03:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN\par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar hasn't faced many serious hurdles as it has firmed to a two-and-a-half year high in recent trading. But continued strength could bring into play a formidable adversary: the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been subdued in recent statements about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citing it as a potential risk to the recovery in exports but not emphasizing the threat. But the bank has used rhetoric as a tool in combating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength in the past and some analysts think it will do so again in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar has rallied since the bank's last policy statement in December, pushing above the psychologically significant parity level with the U.S. dollar and staying there for its first extended stretch since May 2008.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was trading at C$0.9862 Wednesday afternoon, considerably below the C$1.0200 average the Bank had assumed for its two-year forecast period in its most-recent quarterly monetary policy report in October.\par \par \uc2 Supported by the rally in commodity prices, favorable short-term yield differentials against the U.S. and Canada's superior fiscal situation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks poised to continue its rally.\par \par \uc2 If the bank is worried that this situation will be a threat to Canada's export sector, it has a perfect opportunity to voice those fears in the coming days. The bank has its first policy announcement of the year scheduled for next Tuesday. The following day, the bank releases its quarterly monetary policy report and Governor Mark Carney is expected to hold a news conference.\par \par \uc2 "I suspect he'll say something about the Canadian dollar being too high and the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Aaron Fennell, senior market strategist at futures broker \uc2 Lind-Waldock\b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Toronto. But any reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will be "noncommittal"; a reminder of the bank's capacity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than a commitment to take action, he said.\par \par \uc2 That was the pattern the bank followed in the fall of 2009, when an appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted some effective sabre rattling from the bank, but no actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in a joint effort to bolster the euro in the fall of 2000. The last time it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own was in 1998, and that was to prop up the Canadian dollar, not bring it down.\par \par \uc2 Some other analysts believe the bank will refrain from escalating its rhetoric. They think it will instead take the view the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise is justified by the strength in prices for the commodities which make up a big portion of the country's exports, and the dampening effect on export volumes will be offset by the higher prices of the commodities exported.\par \par \uc2 "Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's a little bit stronger than it was the last time they spoke about it, commodity prices are also stronger, so on a net basis, it hasn't become a worse issue since the last time they spoke," said Avery Shenfeld, chief economist at CIBC World Markets.\par \par \uc2 Doug Porter, deputy chief economist at BMO Capital Markets, agrees the bank could take the view that the broadly based rise in commodity prices explains the increase in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far. But any further move higher could elicit concern from the bank, he said.\par \par \uc2 "I think the bank is probably somewhat concerned about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  taking another big step higher from current levels," Porter said.\par \par \uc2 -Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020; don.curren@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Don Curren, a reporter based in Toronto, has covered Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income markets for more than 10 years. He can be reached at 416-306-2020 or at don.curren@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110114e71d001s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw98\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1470\pichgoal600
