\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: BOC's Carney: Interest Rates In Canada "Abnormally, Exceptionally Low"\b0\par\par\uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2010\par 23:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to include additional comments from \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  Governor Mark Carney beginning in paragraph 10.)\par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Interest rates in Canada remain abnormally low even though they have been raised from the emergency levels reached after the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in the fall of 2008, \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  Governor Mark Carney said in an interview with the \uc2 Canadian Broadcasting Corporation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The interview is scheduled for broadcast on Sunday morning but was released in advance by the publicly owned national broadcaster Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Carney said Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised its key policy rate from the record low of 0.25% reached during the financial crisis "because the Canadian economy is back at the level we were before the crash."\par \par \uc2 "We've recovered all the jobs we'd lost during the crash. I mean, things have moved quite positively for Canada, but [interest rates] are still at exceptionally low levels," he said. There's a risk some Canadians will take on more debt on the assumption "that interest rates will always be this way," Carney said.\par \par \uc2 Asked about a recent media report that \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2  is on an international list of banks deemed "Too Big to Fail", Carney said the largest Canadian bank was "absolutely not" on the list.\par \par \uc2 "Let me first clarify that RBC is not on that list. There's a report that it might be, but that was an unsourced report," he said.\par \par \uc2 While governments and central banks had to stand up and provide capital and liquidity for banks during the global financial crisis, it's important that the concept of "too big to fail" does not become established in markets, Carney said.\par \par \uc2 "This isn't how a market economy should work," he said. "There needs to be discipline on the financial institutions and there's a variety of things that we're doing to end 'Too Big to Fail,' not to enshrine 'Too Big to Fail'," he said.\par \par \uc2 Asked if Canada could get "caught in the crossfire" as nations around the world engage in competitive devaluation, Carney said the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  has "a number of tools" available.\par \par \uc2 "We can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if extreme movements in our dollar seriously threaten economic outcomes in Canada," Carney said. "Those are decisions taken by the Minister of Finance and the Governor," he said. "They are extreme."\par \par \uc2 The bank has policy flexibility and would use it, if appropriate, to achieve its 2% inflation target, he said.\par \par \uc2 Carney said efforts to manipulate trade through fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies could be damaging to the global economy. "You can cause damage to the global system in the short-term, but it doesn't work in the long term because ultimately those adjustments to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will come through relative inflation differentials if nothing else," he said.\par \par \uc2 The global financial system came within about 36 hours of a "seize up" in October 2008, Carney said in the interview. "[The] Minister of Finance and I entered a G7 meeting - it was at the time on a Friday afternoon in Washington...at that point I think it was clear that we were going to have nothing less than a seizure of the global financial system if certain decisions were not taken," he said.\par \par \uc2 The international banking system is "without question" more secure now than it was two years ago, Carney said.\par \par \uc2 Globally, the situation has improved because there have been big adjustments to policy, including monetary policy, he said. Additionally, major banks around the world have built up substantial amounts of capital over the last two years, he added.\par \par \uc2 -Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020; don.curren@dowjones.com [ 11-19-10 1750ET ]\par \par \uc2 4346   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101119e6bj000t6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
