\par\par \b \uc2 4th UPDATE: Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2 's Carney:  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Would Be Joint Move\b0\par\par\uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2010\par 01:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with further comments from Carney on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Nirmala Menon \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA (Dow Jones)--\uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Mark Carney said Wednesday unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market "seldom works."\par \par \uc2 If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would do so in cooperation with other countries, Carney said in testimony to the Senate banking committee.\par \par \uc2 Senior Deputy Governor Tiff Macklem also spoke.\par \par \uc2 Carney said the Bank and the federal government would contemplate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two scenarios. One is if there was a "disruption" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, which he said was relatively rare.\par \par \uc2 The second situation, he said, is if "extreme movements" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market "seriously imperiled conditions that would support sustainable long-term growth."\par \par \uc2 "Unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without backing policy actions seldom works sustainably," Carney said. "If conditions warranted, we would look to act with our partners in advanced economies and emerging economies if necessary. And we would always work to ensure that underlying policy was consistent with the action."\par \par \uc2 Carney said the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's economic forecasts assume additional monetary stimulus by the U.S. Federal Reserve. He said it was "no secret" that the Fed is discussing additional quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 "The point is obviously to avoid further disinflation and deflation, and given the debt dynamics in the United States, not just at the government level but very importantly at the personal level, it would be unhelpful to move into a deflationary environment," he said. "That's a downside risk obviously for the Canadian economy and it's one we watch closely."\par \par \uc2 Carney said conditions in Canada are very different from the U.S. In the U.S., he said there is "considerable challenge" over access to credit. The U.S. job market is in "very difficult shape", and there has been limited progress in closing the output gap. He said U.S. conditions warrant additional monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Although the Bank doesn't have direct experience with quantitative and credit easing, its reading of such non-conventional policy measures is that "they're most effective when markets are not functioning well as was the case during the crisis," Carney said.\par \par \uc2 Macklem, meanwhile, said it was important for countries to have greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility. Carney said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflexibility has serious longer-term impact on the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "Ultimately, exchange rates should find their natural level, their appropriate level, that's consistent with fundamentals," he said, adding that Canada has benefited "tremendously" from having a flexible exchange rate which has acted as a "shock absorber" for the economy.\par \par \uc2 "It has made Canada a wealthier country as a whole when combined with a host of other factors," he said. "It is ultimately unsuccessful to resist fundamental adjustments to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Carney said the adjustment will be much more difficult if it comes through relative inflation rates rather than through the nominal exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "What we are seeing now is disinflationary pressures in the United States, and increasing inflationary pressures in China, which is one of the reasons why Chinese authorities raised interest rates last week for the first time since the crisis," he said. "This dynamic is much more difficult adjustment, it brings ultimate costs in growth."\par \par \uc2 He said there are mechanisms that countries can put in place to address the speed and sharpness of adjustment that are still consistent with market-based rates. He noted that the G-20 had committed over the weekend in Gyeongju, South Korea, to move towards market-determined exchange rates, and that he expected to see progress on the matter.\par \par \uc2 Carney also said that returning to a gold standard is "not viable at all."\par \par \uc2 "But certainly the discussion of adjustments to the international monetary system of a different form will be increasingly prevalent over the coming years, we believe," he said.\par \par \uc2 He also said there's a need to remain "vigilant" on household debt in Canada. He said credit for small businesses is improving "quite considerably."\par \par \uc2 -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 613-237-0668; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com [ 10-27-10 1939ET ]\par \par \uc2 4346   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101027e6ar0010g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
