\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Fin Min: Freely Traded  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Could Be Affected By  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2 , Inflexible  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 18:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates throughout with quotes, detail.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Nirmala Menon \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA (Dow Jones)--Canadian Finance Minister Jim Flaherty said Wednesday there is a danger that freely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be "disadvantaged" by \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflexibility.\par \par \uc2 Flaherty made the comments ahead of meetings in Washington of Commonwealth Finance Ministers and G-7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors that he will chair.\par \par \uc2 Several key nations this week took steps to arrest their soaring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Flaherty said there are concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflexibility.\par \par \uc2 "This is a topic that I'm sure we'll be discussing in the next several days," he told reporters after the weekly caucus meeting of the governing Conservative Party.\par \par \uc2 He said Canada has always made it clear that it believes in freely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There is a danger here that countries that do have \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that trade in a market, that trade freely, are disadvantaged by certain \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are made by other countries and by inflexibility by certain \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. "So we don't want these kinds of distortions in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values or distortions in trading relationships. It's a very important topic."\par \par \uc2 Flaherty said U.S. Treasury Secretary Tim Geithner "has a point" in proposing to link International Monetary Fund reform and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices.\par \par \uc2 "He and I have had discussions of late on that and other subjects," he said.\par \par \uc2 Flaherty said G-20 countries have to keep the Toronto G-20 Summit Framework "in the forefront." He said one of the purposes of the meetings this weekend is to make sure countries stay on track to fulfill the commitments made at the Toronto Summit.\par \par \uc2 Flaherty reiterated that economic growth is fragile. But he said the Canadian economy doesn't need further stimulus "at this point."\par \par \uc2 "We watch carefully of course and will continue to watch. The economic recovery is fragile so we have to be prepared to be nimble but there's no intention now to extend stimulus programs," he said.\par \par \uc2 Flaherty said the private sector has to pick up the slack as governments in Canada and other G-20 countries ease out of stimulus. Canada's two-year stimulus program is scheduled to end in March 2011. Flaherty said the IMF's latest figures on Canada's economic growth are "encouraging."\par \par \uc2 -By Nirmala Menon; Dow Jones Newswires; 613-761-2126;\par \par \uc2 nirmala.menon@dowjones.com [ 10-06-10 1244ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101006e6a6000di\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
