\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Focus: Aussie Should Outshine The Commodities\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Gold may be heading even higher but don't expect the precious metal to take all the commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with it.\par \par \uc2 As we have seen this week, both the Canadian and New Zealand dollar are being constrained by their own problems for now.\par \par \uc2 This leaves only the Australian dollar to follow gold higher.\par \par \uc2 As concern over the global recovery has risen, the price of gold has been pushed to new record highs over $1,250 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 With little sign of any turn in sentiment and with longer-term investment funds expected to join the fray, the price could well make it up as high as $1,500 or more by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 For commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this should be good news. More often than not a rally in the price of gold indicates a rise in global demand that should be reflected in commodity prices as a whole.\par \par \uc2 However, support for both the Canadian and New Zealand dollars should prove limited.\par \par \uc2 In Canada's case, there will certainly be resistance from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is becoming increasingly wary of the recent strength in the Canadian dollar.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  Governor Mark Carney issued a strong warning about the downside risks that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is posing for the economy. This was enough to even trigger talk of \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the Canadian dollar's rise, even though the chances of such a move are seen as pretty remote.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar may well have proved a better bet.\par \par \uc2 But the earthquake in Christchurch earlier this month appears to have put a stop to that.\par \par \uc2 By the Reserve Bank of New Zealand's own reckoning, the earthquake will wipe 0.3 basis points from GDP growth in the third quarter in an economy that was already flagging because of slower global growth. Th latest manufacturing purchasing managers index came in at 49.3 in August, under the key 50 level, suggesting that the economy is now contracting rather than expanding.\par \par \uc2 RBNZ Governor Alan Bollard warned Thursday that interest-rate increases will now be fewer and further apart and warned that the recent rise in the New Zealand dollar doesn't reflect fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 In other words, like Canada, New Zealand feels its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen as far as it should go.\par \par \uc2 For commodity bulls, this just leaves the Aussie.\par \par \uc2 The Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  already rallied to a two-year high earlier this week. But, with the Australian economy going from strength to strength, especially now that China looks set to avoid a hard landing, and with Australian interest rates likely to be raised again before the year is out, more gains can be expected.\par \par \uc2 If the price of gold keeps on climbing and with the prospects of the Chinese economy still improving, the Aussie could well find itself testing its mid-2008 high of $0.9847--a level that, prior to then, it hadn't hit in about 27 years.\par \par \uc2 See how the Aussie has been performing in the last few years:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4605"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4605}}}\par \par \uc2 Early Friday, commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were generally higher as risk sentiment improved ahead of the weekend. Apart from Spain's successful bond auction Thursday, the market's mood was being lifted by the lack of any disappointing economic data and a global rally in equity markets.\par \par \uc2 By 0645 GMT, the Aussie was up at $0.9446 from $0.9368 late on Thursday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The euro was up at $1.3145 from $1.3085 and at Y112.79 from Y112.29. The dollar was steady at Y85.80 as the market waited for any signs that the Bank of Japan will return to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Nick Hastings has covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and industry for over 20 years. Apart from his written commentary and analysis, he also appears on Fox Business News and CNBC television in Europe, Asia and the U.S. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 17-09-10 0715GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1066   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100917e69h000hf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
