\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Bonds Dip After Japan  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Johnson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 21:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Canadian bonds notched a slim decline across the curve on Wednesday, but on the long end declined less than their U.S. Treasury counterparts following Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two-year bond yield was at 1.480% late Wednesday, from 1.446% late Tuesday. The 10-year bond yielded 2.963%, from 2.931%. Bond yields and prices move in inverse directions.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. long end is down substantially this afternoon. We are following the pull there," said Sheldon Dong, vice president of fixed-income strategy at \uc2 TD Waterhouse \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , through which Japanese officials bought U.S. dollars and sold the yen, aimed to prevent a surging yen from threatening the economic recovery in the world's third-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year and 30-year U.S. maturities bore the brunt of the selling in a choppy trading session, as Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted investors to pivot into the shorter-dated notes that are believed to be the favored destination of Japan when parking its dollar proceeds.\par \par \uc2 The market also is positioning ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's meeting next week, Dong said.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. dealers are calling for a second round of quantitative easing by the Fed. So there is a bit of uncertainty," he said. "In Canada, we are just sort of muddling along."\par \par \uc2 Canada will keep watch on U.S. data releases, including the August consumer price index, due Friday. Canada's data calendar is empty for the remainder of the week.\par \par \uc2 "Canada is pretty much on hold. I think our market is watching the rest of the world," Dong said. "The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  has pretty much stated that whether they hike rates in October depends on factors outside of our borders."\par \par \uc2 In June, the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  became the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Group of Seven industrialized nations to raise interest rates since the global economic crisis began two years ago. It has made three consecutive rate increases, but now cautions that "any further reduction in monetary policy stimulus would need to be carefully considered in light of the unusual uncertainty surrounding the outlook."\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday in Newfoundland, BOC Deputy Governor Timothy Lane said the recovery in Canada is continuing but will likely be more modest than in previous cycles.\par \par \uc2 In new Canadian bond supply, the Canadian Finance Department reported an average 1.556% yield at an auction of C$3 billion of 1.50% non-callable government bonds due Dec. 1, 2012.\par \par \uc2 The high accepted yield was 1.559%; the low 1.554%. The price equivalent for the average yield was 99.879. The allotment ratio at the high accepted yield was 39.62727%.\par \par \uc2 The aggregate value of the bids received was about C$7.87 billion, including C$37.1 million of non-competitive tenders accepted at the average yield.\par \par \uc2 With the new bonds, the outstanding total of 1.50% bonds due Dec. 1, 2012, is C$6.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 Canada Housing Trust is looking to raise C$6.25 billion from an issue of five-year bonds. The issue has yet to be priced, but the spread guidance is around 24.5 basis points over the government of Canada 3% December 2015 bond benchmark.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Armtec Holdings\uc2  is looking to raise C$150 million from an issue of bonds that mature in September 2017. Pricing for the bonds, which are callable in 2013, is not yet determined. The coupon is expected to be around 8.875%.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Coupon   Matures    Price     Direction     Yield (Today vs Yesterday) \par    2.00s    2012        100.994  dn 0.081      1.480% vs 1.443% \par    3.00s    2015        103.707  dn 0.174      2.241% vs 2.206% \par    3.50s    2020        104.479  dn 0.291      2.963% vs 2.931% \par    5.00s    2037        125.154  dn 0.315      3.531% vs 3.522% \par            10-Yr Spread to U.S. +24 vs +26 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Karen Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2022; karen.johnson@dowjones.com [ 09-15-10 1541ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1182 4152   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000dm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
