\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Turkish Lira Weaker, Bonds Stronger\b0\par\par\uc2 1,212 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2010\par 17:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1501 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira is weaker as USD gains ground against EUR. USD/TRY trades at 1.5170 from 1.5040 Thursday. Meanwhile bonds are stronger after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  monetary policy committee left its key interest rates unchanged late Thursday, as expected. The benchmark April 25, 2012 bond closes at 8.06%, from 8.12% Thursday and it moves to 8.07% in Monday-value-dated transactions. Analysts forecast TRY to trade between 1.4950 and 1.5210 and the benchmark bond to trade between 8.00% and 8.15%, in the short term.(sezin.bala@iha.com.tr)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1459 GMT [Dow Jones] Dismal June wholesale trade data in Canada released Thu suggest renewed recession pressures stateside are finding their way north of the border at a time when the domestic housing market is clearly rolling over, says David Rosenberg, chief economist and strategist at Gluskin/Sheff in Toronto. Why \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  Governor Mark Carney would continue to raise rates in this environment is anyone's guess, but the consensus seems to think there's at least one more interest rate bullet in his chamber, even with today's inflation news that the year-over-year trend in core CPI dipped to 1.6% in July, Rosenberg says. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1458 GMT [Dow Jones] Cook County faces a $300m budget shortfall next year, which administration officials suggest could force across-the-board 10% cuts, the Chicago Tribune reported Fri. The paper said it was told by sources that county chief financial officer Jaye Williams delivered the news this week to other county financial officials. The administration attributes the shortfall to a half-point sales tax cut after earlier having had implemented a 1 percentage point increase, according to the Tribune. Lower federal funding also is expected.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stan.rosenberg@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1456 GMT [Dow Jones] Polish bond yields are significantly lower as the prospect of higher interest rate rises appears increasingly distant, a trader says. Sentiment for bonds is positive, with yields likely to continue falling next week amid strong demand from both domestic and foreign buyers who see Poland's fiscal situation as relatively good. Average yield of 2-year bonds is at 4.55% from 4.65% late Thursday, for 5-year they're at 5.02% from 5.14%, 10-year at 5.34% from 5.54%. EUR/PLN is at 3.9745 from 3.9649, USD/PLN at 3.1336 from 3.0961. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1433 GMT [Dow Jones] September gilts hit a new contract high amid concerns over slowing growth. While the data calendar was empty, core government bonds are boosted after ECB member Axel Weber said it makes sense to keep providing unlimited funds to banks until the end of the year. Whilst UK inflation is running high, data out this week show moderation in core inflation and Capital Economics says the bond market is right to be relatively sanguine over the outlook. "We see scope for 10-year gilt yields both to ease down further to around 2.75%." September gilts up 0.29 at 125.02, having hit a 125.23 contract high. 10-year gilt at 114.639 yielding 2.975%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1415 GMT [Dow Jones] USD broadly stronger, with ICE Dollar Index notching one-month high at 83.200 from 82.347 late Thu, according to EBS via CQG. EUR/USD recently hit intraday low at 1.2673 from 1.2820, as dovish ECB comments and downgrade of France's 2011 growth prospects combine with recent stream of weakening US data, leading investors to flee riskier assets. DJIA down nearly 1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (bradley.davis@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1404 GMT [Dow Jones] Week's deals have been generally well accepted in muni mkt, thanks to continued light supply and strong demand. "Munis have had a nice run, following the Treasury market," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eaton Vance\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Evan Rourke. Fri, munis holding firm, with prices little changed and low trading volume, he says. Looking ahead, it's another light issuance week, with $533M deal from South Calif Public Power one of largest offerings, according to Ipreo. Market participants expect supply to pick up sometime after Labor Day.(kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1402 GMT [Dow Jones] The index rose earlier to 83.214, which the current high for the uptrend from the Aug. 6 low of 80.085. See chart at\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4462"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4462}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 The index is closing in on target resistance at 83.385. But a move below 82.935 would instead point it down to 82.347.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (stephen.cox@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1353 GMT [Dow Jones] Russia is less likely to enact capital controls now, given the less stable external backdrop and negative net private capital flow, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says. Nevertheless, "continued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is costly, and reduced inflows would lessen that cost." Any capital controls would probably be targeted, rather than broad based, the bank says. These may include high tax penalties for withdrawing portfolio investments early, differentiated reserve requirements for banks, caps on foreign borrowing by state-owned companies, and reducing the tax-deductibility of interest payments on foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1348 GMT [Dow Jones] The major theme in recent weeks is global investors gravitating to emerging market local bonds, says RBS. Month-to-date, 2-year through 10-year yields have fallen between 5 bps and 50 bps across key liquid markets, such as Brazil, Mexico, Poland, South Korea and South Africa. Only Turkey is against trend. This comes as (1) investors flock from stocks on global growth fears;(2) EM local bonds have higher yields than most of the world; (3) many developed markets look to be on cusp of recession; and (4) there is some evidence China is buying to diversify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure, says RBS. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1329 GMT [Dow Jones] Emerging market bonds are weaker on the day, with broader market moves, as investors pare riskier positions. The risk premium on \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Emerging Markets Bond Index Global is 1 bp wider to 300 bps over Tsys. The index is down 0.10%. Brazil's benchmark bond, the Global 2040, is down 1/8 to 138 3/4 bid, according to Reuters. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1327 GMT [Dow Jones] The overnight indexed swaps market is pricing in virtually even odds of another 25-basis-point rate raise from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  at its next policy date on Sept 8 after soft Canadian CPI data for July. That's down from a more than 60% chance ahead of the data. One month of softer CPI data won't convince the bank that inflationary pressures are receding on a sustained basis, suggesting that 2Q GDP data to be released on Aug 31, the last important release before the decision date, will be a key input for the bank, one Toronto economist said. "It's really upped the ante on that figure," he said. 